window.FRAMEZ_INDEX=[{"t":"Getting Started","p":"Getting Started","u":"start.html","x":"This page opens Framez, shows what is on screen before an animation is open, and takes one animation from its first pose to the asset id your game plays."},{"t":"What Framez Is","p":"Getting Started","u":"start.html#make","x":"Framez is a keyframe animation editor that runs inside Roblox Studio as a plugin. You select a rig in the viewport, pose its limbs, set keys on a timeline, shape the motion between the keys, and save the result as an animation that your game plays. It works on"},{"t":"Open Framez","p":"Getting Started","u":"start.html#open","x":"Install Framez from the Creator Store. It then shows on Studio's Plugins tab. Click the Framez button. The Framez window opens docked at the bottom of Studio, and the first time it opens the Inspector docks beside it on the right. Plugins ‣ Framez The parts of"},{"t":"The Start Screen","p":"Getting Started","u":"start.html#start-screen","x":"Before an animation is open, Framez asks which rig to work on. The list In this place shows every rig it finds, with its rig type and number of joints, and Recent shows the animations you opened last and the rig each one belongs to. Click a rig in the list, or"},{"t":"Your First Animation","p":"Getting Started","u":"start.html#first","x":"These steps make a short animation on any rig. Skip a step the animation does not need. Select the rig by clicking it in the viewport or in the start screen. A rig made with Studio's Avatar tab is joined with AnimationConstraints instead of Motor6Ds, so Framez"},{"t":"Keeping Your Work Safe","p":"Getting Started","u":"start.html#start-safe","x":"Every save keeps an earlier version you can go back to, described in Version History . Framez also keeps a recovery copy of work that was not saved, described in Unsaved Work and Recovery , and while the Framez Helper runs it writes a copy of every save to dis"},{"t":"Where to Go Next","p":"Getting Started","u":"start.html#start-next","x":"The Framez Window names every part of the screen, and Timeline and Toolbar explains the buttons, the track list and the rows of the timeline. Workspaces describes the three jobs the window switches between, and Working with Tools explains what every tool has i"},{"t":"Installing the Framez Helper","p":"Getting Started","u":"setup.html","x":"Framez works without anything else installed. The Framez Helper is a free Windows program that adds what a Studio plugin cannot do on its own, such as playing video, linking to Blender and reading a webcam. It is needed "},{"t":"What the Helper Is For","p":"Installing the Framez Helper","u":"setup.html#setup-what","x":"Studio plugins cannot read video files, run other programs or talk to Blender. The Framez Helper does that work on your PC and answers only programs on the same computer, at the address 127.0.0.1 on port 34880. It has no window. It waits in the background and "},{"t":"Install the Helper","p":"Installing the Framez Helper","u":"setup.html#setup-install","x":"The helper installs once per PC, for the current Windows user only. It needs Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit, and does not ask for administrator rights. Open the helper's download page. Framez cannot open a web browser, so it shows the link in a box. Click the link, "},{"t":"Check That the Helper Works","p":"Installing the Framez Helper","u":"setup.html#setup-check","x":"Ask Framez whether the helper answers. The status line at the bottom of the window then names the helper's version and the folders its clips come from, or says what to do. Help ‣ Helper status If the status line says to start the Framez Helper, open Framez Hel"},{"t":"Install the Blender Add-On","p":"Installing the Framez Helper","u":"setup.html#setup-blender","x":"The add-on puts a Framez tab in Blender's sidebar so that an animation can be sent from Framez to Blender, edited there and sent back. It needs Blender 4.2 or newer, and the helper must be running, because the two programs never talk to each other directly. Ru"},{"t":"Remove the Helper","p":"Installing the Framez Helper","u":"setup.html#setup-remove","x":"Open the Windows list of installed apps and remove Framez Helper. This stops the helper, deletes its files, its Start menu and startup entries and its settings, and takes the Framez tab out of Blender. Windows Settings ‣ Apps ‣ Installed apps ‣ Framez Helper ‣"},{"t":"The Framez Window","p":"Interface","u":"window.html","x":"The Framez window holds the menus, the toolbar, the track list and the timeline. The tools that need more room open as separate windows beside it."},{"t":"Parts of the Window","p":"The Framez Window","u":"window.html#window-parts","x":"The Framez window docks at the bottom of Studio. It has the same seven parts in every workspace, although the menus, toolbar buttons and timeline rows change with the workspace. The Framez window with an animation open, with its seven parts numbered to match t"},{"t":"Top Bar","p":"The Framez Window","u":"window.html#f-topbar","x":"Workspace All The top bar runs along the upper edge of the window. From the left it holds the Framez mark, the three workspace buttons, the menus, then the rig and animation name, the undo and redo buttons and the Save button. The rig and animation name are wr"},{"t":"Status Line","p":"The Framez Window","u":"window.html#f-statusline","x":"Workspace All The status line is the strip along the bottom edge of the window. Its left side says what Framez last did, such as \"Keyed all 15 limbs at frame 12\", and shows a problem in red. While the pointer rests on a button or a row, the line shows that con"},{"t":"Narrow Windows","p":"The Framez Window","u":"window.html#f-narrow","x":"Workspace All The Framez window can be docked at any width, so the top bar and the toolbar fold in steps instead of being cut off. Below about 1150 pixels the zoom slider goes, leaving the zoom buttons. Below about 960 pixels the view switches in the toolbar f"},{"t":"Side Windows","p":"The Framez Window","u":"window.html#windows","x":"Windows other than the Framez window stay out of the way until you open them. Each one has its own page. Inspector shows the numbers of whatever is selected, such as a limb's rotation and position, the animation's length, priority and asset id, and the posing "},{"t":"Opening and Arranging Windows","p":"The Framez Window","u":"window.html#f-openwin","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Inspector, Reference video, Poses, Curve editor, Physics, Modifiers, Check animation View ‣ Windows ‣ Camera preview, Subtitle lines Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... and Keyboard shortcuts... Game ‣ Test bench... Help ‣ Scripting... Works"},{"t":"Workspaces","p":"Interface","u":"workspaces.html","x":"A workspace chooses which menus, toolbar buttons, timeline rows and windows the Framez window shows for one kind of job. Changing workspace never changes the animation."},{"t":"Switching Workspaces","p":"Workspaces","u":"workspaces.html#f-workspace","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Animate, Scene, Game data Shortcut Ctrl + Page Up and Ctrl + Page Down Workspace All Framez has three workspaces, Animate, Scene and Game data, and the three buttons at the left of the top bar switch between them. The Page Up and Page Dow"},{"t":"Animate Workspace","p":"Workspaces","u":"workspaces.html#f-ws-animate","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Animate The Animate workspace is for posing a rig and timing its keys. It shows the Key and Pose menus and every toolbar button, and the timeline lists the posed rig's limbs with the rows that belong to them, such as the face, parent, lay"},{"t":"Scene Workspace","p":"Workspaces","u":"workspaces.html#f-ws-scene","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Scene The Scene workspace is for building a shot from several parts, such as other rigs, props, cameras, lights, sound, subtitles and screen effects. It shows the Scene menu in place of Key and Pose, and its toolbar adds a button that loo"},{"t":"Game Data Workspace","p":"Workspaces","u":"workspaces.html#f-ws-game","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Game data The Game data workspace is for what your game reads from an animation, such as events, hit windows, foot contacts, body masks, the way Roblox plays the clip and the scripts the game needs. It shows the Game menu in place of Key "},{"t":"What Each Workspace Remembers","p":"Workspaces","u":"workspaces.html#f-ws-memory","x":"Workspace All Each workspace keeps its own arrangement. It remembers which of the Inspector, Reference video, Poses, Curve editor, Check animation and Modifiers windows are open, which Inspector page is showing, whether the rig's tracks are folded, and whether"},{"t":"Reset This Workspace","p":"Workspaces","u":"workspaces.html#f-ws-reset","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Reset this workspace Workspace All Reset this workspace puts the current workspace back to how it looked the first time you used it, including its windows, its Inspector page, the folding of the rig's tracks and its overlays. It does not "},{"t":"Menus and Tools in Every Workspace","p":"Workspaces","u":"workspaces.html#f-ws-all","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Workspaces The setting Menus and tools chooses how much each workspace shows. Its own, the default, shows what each workspace's job needs. All shows every menu, toolbar button, timeline row and Inspector section in every"},{"t":"Timeline and Toolbar","p":"Interface","u":"timeline.html","x":"The toolbar runs along the top of the Framez window. Under it, the track list and the timeline have a row for every limb, and extra rows for events, layers, faces and the other things an animation carries."},{"t":"Toolbar","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#timeline-toolbar","x":"The toolbar sits under the menu bar and is faded until a rig is selected. A button that belongs to one workspace shows only in that workspace, so the Scene workspace has fewer posing buttons and a button for looking through a camera. In a narrow window the vie"},{"t":"Go to Frame 0 and Go to Last Key","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-ends","x":"Shortcut Home for frame 0, End for the last key Workspace All The first transport button moves the playhead to frame 0, and the last one moves it to the last key of the animation. The last key is the latest key on any limb. In a scene it also counts the keys o"},{"t":"Previous Key and Next Key","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-hop","x":"Shortcut ← and → Alt + ← and Alt + → step by frames Shift + ← and Shift + → move the selected limb's keys Workspace All These two buttons move the playhead to the nearest key before or after it, on any limb, so you can move through an animation pose by pose wi"},{"t":"Play and Stop","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-play","x":"Shortcut Space Workspace All The play button plays the animation from the playhead at the animation's frame rate, and turns into a stop button while it plays. Playback follows real time, and when the screen refreshes faster than the animation's frame rate the "},{"t":"Loop","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-loop","x":"Menu Key ‣ Loop ‣ Looping Shortcut Tab Workspace Animate The loop button switches Looping on or off for the animation. The setting is saved with the animation and is what the game uses, so a cycle that plays round in Studio also plays round in the game. The bu"},{"t":"Key","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-key","x":"Menu Key ‣ Insert key Shortcut K Workspace Animate, Scene The key button keys the selected limb at the playhead, exactly as it stands, which is the same as Insert Key . If no limb is selected the status line asks you to click one. In a scene with a camera, pro"},{"t":"Rotate and Move","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-mode","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Rotate Pose ‣ Move Shortcut R switches between them Workspace Animate These two buttons choose what the handles on the selected limb do. In Rotate mode the limb is surrounded by rings, and dragging a ring turns the limb about that axis. In Move mod"},{"t":"World Axes","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-world","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Posing ‣ World space Shortcut Ctrl + L Workspace Animate The world axes button makes the rings and arrows act along the world's axes instead of the selected limb's own axes. It is lit while World space is on. The status line says World space or Loc"},{"t":"IK Legs","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-ik","x":"Workspace Animate The IK legs button makes a dragged foot bend the knee, so you pose a leg by placing the foot. Turning it on also keeps feet planted while you pose. The button is lit while it is on, and Framez keeps the choice with your other posing options. "},{"t":"Onion Skin","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-onion","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Onion skin ‣ Show onion skin Right-click The button Workspace Animate The onion skin button shows the rig at the keys on either side of the current frame as ghosts, so you can judge how a pose sits between its neighbours. Only the parts "},{"t":"Motion Paths","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-paths","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Motion paths ‣ Show motion paths Right-click The button Workspace Animate The motion paths button draws the path that the hands, feet, head, weapon tip and torso travel along over the animation. Right-clicking it opens the choices for wh"},{"t":"Reference Video","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-ref","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Reference video Workspace Animate Needs The Framez Helper, for video files The reference video button opens and closes the Reference video window, which plays a recording or a set of numbered image frames beside the timeline. See Also Ref"},{"t":"Curve Editor","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-curves","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Curve editor Workspace Animate The Curve editor button opens and closes the Curve editor window, which draws each channel of the selected limb as a curve and shows how fast the limb moves. See Also Curve Editor ."},{"t":"Show or Hide Handles","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-handles","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Handles Right-click The button Shortcut P Workspace All The eye button hides the rings, arrows and limb boxes so that you can watch the animation as it will look, and shows them again. Hiding the handles also turns off onion skin and bri"},{"t":"Look Through Camera","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-look","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Look through camera Shortcut C Workspace Scene The camera button shows the viewport through a camera of the scene, so you see the shot as the game will. It is dimmed until the scene has a camera, and lit while you are looking through one. See Also"},{"t":"More","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-more","x":"Workspace All When the Framez window is narrower than about 960 pixels, the view switches of the toolbar fold into one More button. Its list shows the same switches with a tick when they are on. They are IK legs, Onion skin, Preview, which hides the handles, M"},{"t":"Frame Counter and Zoom","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#timeline-counter","x":""},{"t":"Frame Counter","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-now","x":"Workspace All The frame counter in the middle of the toolbar shows the playhead's frame, the animation's length in frames and the playhead's time in seconds, for example 14 / 60 and 0.47 s. Click it to open a small box where you can type a frame to go to, chan"},{"t":"Zoom Out, Zoom Slider and Zoom In","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-zoom","x":"Menu View ‣ Timeline zoom ‣ Zoom in, Zoom out Workspace All The minus and plus buttons at the right end of the toolbar change how many frames fit across the timeline. Each click zooms about the middle of what is on screen, by a factor of 1.4. The slider betwee"},{"t":"Fit","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tb-fit","x":"Menu View ‣ Timeline zoom ‣ Frame all Workspace All The fit button at the far right of the toolbar zooms the timeline to show the whole animation, from frame 0 to its last frame."},{"t":"Track List, Ruler and Keys","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#timeline-tracks","x":""},{"t":"Track List","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-tracks","x":"Right-click A limb's name Workspace All The track list on the left of the timeline has a row for each joint of the rig, nested the way the rig is built, so that every limb sits under the limb it hangs from. The rig's own row stays pinned at the top. A small ar"},{"t":"Ruler and Playhead","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-ruler","x":"Workspace All The ruler runs along the top of the timeline. Its numbers are frames, spaced a round number apart that suits how many frames are on screen, with a dot for each frame between them. The band behind the numbers is dark across the animation's length "},{"t":"Keys","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-keys","x":"Right-click A key on the timeline Empty space in the rows Workspace All Each key is a diamond on its limb's row, at its frame. A white diamond is a key, a blue one is a breakdown, which is a pose between two keys that shapes the move, and a small grey one is a"},{"t":"Locking a Limb","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-lock","x":"Menu Key ‣ Track ‣ Lock track Right-click A limb ‣ Track ‣ Lock track Workspace Animate A locked limb is protected from your edits. You cannot select the limb or its keys, and moving, scaling, easing and deleting all leave its keys alone, while the limb keeps "},{"t":"Zooming and Panning the Timeline","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-pan","x":"Workspace All Scrolling the mouse wheel over the timeline zooms in or out about the frame under the pointer, so the frame you are looking at stays where it is. Once the timeline shows only part of the animation, a pan bar appears under the rows. Its grip is th"},{"t":"Rows of the Timeline","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#timeline-rows","x":"Besides the limbs, the timeline can show other rows between and under the rig. A row appears when the animation has something to put in it, and some rows belong to one workspace."},{"t":"Events Lane","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-events","x":"Menu Game ‣ Add event here... Right-click An event pin Workspace All The events lane runs under the ruler numbers, beside the Events row of the track list. Each event is a pin with its name written next to it, and events on one frame share a pin. Drag a pin to"},{"t":"Bookmarks Lane","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-bookmarks","x":"Menu View ‣ Bookmarks ‣ Add bookmark here... Right-click A bookmark Workspace Animate Bookmarks are named, coloured frames or ranges that help you find your way round a long animation. They sit in a lane under the events lane, which appears once the animation "},{"t":"Face Rows","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-facerow","x":"Right-click A face key A head whose face is a Decal gets a face row directly under the Head row. Each face key shows the face itself, its name and how many frames it holds, with a grey bar to the next key, or a thin line when it returns to the rig's own face. "},{"t":"Layer Weight Row","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-weightrow","x":"Right-click A weight key, or the row Workspace Animate An animation layer's weight says how strongly the layer applies, from 0 to 100 percent. The layer weight row, under the rig, draws that weight over time for the layer you are keying and for every other lay"},{"t":"Modifiers Row","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-modrow","x":"Right-click A bar Workspace Animate Once a tool such as a cycle or a clean-up filter has been run, the animation gets a Modifiers row under the rig. Its name shows how many results the animation holds, and each result is a bar spanning the frames it works on. "},{"t":"Hit Windows and Foot Contacts Rows","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-hits","x":"Right-click A bar, or the row Workspace Game data The Hit windows row shows each hit window as a bar, coloured by its kind, with overlapping windows stacked. The Foot contacts row shows each foot contact as a strip for each foot, L and R, with a bar while that"},{"t":"Parent Row","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-parent","x":"Right-click A block A limb, a whole rig, a held object, a prop or a camera that has parent switches gets a row named after it with the word parent, such as RightHand · parent. The row shows which part the item follows, and when that changes. Each switch is a b"},{"t":"Control Rows","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-controls","x":"Right-click A key on a control's row Workspace Animate A limb that a control drives has a row of the control's own in the track list. It is named after the control, such as Left foot, and has a small square in the control's colour and an arrow. The row stands "},{"t":"Track Groups","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-groups","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Track groups Right-click A group's row A limb ‣ Track ‣ Group Workspace All A rig with many joints lists its tracks in groups, one for each branch of the rig, such as Body, Tail or Thigh.L, and any group you make yourself. Groups turn on "},{"t":"Subtitles Row","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-subs","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Subtitles Right-click A subtitle block Workspace Scene The Subtitles row sits after the scene's items and holds one block for each subtitle line, coloured by who says it. Drag a block to move the line and drag its end to trim it. Double-clic"},{"t":"Clips Rows","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-clips","x":"Right-click A clip block, a Clips row, or empty space in a Clips row Workspace Scene Once an animation has clip blocks, Clips rows appear under the posed rig, above its body parts. The rows are named Clips 1, Clips 2 and so on, with Clips 1 at the bottom and t"},{"t":"Squash and Stretch Row","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-squash","x":"Right-click A squash key A part that has squash and stretch keys gets a Squash and stretch row under it, and the whole body's size keys have a row of their own, called Whole body squash, under the first body row. Drag a key on the row to move it in time, or ri"},{"t":"Scene Rows","p":"Timeline and Toolbar","u":"timeline.html#f-tl-scene","x":"Workspace Scene In the Scene workspace every item of the scene is a row at the top of the track list, drawn with a figure for a rig, a box for a prop, and an icon each for a camera, a light, a sound, a screen effect, a scene look, an effect and a path. The rig"},{"t":"Working with Tools","p":"Interface","u":"anytool.html","x":"Every tool that makes or changes keys works the same way. You choose it, it runs at once, and you adjust its values afterwards. Most of them also keep their result in the Modifiers window, so you can change it again late"},{"t":"Finding a Tool","p":"Working with Tools","u":"anytool.html#any-find","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Menu search... Right-click A limb, a key, a row of the timeline or a result in the Modifiers window Shortcut Ctrl + K Each tool has one name, and the name is the same everywhere it appears. A tool is in the menu for its job, in the right-click menu"},{"t":"Running a Tool","p":"Working with Tools","u":"anytool.html#r-tool","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Generate Pose ‣ Physics Key ‣ Clean up Shortcut F9 opens the values of the last tool again Workspace Animate Choosing a tool from a menu runs it at once. It uses the values it had the last time you ran it, or its default values the first time, so y"},{"t":"Adjust","p":"Working with Tools","u":"anytool.html#f-adjust","x":"Shortcut F9 opens it again, Esc closes it Workspace All Adjust is a small panel at the foot of the Framez window, titled with the name of the tool. It opens each time a tool runs from a menu and holds the values of that run. Every change redoes the run with th"},{"t":"Presets","p":"Working with Tools","u":"anytool.html#f-presets","x":"Workspace Animate The Presets row at the top of a result's values, in Adjust and in the Modifiers window, keeps sets of values you want to use again. Choosing a preset puts its values into the tool and runs it again. A preset holds the look of a tool and not t"},{"t":"Mix","p":"Working with Tools","u":"anytool.html#any-mix","x":"Workspace Animate Mix is the last section of a result's values, in the Modifiers window and in Adjust. It is folded until you open it, and it opens by itself when a result already uses it. Mix works the same way for every tool, because it controls how the tool"},{"t":"Undo","p":"Working with Tools","u":"anytool.html#any-undo","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Undo Edit ‣ Redo Shortcut Ctrl + Z and Ctrl + Y A tool and everything you change about its result are single undo steps. Ctrl + Z takes back one run of a tool, or one whole drag of a value in Adjust, however many keys it changed, and Ctrl + Y puts "},{"t":"Dialogs","p":"Working with Tools","u":"anytool.html#any-dialogs","x":"Shortcut Enter presses the main button, Esc closes the dialog A tool whose name ends in three dots opens a dialog. The dialog's main button is named after what it does, such as Apply, Record, Repeat or Bake, and it is the red one. The dialog opens with its fir"},{"t":"Number Boxes","p":"Working with Tools","u":"anytool.html#any-numbers","x":"Workspace All A number box in a tool's dialog or in Preferences accepts a sum as well as a number. It understands the operators + , - , * , / , % and ^ , brackets, the constants pi , e and tau , and the functions sin , cos , tan , asin , acos , atan , sqrt , a"},{"t":"Tools and Layers","p":"Working with Tools","u":"anytool.html#any-layers","x":"Menu Key ‣ Layers Workspace Animate A tool's result stays on the animation layer it was made on, whichever layer you key into later. When you change the result in Modifiers, Framez switches to that layer to run the tool again and switches back afterwards. Resu"},{"t":"File Menu","p":"Menus","u":"file.html","x":"The File menu starts, opens, saves and publishes animations, brings animations in from Roblox, Moon Animator 2, Blender and the game, sends them out again, and sets up the rigs you animate. Its rows are the same in every"},{"t":"New Animation","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-new","x":"Menu File ‣ New animation New animation starts an empty animation on a rig. When no animation is open it uses the rig you clicked in the viewport or selected on the start screen. When an animation is open it clears that animation and starts again on the same r"},{"t":"Open","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-open","x":"Menu File ‣ Open... Open lists the saved animations of the rig so you can carry on with one. It asks about unsaved changes first, as New animation does, then lets go of the open animation and shows the rig's animation browser in place of the timeline. A search"},{"t":"Save","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-save","x":"Menu File ‣ Save Shortcut Ctrl + S Save stores the animation in the place, under the rig's name in the RBX_ANIMSAVES folder of ServerStorage, using the name shown in the header. An animation that is still called Untitled opens Save as first. In a scene it save"},{"t":"Save As","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-saveas","x":"Menu File ‣ Save as... Save as saves the animation under a name you type, next to the rig's other saves. The animation then carries the new name. A small box asks for the Name , and Enter confirms it. If a save with that name already exists, Framez asks \"NAME "},{"t":"Publish to Roblox","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-publish","x":"Menu File ‣ Publish to Roblox... Publish to Roblox saves the animation, selects the saved copy and opens Roblox's own upload window for it. A small box beside that window lists the two steps. In the upload window you set Creator to whoever owns the game and su"},{"t":"Version History","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-history","x":"Menu File ‣ Version history... Right-click A save in the Open browser, then Version history... Every Save keeps a copy of the animation as a version. Version history lists the versions of the open animation, newest first. Each row shows the version number (v1,"},{"t":"Import","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#file-import","x":"The Import rows bring motion in from Roblox, from Moon Animator 2 saves, from Blender and from scenes that were put in the game. They all work on the open animation, so most are greyed until one is open. The Import submenu."},{"t":"Roblox Animation","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-importroblox","x":"Menu File ‣ Import ‣ Roblox animation... Roblox animation puts an existing Roblox animation on the open rig as keys. The animation can be a published one, given by its asset id or link, or an Animation, KeyframeSequence or CurveAnimation that already exists in"},{"t":"Moon Animator 2 File","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-moon2","x":"Menu File ‣ Import ‣ Moon Animator 2 file... Moon Animator 2 file opens a Moon Animator 2 save that exists in the place. The rig's keys come in with their easing styles, and the other rigs, cameras and parts in the save join the scene with their keys. A list s"},{"t":"Eclipse Save","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-eclipse","x":"Menu File ‣ Import ‣ Eclipse save... Eclipse save opens an Eclipse Animator animation or project that exists in the place. Eclipse keeps its saves inside the place: an animation under its rig's AnimSaves, a project in ServerStorage, in Eclipse Animator Saves. "},{"t":"FBX Animation","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-importfbx","x":"Menu File ‣ Import ‣ FBX animation... Needs The Framez Helper, and Blender with the Framez tab FBX animation brings a clip from another 3D program or a motion capture file onto the open rig through Blender. Framez sends the rig to Blender. In the Framez tab of"},{"t":"Get from Blender","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-getblender","x":"Menu File ‣ Import ‣ Get from Blender Needs The Framez Helper, and Blender with the Framez tab Get from Blender brings back what the Framez tab in Blender sent with Send to Framez and writes it into the open animation as keys. Where Blender marks which frames "},{"t":"Rig Setups from Blender","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-blendersetup","x":"Menu File ‣ Import ‣ Get from Blender Needs The Framez Helper, and Blender with the Framez tab A Blender armature can carry constraints, limits and drivers. Select the armature in Blender, open the Framez tab in the 3D viewport's side panel ( N ), and click Se"},{"t":"Scene from the Game","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-scenefromgame","x":"Menu File ‣ Import ‣ Scene from the game... Scene from the game puts a scene that was exported to the game back into this place's saves. The scene's record returns, together with the animation of every rig that this place no longer has. The row opens a list of"},{"t":"Unsaved Work and Recovery","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-recover","x":"Menu File ‣ Import ‣ Recover unsaved work... While an animation has unsaved changes, Framez keeps a recovery copy a few seconds after the last edit. It is a copy of the animation exactly as Save would write it, including keys, curves, events, layers and pins, "},{"t":"Export","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#file-export","x":"The Export rows turn the open animation into something else, such as an emote for the Marketplace, a set of movement animations, an additive layer, a Blender file, a set of aim poses or a video. The Export submenu in the Animate workspace."},{"t":"Emote","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-emote","x":"Menu File ‣ Export ‣ Emote... Emote checks the open animation against the rules Roblox applies to UGC emotes and saves it as an emote. The window lists one row for each rule. A red row is a rule that makes Roblox refuse the upload, a grey row is something Save"},{"t":"Animation Pack","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-pack","x":"Menu File ‣ Export ‣ Animation pack... Animation pack builds a set of movement animations for the Marketplace or for your own game. The window has a slot for each animation of a pack. The slots are Idle, Idle 2, Walk, Run, Jump, Fall, Climb, Swim and Swim idle"},{"t":"Additive Animation","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-additive","x":"Menu File ‣ Export ‣ Additive animation... Additive animation saves the open animation as an additive layer, named after the animation with _ADD added. Every pose is stored as the difference from a reference pose. In a Roblox animation graph, an Add node then "},{"t":"Send to Blender","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-sendblender","x":"Menu File ‣ Export ‣ Send to Blender Needs The Framez Helper, and Blender with the Framez tab Send to Blender sends the rig and the open animation to Blender. In the Framez tab of Blender's side panel, Get from Framez builds an armature with a bone for each jo"},{"t":"Aim Offset","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-aimoffset","x":"Menu File ‣ Export ‣ Aim offset... Workspace Animate Aim offset saves nine aiming poses made from the pose at the playhead, for a character whose upper body follows the camera or a target while the legs play their own animation. The poses look left, right, up,"},{"t":"Live Link with Blender","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-live-blender","x":"Menu File ‣ Export ‣ Live link with Blender Needs The Framez Helper, and Blender with the Framez tab Live link with Blender keeps the open animation and Blender in step while you work. An edit in Framez shows in Blender, and an edit in Blender shows in Framez."},{"t":"Playblast","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-playblast","x":"Menu File ‣ Export ‣ Playblast... Workspace Animate and Scene Needs The Framez Helper Playblast records the animation from Studio's viewport to a video. The dialog opens with the settings you used last. Record starts the recording, which steps through the anim"},{"t":"Rig","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#file-rig","x":"The Rig rows put rigs into the place, turn ordinary models into rigs, add or remove joints, tell Framez which part is which on a custom character, and build the controls you pose a rig with. The Rig submenu."},{"t":"New Rig and My Avatar","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-insertrig","x":"Menu File ‣ Rig ‣ New R6 rig File ‣ Rig ‣ New R15 rig File ‣ Rig ‣ My avatar as R6 File ‣ Rig ‣ My avatar as R15 These four rows put a rig into the place and select it. New R6 rig and New R15 rig add Roblox's own default character, in the classic colours: a ye"},{"t":"Avatar or Outfit","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-avatarlook","x":"Menu File ‣ Rig ‣ Avatar or outfit... Avatar or outfit... brings in any character. Type a username or a user id, or paste a profile link, a catalog bundle or outfit link, or a link to one catalog item such as a shirt, hair or a hat. Words that are none of thes"},{"t":"Rig Selected Model","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-rigmodel","x":"Menu File ‣ Rig ‣ Rig selected model Rig selected model gives the model selected in the Explorer, such as a door, a chest or a creature, a joint on every piece so that it can be animated. The row is greyed until a model is selected. The main part is the model'"},{"t":"Create Joint","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-joinparts","x":"Menu File ‣ Rig ‣ Create joint... Create joint adds a Motor6D between two selected parts, so that one of them can be animated. Select two parts in the Explorer, the part that holds first and the part that moves second, by holding Ctrl while clicking. A part ca"},{"t":"Unjoin Selected Joint","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-unjoin","x":"Menu File ‣ Rig ‣ Unjoin selected joint Unjoin selected joint turns the Motor6D selected in the Explorer into a weld. The part stays where it is and is no longer animated. The status line says which part stopped animating. As with Create joint, the joint must "},{"t":"Rig Mapping","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-rigmap","x":"Menu File ‣ Rig ‣ Rig mapping... Rig mapping tells Framez which part of a custom rig is which part of the body, so that IK, mirroring, generators, full-body IK and Import work on creatures, mechs and characters whose parts are not named as Roblox names them. R"},{"t":"Control Rig","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-controlrig","x":"Menu File ‣ Rig ‣ Control rig ‣ Build control rig File ‣ Rig ‣ Control rig ‣ Control rig... File ‣ Rig ‣ Control rig ‣ Remove control rig Needs An animation open on a rig A control rig puts a few shapes in the view that you use instead of the joints. A foot co"},{"t":"Controls in the View","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-controlshapes","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Controls Needs A control rig Workspace Animate The controls of a rig are drawn over it as outlines. Controls on the left of the body are blue, controls on the right are red, and controls on the middle of the body are yellow. Framez draws"},{"t":"Control Rig Window","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-controlwin","x":"Menu File ‣ Rig ‣ Control rig ‣ Control rig... Needs An animation open on a rig The Control rig window shows the controls of the open rig on a faint body. Each control is an icon: a foot for a leg, a hand for an arm, a torso for the hips and the chest, a head,"},{"t":"Restore Deleted Rig","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-putbackrig","x":"Menu File ‣ Rig ‣ Restore deleted rig Restore deleted rig puts back a rig that was deleted while its animation was open, where it stood, and opens it again. Studio's own undo cannot bring the rig back while an animation is open, because Framez switches it off "},{"t":"Mesh Bones","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-meshbones","x":"Needs A rig with bones Creatures, custom avatars, capes, tails, hair and wings that are made as one skinned mesh are animated through their bones. There is no menu row for it. Click the model and open an animation, and its bones show through the body. Every to"},{"t":"Close","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#file-close","x":""},{"t":"Close Animation","p":"File Menu","u":"file.html#f-close","x":"Menu File ‣ Close animation Close animation lets go of the rig and returns to the start screen. With unsaved work it asks \"Save changes to NAME first?\" with Save, Do not save and Cancel. In a scene it asks before closing the scene. Once the animation is closed"},{"t":"Edit Menu","p":"Menus","u":"edit.html","x":"The Edit menu searches every command, undoes and redoes changes, copies and pastes poses, selects and deletes keys, and opens the settings and keyboard shortcuts. The undo, select and settings rows are the same in every "},{"t":"Menu Search","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-find","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Menu search... Shortcut Ctrl + K Menu search finds a command without you needing to know which menu it is in. A search box opens over the Framez window. Type a few words of what you want to do, and every command whose name, menu or description cont"},{"t":"Undo","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-undo","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Undo Shortcut Ctrl + Z Undo takes back the last change. One run of a tool, or one whole drag of a value, is a single step, however many keys it changed. Framez keeps the last 300 steps, fewer on a very large animation, and reports in the status lin"},{"t":"Redo","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-redo","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Redo Shortcut Ctrl + Y Redo puts back what Undo took back. Making a new change after an undo discards the steps that could have been redone. See Also Undo describes what counts as one step."},{"t":"Copy Pose","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-copy","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Copy pose Workspace Animate Copy pose copies what is selected. With keys selected on the timeline it copies those keys, each with its easing and with its distance in time from the first one. With no keys selected it copies the whole pose at the pla"},{"t":"Paste Pose","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-pastepose","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Paste pose Right-click A limb, then Paste onto this limb Workspace Animate Paste pose puts what was copied at the playhead. Copied keys land from the playhead onwards, each as far after it as it was after the first copied key, and each keeps its ea"},{"t":"Paste Pose Flipped","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-pasteflipped","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Paste pose flipped Workspace Animate Paste pose flipped pastes the copied keys or pose onto the opposite side of the body, mirrored. A left arm pose becomes a right arm pose, and the keys land at the playhead as Paste pose would place them. The row"},{"t":"World Transform","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#edit-world","x":"A normal paste is relative to the parent, so a pasted pose moves with the torso. The World transform rows copy where a limb is in the world instead, so it can be put back at exactly that place however the rest of the body has moved. They work on the selected l"},{"t":"Copy World Transform","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-world","x":"Menu Edit ‣ World transform ‣ Copy world transform Workspace Animate Copy world transform remembers where the selected limb is in the world, both its position and its turn. Every other selected limb is remembered as well. The row is greyed until a limb is sele"},{"t":"Paste World Transform","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-pasteworld","x":"Menu Edit ‣ World transform ‣ Paste world transform Workspace Animate Paste world transform puts each copied limb back at its copied place in the world at the playhead, keyed, whatever the torso is doing at that frame. The limbs are found by name, so the paste"},{"t":"Paste to Selected Keys","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-pasteworldkeys","x":"Menu Edit ‣ World transform ‣ Paste to selected keys Workspace Animate Paste to selected keys places the copied limb at its copied world position on every selected key of that limb, instead of adding a key at the playhead. Each key is changed so that the limb "},{"t":"Select","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#edit-select","x":"The Select rows choose keys by a rule instead of one at a time. Each row replaces the current key selection. The same rows are in the timeline's right-click menu, where the current frame means the frame you clicked. Keys on locked tracks are never selected."},{"t":"Select All","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-select","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Select ‣ Select all Right-click The timeline Shortcut A Select all selects every key on every unlocked track. The status line reports how many keys are selected. Example For example, select all keys and drag the selection box's edge to stretch the "},{"t":"Select None","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-selectnone","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Select ‣ Select none Right-click The timeline Shortcut Esc Select none lets go of every selected key and of the selected limb, so that nothing is selected."},{"t":"On Current Frame","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-selectframe","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Select ‣ On current frame Right-click The timeline On current frame selects every limb's key on the playhead's frame. From the timeline's right-click menu it selects the keys on the frame you clicked. Example For example, select the column of keys "},{"t":"Before Current Frame","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-selectbefore","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Select ‣ Before current frame Right-click The timeline Before current frame selects every key that comes before the playhead's frame, on every unlocked track."},{"t":"After Current Frame","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-selectafter","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Select ‣ After current frame Right-click The timeline After current frame selects every key that comes after the playhead's frame. Example For example, with the playhead on the frame where a hit lands, select everything after it and move it two fra"},{"t":"Between Bookmarks","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-selectbetween","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Select ‣ Between bookmarks Right-click The timeline Between bookmarks selects the keys between the bookmark before the playhead and the bookmark after it. When the playhead is inside a range bookmark, it selects the keys of that range. With no book"},{"t":"Select More","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-selectmore","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Select ‣ Select more Right-click The timeline Select more grows the selection by one key at each end. For every run of selected keys on a limb, it adds the key before the run and the key after it."},{"t":"Select Less","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-selectless","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Select ‣ Select less Right-click The timeline Select less shrinks the selection by one key at each end. For every run of selected keys on a limb, it takes off the run's first and last key. Example For example, select all the keys between two bookma"},{"t":"Delete","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#edit-delete","x":""},{"t":"Delete Keys","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-delkeys","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Delete keys Shortcut Del or Backspace Delete keys deletes the selected keys in one undo step. The row is greyed, with the message \"Select keys first\", while no keys are selected. A locked track keeps its keys, and the status line says how many were"},{"t":"Settings","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#edit-settings","x":"The Settings submenu holds the options of the open animation and the two windows for the settings that are kept between sessions."},{"t":"Animation Settings","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-clipprops","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Animation settings Animation settings opens the Clip page of the Inspector , which holds the settings of the open animation. They are the animation's length, its frame rate, the priority Roblox plays it at, whether it loops, the weapon, "},{"t":"Preferences","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-openprefs","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... Preferences opens the Framez Preferences window, which holds every setting that you set once and keep, such as handles, timeline behaviour, new animation defaults, navigation, overlays, saving and workspaces. Changes apply"},{"t":"Keyboard Shortcuts","p":"Edit Menu","u":"edit.html#f-openkeys","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Keyboard shortcuts... Keyboard shortcuts opens the window that lists every command with its key and lets you give any command a key of your own. The window has its own page, Keyboard Shortcuts . The default keys are listed on the Shortcu"},{"t":"Key Menu","p":"Menus","u":"key.html","x":"The Key menu holds everything that works on keys: setting them, changing how the motion travels between them, moving them in time, and cleaning up a finished clip. It shows in the Animate workspace."},{"t":"Insert Key","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-key","x":"Menu Key ‣ Insert key Key ‣ Insert key on all limbs Right-click A limb in the viewport or the track list Shortcut K A key stores the pose of a limb at one frame. Insert key keys the selected limb at the playhead exactly as it stands, and Insert key on all limb"},{"t":"Easing","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-easing","x":"Menu Key ‣ Easing Right-click A key on the timeline Shortcut 7 Easing sets how a limb speeds up and slows down on its way from one key to the next. It belongs to the key that starts the move, so to change how a swing lands you select the key where the swing be"},{"t":"Timing","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#key-timing","x":"The Timing tools move keys earlier or later and change how long a stretch of the animation lasts."},{"t":"Scale Keys","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-stretch","x":"Menu Key ‣ Timing ‣ Scale keys... Right-click A key on the timeline Scale keys makes the selected keys faster or slower by spreading them apart or pulling them together. With fewer than two keys selected it scales the whole animation, and then events, weapon s"},{"t":"Move Keys","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-movekeys","x":"Menu Key ‣ Timing ‣ Move keys... Shortcut Shift + Left and Shift + Right move the selected keys by one frame Move keys shifts the selected keys earlier or later by a number of frames that you type. Every selected key moves by the same amount, so the spacing be"},{"t":"Ripple Edits","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-ripple","x":"Menu Key ‣ Timing ‣ Ripple edits Shortcut Hold Shift while dragging to switch it for one drag When Ripple edits is on, changing the timing of some keys also moves everything after them. Dragging keys later, or stretching the selection box's right edge, pushes "},{"t":"Retime Tool","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-retime","x":"Menu Key ‣ Timing ‣ Retime tool Right-click A stretch on the retime strip, for Retime easing The Retime tool speeds up or slows down stretches of an animation by dragging markers, so you do not have to type a factor. Choosing it switches the tool on and shows "},{"t":"Retime Easing","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-retimeease","x":"Menu Key ‣ Timing ‣ Retime easing ‣ Ease in Key ‣ Timing ‣ Retime easing ‣ Ease out Key ‣ Timing ‣ Retime easing ‣ Ease in and out Right-click A stretch on the retime strip Retime easing changes how the keys inside one retimed stretch are spaced, so the stretc"},{"t":"Insert Frame","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-insertframe","x":"Menu Key ‣ Timing ‣ Insert frame Insert frame adds one frame at the playhead. Every key after the playhead moves one frame later and the animation becomes one frame longer. Events, weapon switches, face keys and show or hide switches after the playhead move wi"},{"t":"Remove Frame","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-removeframe","x":"Menu Key ‣ Timing ‣ Remove frame Remove frame takes one frame out after the playhead. Every key after the playhead moves one frame earlier and the animation becomes one frame shorter. Events, weapon switches, face keys and show or hide switches move with the k"},{"t":"Offset Limbs","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-offset","x":"Menu Key ‣ Timing ‣ Offset limbs Offset limbs staggers the selected keys of several limbs so that each limb moves a few frames after the one before it. Parts that follow what carries them, such as an arm trailing the torso, look natural when they overlap like "},{"t":"Snap Keys to Video Hits","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-snap","x":"Menu Key ‣ Timing ‣ Snap keys to video hits Needs The Framez Helper, and a reference video linked to the timeline Snap keys to video hits moves each selected key onto the nearest beat of the linked reference video. A beat is a moment where the picture stands s"},{"t":"Stretch by Dragging","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-stretchbox","x":"Shortcut Hold Alt while dragging to stretch around the middle of the selection When the selected keys cover two frames or more, a box appears around them on the timeline with a handle at each end, and a label over the box shows its length in frames. Drag the r"},{"t":"Range","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#key-range","x":"The Range tools work on a stretch of the animation. The stretch is the frames covered by the selected keys, or the whole animation when no keys are selected. Repeat, Delete range, Crop to range and Bake keys run as soon as you choose them, using the values fro"},{"t":"Reverse","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-reverse","x":"Menu Key ‣ Range ‣ Reverse Right-click A key on the timeline ‣ Range ‣ Reverse Reverse plays the selected keys backwards inside their own stretch. The stretch keeps its first and last frame, and every pose lands on the mirrored frame, so the motion now runs fr"},{"t":"Repeat","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-repeat","x":"Menu Key ‣ Range ‣ Repeat Right-click A key on the timeline ‣ Range ‣ Repeat Repeat adds copies of the selected keys after them. Each copy starts on the last frame of the one before it, so a stretch that was built to loop repeats without a seam. Events and wea"},{"t":"Fill to Playhead","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-fill","x":"Menu Key ‣ Range ‣ Fill to playhead Right-click A key on the timeline ‣ Range ‣ Fill to playhead Fill to playhead repeats the selected keys again and again after themselves until it reaches the playhead. The last copy is cut at the playhead with a key that hol"},{"t":"Ping-Pong","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-pingpong","x":"Menu Key ‣ Range ‣ Ping-pong Right-click A key on the timeline ‣ Range ‣ Ping-pong Ping-pong adds a copy of the selected keys played backwards right after them. The motion goes through the stretch and then comes back to where it began. Keys after the stretch m"},{"t":"Delete Range","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-croprange","x":"Menu Key ‣ Range ‣ Delete range Right-click A key on the timeline ‣ Range ‣ Delete range Delete range removes every key between two frames and keeps the motion on either side. A key is added on each edge of the range, holding the pose the animation had there, "},{"t":"Crop to Range","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-cropto","x":"Menu Key ‣ Range ‣ Crop to range Right-click A key on the timeline ‣ Range ‣ Crop to range Crop to range keeps only the frames between two frames and cuts the rest of the animation away. A key is added on each edge, so the first and last pose of the kept part "},{"t":"Bake Keys","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-bake2","x":"Menu Key ‣ Range ‣ Bake keys Right-click A key on the timeline ‣ Range ‣ Bake keys Bake keys replaces the keys in a stretch with a new key every few frames, read from the motion as it plays. It works on the stretch and the limbs of the selected keys, or on eve"},{"t":"Reshape Keys","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-reshape","x":"Menu Key ‣ Range ‣ Reshape keys Reshape keys exaggerates, calms, shifts or retimes the motion of many keys at once, using curves that change over the frames. Framez reads the motion over the frames you choose, applies the curves, writes a key on every frame an"},{"t":"Track","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#key-track","x":"The Track tools work on one limb's track, the row that holds all of its keys. They need a limb to be selected."},{"t":"Select Track","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-track","x":"Menu Key ‣ Track ‣ Select track Right-click A limb in the viewport or the track list ‣ Track ‣ Select track A key on the timeline ‣ Select track Select track selects every key of the selected limb. Tools that work on selected keys, such as Scale keys, Offset l"},{"t":"Move 1 Frame Earlier","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-trackearlier","x":"Menu Key ‣ Track ‣ Move 1 frame earlier Right-click A limb in the viewport or the track list ‣ Track ‣ Move 1 frame earlier Shortcut Shift + Left Move 1 frame earlier moves the selected keys one frame earlier. With no keys selected it moves the whole track of "},{"t":"Move 1 Frame Later","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-tracklater","x":"Menu Key ‣ Track ‣ Move 1 frame later Right-click A limb in the viewport or the track list ‣ Track ‣ Move 1 frame later Shortcut Shift + Right Move 1 frame later is the same move in the other direction. It moves the selected keys one frame later, or the whole "},{"t":"Lock Track","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-tracklock","x":"Menu Key ‣ Track ‣ Lock track Right-click A limb in the viewport or the track list ‣ Track ‣ Lock track Lock track protects a limb's animation while you work on other limbs. A locked limb cannot be selected or changed. Deleting, moving, stretching, easing and "},{"t":"Key Times and Types","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#key-times","x":"These tools copy the frames that keys sit on from one limb to another, and set what kind each key is."},{"t":"Share Key Times","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-sharetimes","x":"Menu Key ‣ Key times and types ‣ Share key times Share key times makes several limbs key on the same frames without changing any pose. Every limb with selected keys gets a key on every frame where any of them has a key, inside the stretch of the selected keys."},{"t":"Copy Key Times","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-copytimes","x":"Menu Key ‣ Key times and types ‣ Copy key times Copy key times remembers the frames that keys sit on, without their poses. It copies the frames of the selected keys, or every frame where the selected limb has a key when no keys are selected. The copy stays unt"},{"t":"Paste Key Times","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-pastetimes","x":"Menu Key ‣ Key times and types ‣ Paste key times Paste key times keys the selected limb on the frames that Copy key times remembered. A frame where the limb already has a key is left as it is. Each new key holds the pose the limb already has there, so nothing "},{"t":"Paste Key Times Only","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-pastetimesonly","x":"Menu Key ‣ Key times and types ‣ Paste key times only Paste key times only does what Paste key times does and also removes the limb's other keys inside the copied stretch, from the first copied frame to the last. Afterwards the limb is keyed on exactly the cop"},{"t":"Key Types","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-keytypes","x":"Menu Key ‣ Key times and types ‣ Key Key ‣ Key times and types ‣ Breakdown Key ‣ Key times and types ‣ Generated Right-click A key on the timeline ‣ Key type Every key has a type, shown by how it looks on the timeline. A Key , drawn as a white diamond, is a po"},{"t":"Select by Key Type","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-selectbytype","x":"Menu Key ‣ Key times and types ‣ Select every key pose Key ‣ Key times and types ‣ Select every breakdown Key ‣ Key times and types ‣ Select every generated key These rows select every key of one type. They work on the selected limb, or on every limb when none"},{"t":"Loop","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#key-loop","x":"The Loop tools make an animation repeat, and make its end meet its start."},{"t":"Looping","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-loops","x":"Menu Key ‣ Loop ‣ Looping Shortcut Tab Looping switches repeat on or off for the animation. A looping animation plays round and round, in Framez and in your game. The row is ticked while looping is on. The setting belongs to the animation, so save it to keep i"},{"t":"Make Cyclic","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-makeloop","x":"Menu Key ‣ Loop ‣ Make cyclic Make cyclic changes the animation so that its end meets its start, and turns Looping on. It can copy the first pose onto the last frame, or build the second half of the animation from the first half with left and right swapped. Al"},{"t":"Layers","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#key-layers","x":"A layer holds keys of its own on top of the animation and is saved with it. A change made on a layer can be compared, muted and removed without touching the animation's own keys, which are called the base. The Layers rows here are the same layers that the Insp"},{"t":"Add Layer","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-layersmenu","x":"Menu Key ‣ Layers ‣ Add layer Add layer creates an additive layer. Its poses are added on top of everything under it and follow it wherever it goes, which makes it the right kind of layer for a lean, a breath or a correction over an animation that may still ch"},{"t":"Add Override Layer","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-addoverride","x":"Menu Key ‣ Layers ‣ Add override layer Add override layer creates a layer that replaces what is under it on the limbs it keys. Limbs it does not key play as they did before. Like any layer, it becomes the active layer when it is created, and its weight decides"},{"t":"Active Layer","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-activelayer","x":"Menu Key ‣ Layers ‣ Active layer Active layer chooses which set of keys you are editing. The list starts with Base, the animation's own keys, followed by every layer you added. The keys you set, and every tool that works on keys, then work on the active layer,"},{"t":"Selected Layer","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-selectedlayer","x":"Menu Key ‣ Layers ‣ Selected layer Selected layer holds the settings of the active layer. The row shows the layer's name, and it is greyed until a layer is the active layer. Its submenu changes how the layer plays and lets you rename or delete it. Options Addi"},{"t":"Preview","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-watchonly-menu","x":"Menu Key ‣ Layers ‣ Preview Preview changes what you see in Framez while you work, without changing any key or anything that is saved. It can mute or solo parts of the rig, or play another saved animation underneath this one, so you can judge a skill's arms ov"},{"t":"Clip Block","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-clipblockrow","x":"Menu Key ‣ Layers ‣ Clip block for (the rig's name) A clip block plays a saved animation as a block over your keys, starting on the playhead frame. It lets you put a finished animation, such as a run or a draw, under or around the animation you are making with"},{"t":"Merge into Base","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-mergebase","x":"Menu Key ‣ Layers ‣ Merge into base Merge into base bakes every layer into the animation's own keys and removes the layers. What plays stays exactly as it was, because the layered result becomes the base. The row is greyed until a layer exists, and merging is "},{"t":"Clean Up","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#key-cleanup","x":"The Clean up tools tidy an animation that came from a video, motion capture or an import, and polish one that you finished by hand. Apart from Check animation, they run as soon as you choose them, using the values from their last run, and their values open in "},{"t":"Check Animation","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-check-menu","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Check animation View ‣ Windows ‣ Check animation Check animation looks through the whole animation for what will look wrong in your game, such as sliding feet, a limb passing through the torso, a pop in speed or a loop that does not close"},{"t":"Butterworth Smooth","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-smooth","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Butterworth smooth The Curve editor's Clean up button Butterworth smooth takes jitter out of the keys that are already there. It adds no keys and removes none. Each key's pose is smoothed using the keys around it, and the Butterworth filt"},{"t":"Gaussian Smooth","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-gaussian","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Gaussian smooth The Curve editor's Clean up button Gaussian smooth softens everything evenly. Every key is blended with the keys around it, so small and large moves are rounded alike, and the result is a gentle, uniform softening. It runs"},{"t":"Peak Removal","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-peaks","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Peak removal A peak is a pose that jumps far away from the line between its neighbours for a frame or two and then comes back. Peak removal puts each peak back on the line between the frames either side of it. It works on the selected key"},{"t":"Euler Filter","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-euler","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Euler filter Euler filter turns back a limb that flips half a turn in one frame. Imported and captured motion can contain a frame where a forearm or a hand spins round and then returns. The filter finds the flipped frames, turns their key"},{"t":"Unbake at Contacts","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-unbake","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Unbake at contacts Unbake at contacts turns an animation that has a key on every frame into a few key poses that you can edit. It keeps the frames where the feet land and lift, the frames where a hand, foot, the head or the weapon tip slo"},{"t":"Mocap Cleanup","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-capture-cleanup","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Mocap cleanup Needs A rig with legs Mocap cleanup prepares a captured animation, from a video or a motion capture file, in one step. It smooths the motion, puts the body at the right height so that planted feet stand on the floor, plants "},{"t":"Cleanup Stack","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-cleanchain","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Cleanup stack Cleanup stack runs several clean-up tools one after another in one step, each with its own values. It saves you from running the same sequence by hand on every captured animation. The tool runs as soon as you choose it, and "},{"t":"Reduce Keys","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-reduce","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Reduce keys Reduce keys removes the keys an animation does not need. A key goes when the keys on either side of it play within the error you allow, so the animation looks the same with far fewer keys. It is meant for an animation that has"},{"t":"Curve Modifiers","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#key-modifiers","x":"Curve modifiers add motion to a limb without adding or changing any key. They sit inside Clean up, and the Curve editor has the same rows. Every modifier works on the selected limb, so the Curve modifiers row is greyed until a limb is selected."},{"t":"Curve Modifiers","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-mods","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers The Curve editor's Modifiers button A curve modifier changes how a limb's track plays while its keys stay exactly as they are. Noise, a wiggle, a repeat of the keyed stretch and more play on top of the keys, so you can add"},{"t":"Copy Modifiers","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modcopy","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Copy and paste ‣ Copy modifiers Copy modifiers copies every modifier of the selected limb, with their values, for the rest of the Studio session. The row is greyed, and says so, when the limb has no modifiers. Use Paste "},{"t":"Paste Modifiers","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modpaste","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Copy and paste ‣ Paste modifiers Paste modifiers adds the copied modifiers to the selected limb and to every other limb that has selected keys, after the modifiers those limbs already have. Locked limbs are skipped. It i"},{"t":"Paste and Replace","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modpasterep","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Copy and paste ‣ Paste and replace Paste and replace puts the copied modifiers on the selected limb and on every other limb that has selected keys in place of the modifiers those limbs have now. Locked limbs are skipped."},{"t":"Noise","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modnoise","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Add a modifier ‣ Noise... Noise adds smooth random motion to the selected channels. The motion drifts up and down by a random amount that changes gradually, never in sudden jumps. Options The common values described unde"},{"t":"Wiggle","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modwiggle","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Add a modifier ‣ Wiggle... Wiggle adds a wave to the selected channels that can die out over time. It begins at the frame you choose and swings back and forth at a steady rate, fading according to its Decay. It is what a"},{"t":"Function","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modfunction","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Add a modifier ‣ Function... Function adds the value of a mathematical function of the frame number to the selected channels. The value is Amplitude times the function of the Phase multiplier times the frame plus the Pha"},{"t":"Generator","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modgenerator","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Add a modifier ‣ Generator... Generator adds the value of a polynomial of the frame number to the selected channels, where the frame number is the variable. Higher orders give curves that bend more. A new Generator is a "},{"t":"Envelope","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modenvelope","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Add a modifier ‣ Envelope... Envelope reshapes the range of a limb's motion over time. The part of the motion between Reference plus Min and Reference plus Max is stretched to the Min and Max set at each point, and the v"},{"t":"Limits","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modlimits","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Add a modifier ‣ Limits... Limits keeps a limb's value between a lowest and a highest value, and can hold the time before or after a frame. A value past a limit stays at the limit. A time limit shows the pose of the limi"},{"t":"Cycles","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modcycles","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Add a modifier ‣ Cycles... Cycles plays the keyed stretch of a limb again before its first key and after its last key. The keyed stretch is the part from the limb's first key to its last. You can key one cycle of a walk "},{"t":"Loop","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modloop","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Add a modifier ‣ Loop... Loop is the Cycles modifier set to repeat in both directions and to ease the end of each repeat into the start over 6 frames. It opens the same dialog as Cycles , with the same values, and a limb"},{"t":"Stepped","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modstepped","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Add a modifier ‣ Stepped... Stepped holds a limb's pose for a number of frames at a time, so the motion plays in steps instead of continuously. Stepped acts on all channels and has no channel boxes. Options The common va"},{"t":"Bake to Keys","p":"Key Menu","u":"key.html#f-modbake","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Bake to keys Bake to keys turns the selected limb's modifiers into keys. It reads the limb as it plays with its modifiers on, writes a key on every frame of the animation, thins the result to the keys the motion needs, a"},{"t":"Pose Menu","p":"Menus","u":"pose.html","x":"The Pose menu holds the tools for posing a rig. They cover how the handles behave, the commands for the selected limb, the slider, mirroring, constraints, physics and the generators. It shows in the Animate workspace. Wh"},{"t":"Rotate and Move","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#pose-handles","x":"These two rows decide what the handles on a limb do, and the second article describes how to select more than one limb so a single drag poses them all."},{"t":"Rotate and Move","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-rotatemove","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Rotate Pose ‣ Move Shortcut R switches between the two Rotate and Move choose what the handles on the selected limb do. In Rotate the limb shows rings that turn it about its joint, and in Move it shows arrows that slide it along an axis. Only one o"},{"t":"Select Several Limbs","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-multipick","x":"Shortcut Shift +click a limb in the viewport Selecting more than one limb lets one drag pose all of them. Hold Shift and click a limb to add it to the selection, or click a selected limb again to take it off. The limbs in the selection have an orange outline. "},{"t":"Posing","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#pose-posing","x":"The Posing submenu holds the switches that change how the handles pose the rig, and the tools that act on the whole rig rather than on one limb."},{"t":"Full-Body IK","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-fullbody","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Posing ‣ Full-body IK With Full-body IK on, a hand or foot that you drag beyond the reach of its own limb pulls the body along. The torso leans, turns and shifts, the hips follow, and the hand or foot reaches from where its shoulder or hip has move"},{"t":"World Space","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-worldaxes","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Posing ‣ World space Shortcut Ctrl + L , or the globe button on the toolbar With World space on, the rings and arrows line up with the world instead of with the limb. A hand can then be moved straight up or along the ground whatever its tilt. While"},{"t":"Snap Rig to Floor","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-snapfloor","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Posing ‣ Snap rig to floor Snap rig to floor moves the whole rig straight down or up until its lowest point rests on the surface under it. If nothing is underneath, the rig is put on the ground plane at height 0. The message that follows names the "},{"t":"Place Rig","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-placerig","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Posing ‣ Place rig Place rig moves or turns the whole rig in the world with Studio's own Move and Rotate tools while the animation is open. It selects the rig's root part and switches to the Move tool; every limb follows the root as it is posed. Ch"},{"t":"Joint Limits","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-limits","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Posing ‣ Joint limits With Joint limits on, each joint stops at its range while you pose, with the rings, the arrows or IK. A drag that goes past the range stops at the edge. Joint limits are on by default. Hold Alt while dragging to pass the limit"},{"t":"Body Collision","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-bodycollision","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Posing ‣ Body collision With Body collision on, a limb stops where it meets the torso or another limb, so a hand cannot be dragged through the chest. Limbs that already touch when the drag starts are left alone. Hold Alt while dragging to pass thro"},{"t":"Proportional Editing","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-spread","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Posing ‣ Proportional editing Shortcut O With Proportional editing on, a pose change made with the handles also goes into the keys around the playhead. The change applies in full at the playhead and fades to nothing at the edges of a band of frames"},{"t":"Proportional Editing Settings","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-spreadsettings","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Posing ‣ Proportional editing settings... Proportional editing settings... opens the values that shape the band of frames Proportional editing reaches. The dialog's main button reads Turn on while the tool is off and Apply while it is on. Cancel pu"},{"t":"Squash and Stretch","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-squash","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Posing ‣ Squash and stretch ‣ Key here Pose ‣ Posing ‣ Squash and stretch ‣ Delete key here Pose ‣ Posing ‣ Squash and stretch ‣ Open the settings Pose ‣ Posing ‣ Squash and stretch ‣ Squash handles Squash and stretch changes the size of a part, or"},{"t":"Selected Limb","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#pose-limb","x":"The submenu that carries the name of the selected limb holds every command for that limb. It reads This limb and is greyed out until a limb is selected. The same rows open when you right-click a limb in the viewport, or its name in the track list, and the righ"},{"t":"Reset Limb","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-resetlimb","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Reset limb ‣ All Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Reset limb ‣ Rotation only Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Reset limb ‣ Position only Right-click A limb in the viewport or the track list Reset limb puts the selected limb back at its rest pose and keys it at the"},{"t":"IK / FK","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-ikfk","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ IK / FK ‣ IK from here Pose ‣ This limb ‣ IK / FK ‣ FK from here Pose ‣ This limb ‣ IK / FK ‣ Match IK to FK Pose ‣ This limb ‣ IK / FK ‣ Match FK to IK Right-click A limb that has a control ‣ IK / FK Needs A control rig The IK / FK sub"},{"t":"Parent To","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-scenefollow","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Parent to... Right-click A limb, a held object, or the row of a prop or camera Parent to... makes something ride another part from the playhead on. The child can be a limb, the whole rig (select its root), a held object, a prop or a cam"},{"t":"Mirror Onto","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-mirrorlimb","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Mirror onto twin Right-click An arm or leg Mirror onto puts the selected limb's pose, mirrored, onto its twin on the other side of the body and keys it at the playhead. The row names the twin, for example Mirror onto LeftUpperArm. Only "},{"t":"Hide","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-limbhide","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Hide ‣ Hide Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Hide ‣ Isolate Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Hide ‣ Show all Right-click A limb ‣ Hide Shortcut H Hide, Ctrl + H Isolate, Alt + H Show all Hide hides the selected limb in the viewport, together with anything it carri"},{"t":"Paste onto This Limb","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-pastelimb","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Paste onto this limb Right-click A limb in the viewport or the track list Paste onto this limb puts keys or a pose that you copied onto the selected limb. Copied keys land from the playhead on, each as far after it as it was after the f"},{"t":"Aim (Selected Limb)","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-look","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Aim Right-click A limb in the viewport or the track list Aim turns the selected limb to face a target. A head turns so that its front faces the target, and any other limb points along its length at it. The target is the part or model se"},{"t":"Propagate Pose","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-carry","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Propagate pose Right-click A limb in the viewport or the track list Propagate pose copies the pose of the selected limb's key at the playhead onto the keys that follow it. By default it takes the run of keys after the playhead that hold"},{"t":"Pivot","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-turnabout","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Pivot Right-click A limb or held object ‣ Pivot Shortcut Alt +click a spot on the body in the viewport Pivot chooses the point that the selected limb, object or whole body (the torso) turns about, instead of its joint. The rings move to"},{"t":"Edit Pivot","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-pivot-edit","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Pivot ‣ Edit pivot... Right-click A limb or held object ‣ Pivot ‣ Edit pivot... Edit pivot... moves the point a part turns about, such as a sword's grip, a door's hinge or a creature's joint. The part does not move at rest, and only the"},{"t":"Ragdoll (Selected Limb)","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-limb-ragdoll","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Ragdoll... Right-click A limb ‣ Ragdoll... Ragdoll... on a limb opens the Ragdoll window with the push set to land on that limb. Everything else works as described for the Ragdoll window on the Physics page. See Also Ragdoll"},{"t":"Pin","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-pin","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Pin ‣ Pin here Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Pin ‣ Release here Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Pin ‣ Pin weight Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Pin ‣ Pin blend Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Pin ‣ Bake into keys Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Pin ‣ Delete this pin Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Pin ‣ Delete"},{"t":"Hand on Object","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-twohand","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Pin ‣ Hand on object Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Pin ‣ Other hand on it Right-click A hand while the rig holds an object, or the held object These two rows make a two-handed grip. From the playhead the hand stays where it is on the held object, "},{"t":"Hold the Selected Object","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-hold","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Hold the selected object Right-click A limb ‣ Hold the selected object, or a held object ‣ Put in hand, Let go of it Hold the selected object joins the object you selected in the Explorer to the selected limb while the animation is open"},{"t":"Track","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-limbtrack","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Track ‣ Select track Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Track ‣ Move 1 frame earlier Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Track ‣ Move 1 frame later Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Track ‣ Lock track Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Track ‣ Group Right-click A limb ‣ Track Shortcut Shift + Left a"},{"t":"Constraints (Selected Limb)","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-limbconstraints","x":"Menu Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Constraints Right-click A limb ‣ Constraints The Constraints row in the limb's submenu lists the same constraints as the Constraints submenu of the Pose menu, from Look at to Add constraint..., and each one is made on the selected limb."},{"t":"Held Objects","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#pose-held","x":"Right-clicking an object that a limb holds opens a menu of its own. It offers Key this object, Reset, Held by, Put in hand, Throw or drop, Let go of it, Hide from here or Show from here, Pivot, Pin and Track, so it works much like the menu of a limb. Put in ha"},{"t":"Throw or Drop","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-throw","x":"Right-click A held object ‣ Throw or drop Throw or drop makes a held object let go and fly along gravity's arc to the floor from a frame you choose. The object stays where it lands until a hand takes it again. The dialog says on which frame it lands, and its v"},{"t":"Throws That Bounce","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-bouncethrow","x":"Right-click A held object ‣ Throw or drop ‣ Bounces When the throw is set to Bounces , the thrown object hits the walls, crates and floors of the place, bounces and rolls, and rests where physics leaves it. Framez simulates a copy of the object from the releas"},{"t":"Hide from Here","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-hide","x":"Right-click A held object ‣ Hide from here, Show from here Hide from here makes the held object vanish from the playhead frame on, and Show from here brings it back. The row reads Show from here while the object is hidden. A switch is kept for each frame where"},{"t":"Slider","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#pose-slider","x":"The slider moves a pose between two states while you watch. The Slider submenu opens it in the last mode you used or in any of its eleven modes, and it also holds presets that key a mode at a fixed amount in one click."},{"t":"Slider","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-slider","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Open at the cursor Right-click A key or a limb ‣ Slider Shortcut T The slider is a control that opens at the mouse and moves a pose between two states while you watch. It is quicker than the handles for placing breakdowns, making a pose st"},{"t":"Breakdown","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-slider-breakdown","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Breakdown Shortcut Shift + B Breakdown places the pose between the key before the playhead and the key after it. The left end of the slider is the key before, the right end is the key after, and the middle is halfway between them. With Ove"},{"t":"Blend to Neighbour","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-slider-neighbour","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Blend to neighbour Shortcut Shift + N Blend to neighbour moves the pose as it is toward the key before the playhead (to the left) or the key after it (to the right). The current pose is the starting point, so nothing changes at the middle "},{"t":"Push / Relax","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-slider-push","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Push / relax Shortcut Shift + H Push / relax compares the pose with the line that the keys on either side give at this frame. Moving to the left relaxes the pose onto that line, and at −100 % it is exactly on it. Moving to the right pushes"},{"t":"Blend to Frame","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-slider-frame","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Blend to frame Shortcut Shift + J Blend to frame blends the pose toward the pose on another frame. Click a frame on the ruler to choose it, then move the slider. At +100 % the pose is the pose of that frame, and to the left it moves away f"},{"t":"Scale Amplitude","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-slider-scale","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Scale amplitude Shortcut Shift + M Scale amplitude scales the motion of the selected keys about a pivot pose. At the left end the motion is flat, because every key sits on the pivot, and at the right end it is twice as large. It needs sele"},{"t":"Blend to Default","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-slider-default","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Blend to default Shortcut Shift + U Blend to default moves the pose toward the rest pose. If the Poses window has a saved pose named Default, the pose moves toward that instead. At the right end the pose is exactly the rest or default pose"},{"t":"Time Offset","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-slider-offset","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Time offset Shortcut Shift + O Time offset moves the motion earlier or later while the keys stay on their frames. Each key takes the pose that the motion had a number of frames away. The amount is a number of frames, up to half the length "},{"t":"Blend to Ease","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-slider-ease","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Blend to ease Blend to ease moves the pose toward an eased line between the keys on either side. The left end is a slow start and the right end is a slow end. It has no shortcut of its own, so choose it from the menu or from Menu search. E"},{"t":"Blend Offset","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-slider-blendoffset","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Blend offset Blend offset moves a run of selected keys as one toward the key before the run (to the left) or the key after it (to the right). Every key in the run keeps its relationship to the others. It has no shortcut of its own. Example"},{"t":"Shear","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-slider-shear","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Shear Shear makes a run of selected keys lean. Moving to the left pulls the start of the run back toward the key before it, and moving to the right pushes the end of the run forward toward the key after it. It has no shortcut of its own. E"},{"t":"Match Slope","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-slider-slope","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Match slope Match slope moves the selected keys toward a straight line carried on from the keys beside them. The left end is Slope before, the line that the two keys before the selection make. The right end is Slope after, the line that th"},{"t":"Slider Presets","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-sliderpresets","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Presets ‣ Before Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Presets ‣ 1/3 Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Presets ‣ Half Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Presets ‣ 2/3 Pose ‣ Slider ‣ Presets ‣ After Presets keys the mode that the Inspector's slider currently shows at a fixed amount, at once and wi"},{"t":"Mirror","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#pose-mirror","x":"The Mirror submenu swaps left and right, either for one pose, for the frames of the selected keys or for the whole animation."},{"t":"Mirror Pose","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-mirrorpose","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Mirror ‣ Mirror pose Mirror pose swaps left and right for the whole body at the playhead, or copies one side onto the other. It runs at once at the playhead with the values you used last. Its values open in Adjust , and the result is listed in the "},{"t":"Mirror the Selected Keys","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-mirrorkeys","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Mirror ‣ Mirror the selected keys Mirror the selected keys runs Mirror pose over the frames of the selected keys instead of only at the playhead. The row is greyed out until keys are selected. Its values are the same as for Mirror pose, and they op"},{"t":"Mirror Animation","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-mirrorclip","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Mirror ‣ Mirror animation Mirror animation puts every key of every limb onto its twin, with left and right swapped, and mirrors the root's travel too. Easing, curves and holds go with the keys. Held objects change hands, events and face names swap "},{"t":"Mirror to New Animation","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-mirrornew","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Mirror ‣ Mirror to new animation... Mirror to new animation... mirrors the whole animation, saves the result under a new name and opens it. The saved version of the animation you started from is left as it was. The dialog suggests a name by swappin"},{"t":"Reset Pose","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#pose-reset","x":""},{"t":"Reset Pose","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-resetpose","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Reset pose Reset pose puts every limb of the rig back at its rest pose and keys it at the playhead, as one undo step. It is greyed out while an additive animation layer is selected. Example For example, to start a new animation from a neutral pose,"},{"t":"Constraints","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#pose-constraints","x":"A constraint makes a limb aim at something, go to it, follow it or copy it over the frames you choose. The rows below are starting points that fill in the values of one constraint, and Add constraint... starts from the default values. Driver and Pose controlle"},{"t":"Constraints","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-constraints","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Constraints Right-click A limb ‣ Constraints A constraint links a limb to one or more targets. The targets can be limbs of the rig, objects in the place, fixed points, the camera, or where the limb stands at the start. Each constraint covers a rang"},{"t":"Look At","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-constraint-lookat","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Constraints ‣ Look at Right-click A limb ‣ Constraints ‣ Look at Look at makes an Aim constraint that turns the front of the selected limb toward the target. It is meant for a head. The target is the part or model selected in the Explorer, or a poi"},{"t":"Aim","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-aimconstraint","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Constraints ‣ Aim Right-click A limb ‣ Constraints ‣ Aim Aim makes an Aim constraint that points the bottom of the selected limb at the target, which suits an arm or leg that hangs along its length. The target is the part or model selected in the E"},{"t":"Reach","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-constraint-parent","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Constraints ‣ Reach Right-click A limb ‣ Constraints ‣ Reach Reach sets up a constraint that moves the selected limb to the target's place. It uses the Point kind with the gap turned off, so the limb goes to the target itself rather than staying at"},{"t":"Lock in Place","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-lockinplace","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Constraints ‣ Lock in place Right-click A limb ‣ Constraints ‣ Lock in place Lock in place makes a Follow constraint whose target is where the limb is on the first frame. The limb then stays at that world place and angle while the body around it mo"},{"t":"Limit Distance","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-limitdistance","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Constraints ‣ Limit distance Right-click A limb ‣ Constraints ‣ Limit distance Limit distance makes a Keep a distance constraint. The limb stays no closer than 0 studs and no further than 3 studs from the target, and it is moved back inside that ra"},{"t":"Floor","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-floorconstraint","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Constraints ‣ Floor Right-click A limb ‣ Constraints ‣ Floor Floor makes a Stay above constraint with a height of 0 and no target. The lowest edge of the selected limb is lifted whenever it would go below that height in the world. Change Height in "},{"t":"Follow Path","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-drawpath","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Constraints ‣ Follow path Right-click A limb ‣ Constraints ‣ Follow path Follow path lets you draw one stroke in the viewport that a limb, or the whole rig, then follows. The stroke is kept, so all of it can be changed later. Select a limb first, o"},{"t":"Follow Path on Ground","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-drawpathground","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Constraints ‣ Follow path on ground Right-click A limb ‣ Constraints ‣ Follow path on ground Follow path on ground works like Follow path, except that you draw the stroke on the ground under the rig instead of facing the camera. The values are the "},{"t":"Add Constraint","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-addconstraint","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Constraints ‣ Add constraint... Right-click A limb ‣ Constraints ‣ Add constraint... Add constraint... makes a constraint with the default values and opens them in Adjust. The default is an Aim constraint on the selected limb, over the whole animat"},{"t":"Driver","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-drivers","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Constraints ‣ Driver A Driver makes one value move another. The input can be a joint's turn or place, a part's height or speed in the world, the distance between two parts, or the frame. The output is any channel of any limbs, between two values yo"},{"t":"Pose Controller","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-controllers","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Constraints ‣ Pose controller A Pose controller is a slider or a pad that blends limbs between poses you set. One drag moves many limbs at once, and the value is keyed over the animation. A slider blends between two poses, Low and High, around the "},{"t":"Physics","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#pose-physics","x":"The Physics submenu holds the tools that use motion and physics to make keys. They cover a body turning about a point, a jump arc, parts that trail what moves them, planted feet, a ragdoll fall and a check for spins in the air. Except for the two windows, each"},{"t":"Physics Window","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-menu-physics","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Physics window... View ‣ Windows ‣ Physics Physics window... opens the Physics window, which runs one physics simulation over the animation and writes the result as keys. It is greyed out until an animation is open. The window is describe"},{"t":"Pivot Rotation","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-pivot","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Pivot rotation Pivot rotation spins, flips or rolls the whole body about a point, and keys the result at the playhead. Choose the point, choose how the body turns, and drag the Angle. The body turns as you drag, and planted feet stay put "},{"t":"Ballistic Trajectory","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-arc","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Ballistic trajectory Ballistic trajectory makes the torso fly a true arc between a take-off frame and a landing frame, at a height you set. Between the two frames the root is keyed on a parabola that rises above the straight line from tak"},{"t":"Follow-Through","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-follow","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Follow-through Follow-through is the motion that carries on after whatever moves a part has stopped. It is an arm after a swing, or hair, a cape or a tail. Follow-through makes the head and arms, the selected limb, or a whole chain of joi"},{"t":"Overlap","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-overlap","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Overlap Overlap makes parts start moving a few frames after what carries them. The head and arms, or the limbs you choose, keep part of the turn their parent had a few frames earlier, so a head trails the twist of the torso and arms trail"},{"t":"Foot Lock","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-plant","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Foot lock Foot lock keeps a foot exactly where it is over the frames you choose, and the leg bends to keep it there. On an R15 rig the hip and knee are solved on every frame. On other rigs the leg swings to hold the foot. It needs an unlo"},{"t":"Fix Foot Sliding","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-fixfeet","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Fix foot sliding Fix foot sliding finds every stretch where a foot is on the floor while it slides, and plants it there, as Foot lock does, all in one step. The message says how many slides it fixed, and one Ctrl + Z takes them all back. "},{"t":"Ragdoll","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-menu-ragdoll","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Ragdoll... Ragdoll... opens the Ragdoll window, which pushes the body at a frame and lets Roblox physics take it. It is greyed out until an animation is open. The window is described on the Physics page. See Also Ragdoll"},{"t":"Spin Check","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-spincheck","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Spin check View ‣ Overlays ‣ Spin check In the air nothing turns a body but the body itself. A body turns faster when it is tucked in and slower when it is stretched out, and its centre of mass flies along an arc set by gravity. A flip ke"},{"t":"Fix Spin","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-fixspin","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Fix spin Fix spin keys the torso on every frame through a jump to match the ghosts of Spin check, then thins the keys. The take-off and landing stay as keyed. It needs a take-off and landing at least 4 frames apart, by default, and an unl"},{"t":"Generate","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#pose-generate","x":"The Generate submenu makes keys from a few values. It covers a walk or run cycle, breathing, a swing along a chain and a repeating motion on any channel. Like the tools in the Physics submenu, each one runs at once with the values it used last time and opens t"},{"t":"Locomotion Cycle","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-cycle","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Generate ‣ Locomotion cycle Locomotion cycle builds a looping walk, run, strafe or backpedal for the whole body from a few numbers. The animation becomes exactly one cycle long and is set to loop. The cycle replaces the keys on the unlocked legs, a"},{"t":"Cycle from Poses","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-cyclefromposes","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Generate ‣ Cycle from poses Cycle from poses builds a looping walk or run from two poses that you made. The contact pose has the legs apart with one foot down, and the passing pose has the legs passing under the body. The cycle plays contact, passi"},{"t":"Add Breathing","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-breathe","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Generate ‣ Add breathing Add breathing adds a slow rise and settle of the chest, shoulders and head on top of what is already keyed. An R15 rig breathes in the chest, and an R6 rig, which has one torso block, breathes in the shoulders and the head."},{"t":"Pose from Video","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-posevideo","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Generate ‣ Pose from video Needs The Framez Helper, and a video linked in the Reference window Pose from video keys the torso, head, arms and legs from the person in the linked video, every few frames. The pose is measured from the person's own hip"},{"t":"Anticipation and Overshoot","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-antic","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Generate ‣ Anticipation and overshoot Anticipation and overshoot adds a wind-up before a move and an overshoot after it. At the start the pose is pushed back against the move, and the start pose is held for a few frames before the wind-up. After th"},{"t":"Wave","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-wave","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Generate ‣ Wave Wave makes a swing travel down a chain of joints, such as a tail, a tentacle, hair or a cape. Select the first joint of the chain. Each joint from the selected one down gets a Wiggle modifier, each a little later than the one above "},{"t":"Procedural Motion","p":"Pose Menu","u":"pose.html#f-signals","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Generate ‣ Procedural motion Procedural motion makes a repeating or random motion on any channel of any limbs. The motion can be a wave, noise, steps, a curve you draw, or the motion of another limb. It is written as keys over the frames you choose"},{"t":"Face Menu","p":"Menus","u":"face.html","x":"The Face menu holds the tools for a character's face: posing it, timing mouth shapes to a voice, acting it to a webcam and switching between face pictures. It shows in the Animate and Scene workspaces."},{"t":"Dynamic and Classic Heads","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#face-heads","x":"Roblox has two kinds of head, and the Face tools treat them differently. A dynamic head is a mesh head with a FaceControls object inside it. Its face is moved by 50 face controls, such as Jaw Drop or Left Brow Lowerer, each with a value from 0 to 1. Framez key"},{"t":"Face Controls","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-facecontrols","x":"Menu Face ‣ Face controls The Face row under Head in the track list Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A dynamic head Face controls opens the face map in the Inspector for a head that has FaceControls. Clicking the Face row that Framez adds under Head in the track"},{"t":"Face Map","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-facemap","x":"Menu Face ‣ Face controls Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A dynamic head The face map is the Inspector page for a dynamic head's face. It is a front view of a Roblox head with its default face: brows, oval eyes and a smile. Each handle sits on the part it moves"},{"t":"Face Poses","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-faceposes","x":"Menu Face ‣ Face poses The Face poses button on the face map Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A dynamic head Face poses sets a whole expression in one click. It opens a list of faces. Point at one and the head shows it, move away and the head goes back, and clic"},{"t":"Mirror Face","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-facemirror","x":"Menu Face ‣ Mirror face Right-click A Face row in the track list Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A dynamic head Mirror face swaps every left and right face control at the playhead. A raised left brow becomes a raised right brow, a smirk on the left becomes a sm"},{"t":"Reset Face","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-facereset","x":"Menu Face ‣ Reset face Right-click A Face row in the track list Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A dynamic head Reset face keys every face control that has keys in the clip at 0 on the playhead frame. From that frame on, until the next key, the face is neutral. "},{"t":"Symmetry","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-facesym","x":"Menu Face ‣ Symmetry Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A dynamic head Symmetry is a switch that shows a tick while it is on. With it on, a dot dragged on the face map moves its twin on the other side by the same amount, so both brows lift together or both lip cor"},{"t":"Lip Sync","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-lipsync","x":"Menu Face ‣ Lip sync... Right-click A sound block in the scene, as Lip sync to this sound... Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A dynamic head, or a classic head with a Mouth or face Decal. The Exact method also needs the Framez Helper. Lip sync makes a row of mou"},{"t":"Delete Lip Sync","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-lipdelete","x":"Menu Face ‣ Delete lip sync Right-click A block on the Lip sync row Workspace Animate, Scene Delete lip sync takes the whole lip sync out of the clip, every shape on the Lip sync row. Face keys you made by hand stay. The row is greyed when the clip has no lip "},{"t":"Lip Sync Row","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-liprow","x":"Right-click A shape block on the Lip sync row Workspace Animate, Scene The Lip sync row shows what Lip sync made, as one block per mouth shape. On a dynamic head it sits under the Face row. On a classic head it sits under the Face row of the Decal it changes, "},{"t":"Mouth","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#face-mouth","x":"The Mouth rows set up the pictures or poses that lip sync uses for each of its nine shapes, and add or remove the Mouth Decal on a classic head."},{"t":"Mouth Chart","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-mouthchart","x":"Menu Face ‣ Mouth ‣ Mouth chart... Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A classic head with a Mouth Decal Mouth chart... sets the picture that the Mouth Decal shows for each of the nine lip sync shapes. It opens a chart of nine tiles, each with its letter and sound."},{"t":"Mouth Shapes","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-mouthshapes","x":"Menu Face ‣ Mouth ‣ Mouth shapes... Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A dynamic head Mouth shapes... sets the head's own pose for each of the nine lip sync shapes. Framez starts every head with its own mouth poses, and they may look too small or too wide on a par"},{"t":"Add Mouth Decals","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-givemouth","x":"Menu Face ‣ Mouth ‣ Add mouth decals Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A classic head Add mouth decals adds a Decal named Mouth on the same side of the head as its face Decal, drawn above it, so lip sync has a Decal to change. It shows Framez's own mouth set at o"},{"t":"Remove Mouth Decals","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-delmouth","x":"Menu Face ‣ Mouth ‣ Remove mouth decals Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A Mouth Decal made by Add mouth decals Remove mouth decals takes out the Mouth Decal that Add mouth decals made. It does not touch a Mouth Decal that you added yourself, and the row is grey"},{"t":"Face Capture","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-capture","x":"Menu Face ‣ Face capture... Workspace Animate, Scene Needs The Framez Helper and a webcam Face capture... records your own face with a webcam and puts it on the character as face keys. It opens the Framez Helper's capture page in your browser, which shows the "},{"t":"Face Swaps","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#face-swaps","x":"Face swaps are for a classic head, where the face is a Decal. Every Decal on the head gets a row of its own under Head in the track list. The Decal named face, or the first Decal when none has that name, is the Face row, and any other Decal, such as Mouth, is "},{"t":"Set Face Here","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-face","x":"Menu Face ‣ Face swaps ‣ Set face here... Right-click A Face row in the track list Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A classic head with a face Decal Set face here... chooses which face the head shows from the playhead until the next face key. It opens the face s"},{"t":"Blink Here","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-blink","x":"Menu Face ‣ Face swaps ‣ Blink here Right-click A Face row in the track list Shortcut B Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A classic head with a face Decal Blink here keys the blink face at the playhead. After the blink length, the face that was showing before com"},{"t":"Add Blinks","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-addblinks","x":"Menu Face ‣ Face swaps ‣ Add blinks... Right-click A Face row in the track list Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A classic head with a face Decal, or a dynamic head On a dynamic head, Add blinks... keys the eye-closed face controls instead: Shortest gap and Long"},{"t":"Next Face Here and Previous Face Here","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-facecycle","x":"Menu Face ‣ Face swaps ‣ Next face here Face ‣ Face swaps ‣ Previous face here Shortcut ] for next, [ for previous Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A classic head with a face Decal Next face here and Previous face here change the face at the playhead to the next"},{"t":"Go to Next Face Key and Go to Previous Face Key","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-facestep","x":"Menu Face ‣ Face swaps ‣ Go to next face key Face ‣ Face swaps ‣ Go to previous face key Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A classic head with a face Decal These two rows move the playhead to the next or previous face key and select it. Each is greyed when there "},{"t":"Add Face Decal","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-givehead","x":"Menu Face ‣ Face swaps ‣ Add face decal Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A classic head with no Decal Add face decal appears at the foot of the Face swaps list only when the head has no Decal at all. It adds a Decal named face with Roblox's default Smile, so the"},{"t":"Copy Face, Paste Face Here and Delete Face Key","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-facecopy","x":"Menu Face ‣ Face swaps ‣ Copy face Face ‣ Face swaps ‣ Paste face here Face ‣ Face swaps ‣ Delete face key Right-click A face key on a Face row Workspace Animate, Scene Needs A classic head with a face Decal These three rows work on the face at the playhead. C"},{"t":"Faces in Blender and in the Game","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#face-other","x":"These two entries describe how face animation travels outside Framez."},{"t":"Face Shape Keys from Blender","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-faceblender","x":"Menu File ‣ Import ‣ Get from Blender File ‣ Export ‣ Send to Blender File ‣ Export ‣ Live link with Blender Workspace Animate Needs The Framez Helper, Blender, and a dynamic head Face animation keyed in Blender comes into Framez as keys on the head's face con"},{"t":"Faces in the Game","p":"Face Menu","u":"face.html#f-facecode","x":"Menu Game ‣ In the game ‣ Face swaps in the game... Needs A classic head, and the face script in your game A dynamic head's face plays in any game with no script. A classic head's face swaps need one, because an animation cannot change a Decal. Framez saves ea"},{"t":"Scene Menu","p":"Menus","u":"scene.html","x":"The Scene menu holds everything that turns one animation into a scene: adding rigs, props, cameras, lights, sounds, effects and subtitles, looking through and cutting between cameras, moving props and cameras along paths"},{"t":"About Scenes","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#scene-about","x":"A scene is an animation with more than one thing in it. It begins as soon as you add anything to the animation that is open: another rig, a prop, a camera, a light, a sound, a screen effect, an effect, a path or subtitles. Everything shares the one timeline, s"},{"t":"Saving a Scene","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-scenesave","x":"Menu File ‣ Save Shortcut Ctrl + S Save stores every rig's clip and the scene itself in one step. Each rig's clip is saved under its own rig as an ordinary animation, so Roblox's animation editor can open it too. The scene's record, which holds the props, came"},{"t":"Add","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#scene-add","x":"Add puts another item on the scene's timeline. The same list opens from the + button at the right end of the track list's Filter strip."},{"t":"Add to the Scene","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-sceneadd","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add Track list ‣ + button at the right of the Filter strip Workspace Scene Each row of Add creates one kind of item, and each item gets a row of its own on the timeline. The first thing you add turns the open animation into a scene. Add needs an o"},{"t":"Rig","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-scenerig","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Rig ‣ the rig's name Track list ‣ + ‣ Rig Workspace Scene Rig adds another character to the scene. The list shows every rig in the Workspace that is not in the scene yet, meaning a model whose parts are joined by Motor6D joints. Choosing one"},{"t":"Selected Prop","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-sceneprop","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Selected prop Track list ‣ + ‣ Selected prop Workspace Scene A prop is any part or model without joints that moves through the scene, such as a crate, a door, a weapon on a table or a vehicle. Select it in the Explorer and choose Selected pr"},{"t":"A Prop's Look","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-scenelook","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected prop ‣ Look and Colour Workspace Scene A prop's Look and Colour sections key three values besides its place. Typing a value keys it at the playhead, the same way a light's numbers are keyed. Each keyed value shows as a row under the"},{"t":"Camera from View","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-scenecam","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Camera from view Track list ‣ + ‣ Camera from view Workspace Scene Camera from view adds a camera at the position and angle Studio's viewport has now, with the viewport's field of view, and keys both at the playhead. In the viewport the came"},{"t":"Light","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-scenelight","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Light ‣ the light's name Track list ‣ + ‣ Light Workspace Scene Light adds a PointLight, SpotLight or SurfaceLight that already exists in your place, so that its brightness, colour and range can be keyed. The list shows the lights in the Wor"},{"t":"Sound","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-scenesound","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Sound... Track list ‣ + ‣ Sound... Workspace Scene Sound... puts a sound on the timeline. A box asks for an asset id or link, and also lists the Sounds that are already in the place. The sound starts at the playhead and appears as a block th"},{"t":"Sound Fades and Speed","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-soundfade","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected sound ‣ Fades and Speed Workspace Scene Fades and speed change how a sound plays. Fades are measured in seconds of the timeline from each end of the block, and the two cannot overlap. Speed plays the sound faster or slower, and the "},{"t":"Screen Effects","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-screenfx","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Screen effects Track list ‣ + ‣ Screen effects Workspace Scene Screen effects adds one row that covers the shot with letterbox bars, a vignette, a fade to any colour, camera shake and a subtitle line. A scene has one Screen effects item, so "},{"t":"Effects","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-fxblocks","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Effect Right-click The Effects row Workspace Scene Effect puts an effect on the scene's Effects row at the playhead, already finished: it has a length, and it fades in and out. Choose Camera shake, Fade, Vignette or Letterbox. A scene with n"},{"t":"Scene Look","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-worldlook","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Scene look Track list ‣ + ‣ Scene look Workspace Scene Scene look sets the time of day, fog, colour grade, bloom and depth of field for the shot. A scene has one, so the row is greyed once it exists. Its Inspector page has five sections. Typ"},{"t":"Selected Effect","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-fxtimeline","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Selected effect Track list ‣ + ‣ Selected effect Right-click An effect's row in the track list Selected effect puts particles, trails and beams on the timeline, so they fire on the frames you choose. Select a ParticleEmitter, Trail, Beam, Fi"},{"t":"VFX Packs","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-vfxpacks","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Selected effect, with a pack's part, attachment or model selected Workspace Scene Effect packs from other makers are played the way their maker built them. A pack effect is made with emit attributes on its objects: EmitCount, EmitDelay and E"},{"t":"New Effect on a Limb","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-fxnew","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ New effect on the selected limb ‣ Particles, Trail or Beam Track list ‣ + ‣ New effect on the selected limb Workspace Scene This row makes a new particle effect on the limb that is selected, using Roblox's own defaults so that you can shape "},{"t":"Built-in Effects","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-fxstart","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ New effect on the selected limb ‣ Built-in effects Workspace Scene Built-in effects are ready-made starting points for hits, landings, blasts and casts: Hit sparks, Impact glow, Shock ring, Dust burst, Smoke puff, Fire burst, Energy burst, E"},{"t":"My Effects","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-myfx","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ New effect on the selected limb ‣ My effects Right-click An effect's row ‣ Save as my effect... Workspace Scene My effects keeps an effect you built and lists it ready to add again. Save as my effect... keeps every layer at the effect's poin"},{"t":"Effect Textures","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-fxtexture","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ an effect's page ‣ Texture... Needs The Framez Helper, for Flipbook from frames... Texture... puts an image on a ParticleEmitter, Beam or Trail. It opens a panel of thumbnails with two tabs. Framez holds the textures that come with the plugin,"},{"t":"Place an Effect","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-fxplace","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ an effect's page ‣ Place Right-click An effect's row ‣ Place it Workspace Scene Place moves the point an effect fires from and keys that place. An effect inside a point, an Attachment, has a Place row on the timeline, and its keys move, ease a"},{"t":"Loop Around It","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-fxloop","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ an effect's page ‣ Loop around it Right-click An effect's row ‣ Loop around it Workspace Scene Loop around it plays the frames around an effect over and over while you change the emitter and watch the result. The loop covers the effect's burst"},{"t":"Selected Object","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-keyprop","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Selected object Track list ‣ + ‣ Selected object Right-click A prop's, light's, effect's or object's row ‣ Key a property... Workspace Scene Selected object adds any object selected in the Explorer so that its properties can be keyed. A part"},{"t":"Path","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-path","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Path Track list ‣ + ‣ Path Right-click A path's row Workspace Scene Path draws a path that a prop, a camera or a walking rig can follow. Choosing it hands Studio's mouse to Framez. Each click puts a point where the mouse meets the place, the"},{"t":"Clip Block","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-blocks","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Clip block for the rig Key ‣ Layers ‣ Clip block for the rig Right-click A clip block, or a Clips row Workspace Scene Clip block plays a saved clip as a block over your keys, starting at the playhead, on a Clips row under the rig. The first "},{"t":"Follow Path","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-walkpath","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Follow path: the open rig's name Scene ‣ Add ‣ Follow path: the open rig's name ‣ a path ‣ Your clips ‣ a saved clip Right-click A Clips row ‣ Follow path Workspace Scene Follow path makes the open rig walk or run along a path, starting at t"},{"t":"Subtitles","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-subtitles","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Add ‣ Subtitles Track list ‣ + ‣ Subtitles Right-click A subtitle block, or the Subtitles row Workspace Scene Subtitles puts timed dialogue lines over the shot. Each line is a block on a Subtitles row, marked with a CC symbol, and each block is co"},{"t":"Camera","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#scene-camera","x":"These two rows work on the cameras in the scene."},{"t":"Look Through Camera","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-look-through","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Look through camera Right-click A camera's row ‣ Look through camera Shortcut C Workspace Scene Look through camera shows the shot in the viewport. Studio's view is replaced by the camera the game sees at the playhead, the shot's frame is drawn, t"},{"t":"Key Camera","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-camkey","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Key camera Right-click A camera's row ‣ Key camera Shortcut K Workspace Scene Key camera saves the scene camera's place and field of view as a key at the playhead. It keys the selected camera, or the live camera when no camera is selected. Between"},{"t":"Cuts","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#scene-cuts","x":"With two or more cameras, the Shots row says which camera the game sees. The Cuts submenu starts a new shot and holds the commands of the shot at the playhead."},{"t":"Add Camera Cut","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-cutto","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Cuts ‣ Add camera cut Right-click A camera's row ‣ Add camera cut Workspace Scene Add camera cut starts a shot with the selected camera at the playhead, so the view cuts to that camera on this frame. The cut shows as a block on the Shots row. Cutt"},{"t":"Shots Row","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-shots","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Cuts Right-click A block on the Shots row Workspace Scene The Shots row appears above the cameras once the scene has two. It shows one block for each shot, with the shot's name and its camera. Drag the left edge of a block to move the cut, and Fra"},{"t":"Pose This Rig","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#scene-pose","x":""},{"t":"Pose This Rig","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-scenepose","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Pose this rig ‣ the rig's name Right-click A rig's row Workspace Scene Pose this rig chooses which rig of the scene you are posing. The posed rig's row is open with a \"posing\" tag, and the handles, IK, the Inspector, the Curve editor and every pos"},{"t":"Motion","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#scene-motion","x":"Motion holds the tools that move props and cameras: riding a rig's part, looking at a target, following a path, shaping the path through their keys, and easing keys on scene rows. All but the last need a prop or camera selected in the track list, and are greye"},{"t":"Follow a Part","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-sceneparent","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Motion ‣ Follow a part Right-click A prop's or camera's row ‣ Parent to... Workspace Scene Follow a part makes a prop or a camera ride a part of a rig from the playhead on. The submenu lists the Right hand, Left hand, Head and Torso of each rig. W"},{"t":"Look At","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-lookat","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Motion ‣ Look at Right-click A camera's or prop's row ‣ Look at Workspace Scene Look at turns a camera or a prop to keep a target in view from the playhead on. The submenu lists each rig, up to six, with its Head, Torso, Root, Right hand and Left "},{"t":"Follow a Path","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-ride","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Motion ‣ Follow a path Right-click A camera's or prop's row ‣ Follow a path Workspace Scene Follow a path makes a camera or a prop ride a path from start to end, starting at the playhead. It keeps its height over the ground and the way it faced ag"},{"t":"Moves","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-campath","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Motion ‣ Moves ‣ Smooth path, Straight moves or Even speed Right-click A camera's or prop's row ‣ Moves Workspace Scene Moves sets how a camera or prop travels between its place keys. On a smooth path it moves along a curve through its keyed place"},{"t":"Ease the Selected Keys","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-scenekeys","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Motion ‣ Ease the selected keys Right-click A key on a scene row ‣ Ease to the next key Workspace Scene This sets how an item travels from a scene key to the next one. On a scene row, click a key to select it, add keys with Shift , drag to move th"},{"t":"Framing","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#scene-framing","x":"Framing sets the shape of the shot and the guides drawn over it. The same list opens from the Guides entry of the Camera preview's more menu."},{"t":"Framing","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-framing","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Framing Camera preview ‣ more menu ‣ Guides Workspace Scene Framing sets the shape of the shot and draws composition guides over it while you look through the camera. The aspect ratio belongs to the scene and is saved with it. The guides belong to"},{"t":"More Guides","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-moreguides","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Framing ‣ More guides Workspace Scene More guides holds the extra composition guides. Each one is a switch that draws over the shot while you look through the camera and in the Camera preview, and you can have any number on at once. Options Grid D"},{"t":"Guide Sets","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-guidesets","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Framing ‣ Guide sets Workspace Scene A guide set saves the guides that are on, with their settings, under a name, so that you can bring the same combination back in one click. Sets are kept with Studio and are not part of any scene. Options Save t"},{"t":"Guide Settings","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-guidesettings","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Framing ‣ Guide settings... Workspace Scene Guide settings opens a box with the numbers behind the guides. Press Apply to keep the values. Cancel, or Esc , puts back the values the box opened with. Options Grid columns, Grid rows The size of the G"},{"t":"Put in the Game","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#scene-game","x":""},{"t":"Put in the Game","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-sceneexport","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Put in the game... Game ‣ In the game ‣ Put the scene in the game... Workspace Scene Needs A scene with a second item, saved with a name Put in the game... writes the scene into your game as something players can play. The scene can start from a b"},{"t":"Playing a Scene in the Game","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-sceneplay","x":"Needs A scene put in the game A scene that starts from Code is played from your own script. Only a client can take the camera, so a cutscene is played from a LocalScript. The scene's rigs are matched by the names they had in Framez, and origin says where in th"},{"t":"Scene Settings","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#scene-settings","x":"Two settings for working in a scene. Both are remembered by Studio."},{"t":"Auto Key","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-scenesettings","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Scene settings ‣ Auto key Workspace Scene Auto key decides whether a prop, a camera or an effect's point moved with Studio's tools, or a camera flown while you look through it, is keyed once it comes to rest. It is on by default. With it on, the k"},{"t":"Audio Scrubbing","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-scrubsound","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Scene settings ‣ Audio scrubbing ‣ Off, One frame, 80 ms or 150 ms Workspace Scene Audio scrubbing plays a slice of each sound under the playhead while you scrub. Each time the frame changes while Framez is not playing, by dragging the playhead, w"},{"t":"Delete","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#scene-delete","x":""},{"t":"Delete","p":"Scene Menu","u":"scene.html#f-scenedelete","x":"Menu Scene ‣ Delete Right-click An item's row ‣ Delete Shortcut Del Workspace Scene Delete takes the selected item out of the scene. The label names the item, for example Delete Camera. With nothing selected it removes the rig you are posing, as long as that i"},{"t":"Game Menu","p":"Menus","u":"game.html","x":"The Game menu holds what a game needs to know about a clip: events a script can hear, screen looks for hits, hit windows, foot contacts and body masks, the previews that show how Roblox will play the clip, and the script"},{"t":"Events","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#game-events","x":"An event names a moment of the clip. When the clip plays in a game, a script can listen for that name and run its own code on that frame."},{"t":"Add Event Here","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-events","x":"Menu Game ‣ Add event here... Right-click The timeline Workspace Game data An event names a moment of the clip: Hit, Footstep, Sound, SpawnVFX or any word you choose, with an optional value such as Slash1 or Left. When you save, each event is written as a Robl"},{"t":"Edit Event Here and Delete Event Here","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-eventrows","x":"Menu Game ‣ Events ‣ Edit event here... Game ‣ Events ‣ Delete event here Right-click An event pin on the events lane Workspace Game data Edit event here... changes the name and value of the event at the playhead. It opens the same dialog as Add event here, wi"},{"t":"Go to Event","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-eventgo","x":"Menu Game ‣ Events ‣ Go to event ‣ Next event Game ‣ Events ‣ Go to event ‣ Previous event Workspace Game data Next event and Previous event move the playhead to the next or previous frame that has an event. The status line names the events on that frame. Each"},{"t":"Hearing Events in a Script","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-eventcode","x":"Needs A game script Each event fires AnimationTrack:GetMarkerReachedSignal(name) when the playing clip reaches its frame, and the event's value is passed to the function you connect. local track = animator:LoadAnimation(attackAnimation) track:GetMarkerReachedS"},{"t":"Impact and Flash","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#game-impact","x":"An impact frame or a flash is a look the screen takes for a few frames, such as a white frame on a hard hit. Each one is an event with a look attached, and you build the look in the Inspector."},{"t":"Impact Frame Here","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-impact","x":"Menu Game ‣ Impact and flash ‣ Impact frame here Right-click The timeline Workspace Game data Impact frame here adds an Impact event at the playhead and opens its page in the Inspector. The page builds a look for the hit: a washed-out or darkened world, a tint"},{"t":"Flash Here","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-flash","x":"Menu Game ‣ Impact and flash ‣ Flash here Right-click The timeline Workspace Game data Flash here adds a Flash event at the playhead and opens the same page as an impact frame. It starts from the look you used last for a flash, or from the Flash starting point"},{"t":"Impact Frames and Flashes in the Game","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-impactgame","x":"Menu Game ‣ In the game ‣ Effects in the game Needs The FramezEffects script in your game Effects in the game adds the FramezEffects script, which plays impact frames and flashes whenever the clip plays. It plays every look as built. A look set to Seen by Ever"},{"t":"Hit Windows","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#game-hits","x":"A hit window marks a range of frames that the game reads: an attack that can hurt, a parry, invulnerable frames, or the frames in which a combo can be cancelled. Each window has a kind, a name, a first and last frame, an optional hitbox that follows a part of "},{"t":"Add Hit Window Here","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-hits","x":"Menu Game ‣ Hit windows ‣ Add hit window here Right-click The Hit windows row Workspace Game data Add hit window here starts a Hit window at the playhead and selects it. The window is named Hit1, then Hit2 and so on, and it ends four frames after it starts. It"},{"t":"Add Window Here","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-hitkinds","x":"Menu Game ‣ Hit windows ‣ Add window here Right-click The Hit windows row Workspace Game data Add window here starts a window of the kind you choose at the playhead. The kind is a word that the game reads, and the window is named after it, such as Parry1. Only"},{"t":"Edit Hitbox in the View","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-hitview","x":"Menu Game ‣ Hit windows ‣ Edit hitbox in the view Right-click A bar on the Hit windows row Workspace Game data Edit hitbox in the view turns the selected window's hitbox into a real part in Studio's selection, so you can shape it in the viewport with Studio's "},{"t":"Show Hitboxes","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-hitshow","x":"Menu Game ‣ Hit windows ‣ Show hitboxes Right-click The Hit windows row Workspace Game data Show hitboxes is a switch that shows a tick while it is on, and it is on by default. It draws every hitbox in the viewport while the playhead is inside its window, and "},{"t":"Hit Window Page","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-hitpage","x":"Menu Inspector, while a hit window is selected Workspace Game data The Inspector shows this page when you select a window on the Hit windows row. It is named after the window's kind, such as Hit window, and holds the window's frames, its hitbox and its data. O"},{"t":"Foot Contacts","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#game-contacts","x":"Foot contacts record when each foot is on the floor. They show on the Foot contacts row as one strip for each foot, left above right, with a bar while the foot is down. A game uses them to play footstep sounds and dust at the right frames, and to start a walk "},{"t":"Find Foot Contacts","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-contacts","x":"Menu Game ‣ Foot contacts ‣ Find foot contacts Right-click The Foot contacts row Workspace Game data Find foot contacts reads the contacts off the clip as it plays, animation layers and clip blocks included. For each foot, Framez finds the lowest point its sol"},{"t":"Foot Down Here","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-contactadd","x":"Menu Game ‣ Foot contacts ‣ LeftFoot down here Game ‣ Foot contacts ‣ RightFoot down here Right-click The Foot contacts row Workspace Game data These rows add a contact by hand at the playhead. There is one row for each foot of the rig, and its label is the fo"},{"t":"Clear Foot Contacts","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-contactclear","x":"Menu Game ‣ Foot contacts ‣ Clear foot contacts Right-click The Foot contacts row Workspace Game data Clear foot contacts removes every contact from the clip as one undo step. It is greyed when there are none."},{"t":"Body Mask","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#game-mask","x":"A body mask sets which parts of the rig a clip plays on in the game. The clip keeps every key in Framez, and the mask decides what is written when you save."},{"t":"Body Mask","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-mask","x":"Menu Game ‣ Body mask Inspector ‣ Clip ‣ In the game Workspace Game data A clip with a mask saves keys only for the parts in the mask. In the game, the parts outside the mask play whatever animation plays beneath this one. An attack masked to Upper body plays "},{"t":"Edit Mask","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-maskdialog","x":"Menu Game ‣ Body mask ‣ Edit mask... The Body mask list of a layer or a clip block Workspace Game data Edit mask... makes any mask you like, as a name and the parts it covers, shown as the rig's tree. Each part is on or off and has a share. A share of 100 perc"},{"t":"Add a Masked Copy","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-maskcopy","x":"Menu Game ‣ Body mask ‣ Add a masked copy ‣ Upper body The same list for Lower body, Arms, Legs, Head and your own masks Workspace Game data Add a masked copy saves another clip from the same keys beside this one, with a mask of its own. The copy's name is the"},{"t":"Previews","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#game-preview","x":"These rows show how the clip plays in Roblox, alone or with another save of the same rig."},{"t":"Animator Preview","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-roblox","x":"Menu Game ‣ Animator preview Workspace Game data Animator preview plays the clip on a see-through copy of the rig that stands beside it, through Roblox's own Animator, which is what a game uses. The clip is saved the way the game receives it, with eased spans "},{"t":"Test Bench","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-bench","x":"Menu Game ‣ Test bench... Workspace Game data Test bench... opens a floating window that tests how the clip plays in Roblox: in a running game on your character, joined to the clips around it, on other body shapes, under a game camera, and against other versio"},{"t":"Transition To","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-join","x":"Menu Game ‣ Preview with another clip ‣ Transition to Workspace Game data Transition to previews how another clip joins this one. Choose another save of the rig from the list, and Framez plays the last half second of that save, then this clip from its start, w"},{"t":"Blend With","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-blend","x":"Menu Game ‣ Preview with another clip ‣ Blend with Workspace Game data Blend with plays this clip and another save of the rig together in a Roblox Blend1D, on the see-through copy beside the rig, through Roblox's own Animator. A Blend1D mixes two clips by a nu"},{"t":"Match Steps","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-match","x":"Menu Game ‣ Preview with another clip ‣ Blend with ‣ the Match steps button Workspace Game data Match steps retimes this clip so its left foot lands at the same point of its loop as the left foot does in the other clip, and it stretches each half of the loop t"},{"t":"Additive","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-addtop","x":"Menu Game ‣ Preview with another clip ‣ Additive Workspace Game data Additive plays another save of the rig added over this clip, through Roblox's Add node, on the see-through copy beside the rig. The other save is read as a change from the rest pose, which is"},{"t":"Directional Blend","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-dirs","x":"Menu Game ‣ Preview with another clip ‣ Directional blend... Workspace Game data Directional blend... plays up to five saves in a Roblox Blend2D on the see-through copy beside the rig: an idle at the centre, and then forward, back, left and right. Each save ha"},{"t":"In the Game","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#game-ingame","x":"These rows add scripts to your game, or take them out again. Each script reads what Framez saved in the clip and does the work when the clip plays. They are added to the place you have open. If Studio stops Framez from adding scripts, allow Script injection fo"},{"t":"Put the Scene in the Game","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-gameput","x":"Menu Game ‣ In the game ‣ Put the scene in the game... Workspace Game data Needs An open scene Put the scene in the game... opens the dialog that writes the open scene into the game, with its player, its start and the scripts it needs. It is the same command a"},{"t":"Face Swaps in the Game","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-facegame","x":"Menu Game ‣ In the game ‣ Face swaps in the game... Right-click A Face row in the track list Workspace Game data Face swaps in the game... says whether this game has the face script and adds it. The script is a ModuleScript named FramezFace in ReplicatedStorag"},{"t":"Held Objects in the Game","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-heldgame","x":"Menu Game ‣ In the game ‣ Held objects in the game Workspace Game data Held objects in the game adds a ModuleScript named FramezHeld to ReplicatedStorage and a LocalScript named FramezHeldClient to StarterPlayerScripts. With them, every clip in which an object"},{"t":"Effects in the Game","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-fxgame","x":"Menu Game ‣ In the game ‣ Effects in the game Workspace Game data Effects in the game adds a ModuleScript named FramezEffects to ReplicatedStorage and a LocalScript named FramezEffectsClient to StarterPlayerScripts. They play impact frames, flashes and squash "},{"t":"Hit Windows and Foot Contacts","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-gamedata","x":"Menu Game ‣ In the game ‣ Hit windows and foot contacts Workspace Game data Hit windows and foot contacts adds a ModuleScript named FramezGameData to ReplicatedStorage. It turns a clip's hit windows into hits and its foot contacts into footsteps, on the server"},{"t":"Delete Held Objects from the Game","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-delheld","x":"Menu Game ‣ In the game ‣ Delete held objects from the game Workspace Game data Delete held objects from the game takes FramezHeld and FramezHeldClient out of the game. It asks first, because undo does not bring them back. The row is greyed when the game has n"},{"t":"Delete Effects from the Game","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-delfx","x":"Menu Game ‣ In the game ‣ Delete effects from the game Workspace Game data Delete effects from the game takes the FramezEffects script out of the game, and its LocalScript too unless you changed it. Ctrl + Z brings them back. To add them again, use Effects in "},{"t":"Delete Hit Windows and Foot Contacts from the Game","p":"Game Menu","u":"game.html#f-delgamedata","x":"Menu Game ‣ In the game ‣ Delete hit windows and foot contacts from the game Workspace Game data Delete hit windows and foot contacts from the game takes FramezGameData out of the game. Ctrl + Z brings it back. To add it again, use Hit windows and foot contact"},{"t":"View Menu","p":"Menus","u":"view.html","x":"The View menu controls what you see while you work and how the clip plays back. It holds the overlays drawn over the rig, the camera, the open windows, hidden limbs, the reference video, and playback speed and zoom. Noth"},{"t":"Overlays","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#view-overlays","x":"Overlays are aids drawn over the rig and the timeline. Each one is switched on and off from its own row, and the Animate workspace groups them under Rig and Motion. The same list opens when you right-click the eye button on the toolbar. The list also holds Spi"},{"t":"Handles","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-ov-handles","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Handles Shortcut P Workspace Animate Handles are the rings and arrows you drag to turn and move the selected limb. The row is ticked while they show, which is how Framez starts. Switching them off clears the rings from the rig so you can"},{"t":"Controls","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-ov-controls","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Controls Needs A control rig Workspace Animate Controls shows or hides the shapes of the control rig. The row is ticked while they show, which is how Framez starts. It is greyed, with the reason, until the rig has a control rig. With the"},{"t":"Limb Boxes","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-ov-limbboxes","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Limb boxes Workspace Animate Limb boxes draws a box round each limb of the rig. The boxes are off when you first use Framez. Without them you select a limb by clicking the limb itself, and the selected limb is outlined in orange. Example"},{"t":"Bones","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-bones","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Bones Workspace Animate Bones draws each joint of the rig as a bone, from the point the limb turns about to the next joint down the chain. For a hand, a foot, the head or a held weapon the bone runs to the far end of the part. Clicking a"},{"t":"Side Letters","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-ov-sideletters","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Side letters Workspace Animate An R6 arm or leg is the same colour on every side, so a limb that has turned a quarter of a turn about its own length can look unchanged. Side letters puts a colour and a letter on each face of every arm an"},{"t":"Silhouette","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-silhouette","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Silhouette Workspace Animate Silhouette draws the whole rig as one flat black shape. A pose that reads clearly as an outline will read clearly in the game, where characters are small and seen against a busy background. Example For exampl"},{"t":"Centre of Mass","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-com","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Centre of mass Workspace Animate Centre of mass draws a dot where the weight of the body is, with a line down to the floor and a disc where the line lands. Each part of the rig counts in proportion to its size, and held weapons are left "},{"t":"Motion Paths","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-paths","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Motion paths Right-click The motion paths button on the toolbar Workspace Animate Motion paths draw the route that the hands, feet, head, weapon tip or torso travel through the clip. A dot marks every frame, so the spacing of the dots sh"},{"t":"Onion Skin","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-onion","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Onion skin Right-click The onion button on the toolbar Workspace Animate Onion skin shows ghosts of the rig at the keys on either side of the playhead, so you can compare the pose you are working on with the ones around it. The ghost bef"},{"t":"Onion Captures","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-onioncapture","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Onion captures Right-click The onion button on the toolbar Workspace Animate Onion captures keep the rig as it stands now as a pink ghost that stays in place while you scrub and key. You can keep up to four, with the newest the clearest."},{"t":"Annotate","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-annotate","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Annotate Workspace Animate Annotate lets you draw lines over the viewport, the way you would sketch on a printed frame. A bar appears at the top of the viewport and the pen takes the mouse, so a drag draws a line instead of selecting som"},{"t":"Foot Sliding","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-slides","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Foot sliding Workspace Animate Foot sliding looks through the clip for frames where a foot that is on the floor moves along it, and marks each one with a small red mark in the events lane above the timeline. Pointing at a mark names the "},{"t":"Floor","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-ov-floor","x":"Menu View ‣ Overlays ‣ Floor Floor adds a flat grey slab, 64 studs square, directly under the lowest point of the rig, so you have a surface to judge foot contact against at the height the character stands. The slab does not collide with anything and is not sa"},{"t":"Camera","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#view-camera","x":"The Camera submenu moves Studio's camera around the rig, saves the angles you want back, and holds the navigation and green screen options."},{"t":"Frame Selected","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-camera","x":"Menu View ‣ Camera ‣ Frame selected Shortcut F , Num . Frame selected moves the camera back along its line of sight until the selected limb fills the view. The camera keeps its direction and glides to the new place over a fraction of a second. With no limb sel"},{"t":"Frame All","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-cam-frameall","x":"Menu View ‣ Camera ‣ Frame all Frame all moves the camera back until the whole rig fits in the view, keeping the direction the camera looks in. It frames the whole body whichever limb is selected."},{"t":"Views","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-cam-views","x":"Menu View ‣ Camera ‣ Views The Views submenu places the camera at a standard angle on the whole rig. The angles follow the character, not the world, so Front is always the side the character faces however it is turned. The camera glides to the new view. Option"},{"t":"Saved Views","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-cam-saved","x":"Menu View ‣ Camera ‣ Saved views A saved view keeps the position and field of view of Studio's camera, so you can come back to it later. The views are kept with the animation, one set for each animation, and each is stored relative to the rig's root, so moving"},{"t":"Follow Rig","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-cam-follow","x":"Menu View ‣ Camera ‣ Follow rig With Follow rig ticked, the camera keeps its distance from the torso while the clip plays, so a rig that travels stays in the frame. When playback stops, the camera stays where it ended up. The camera does not follow while you s"},{"t":"Blender Navigation","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-blender-nav","x":"Menu View ‣ Camera ‣ Blender navigation Blender navigation changes how the mouse moves Studio's camera. It orbits, pans and zooms the way Blender's viewport does, so one set of mouse moves works in both programs. It applies while the Framez window is open, and"},{"t":"Green Screen","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-greenscreen","x":"Menu View ‣ Camera ‣ Green screen View ‣ Camera ‣ Green screen settings... Green screen shows the rigs over one flat colour in the viewport, in the camera preview and in a playblast, ready to be keyed out in a video editor. Everything else in the place is hidd"},{"t":"Windows","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#view-windows","x":"The Windows submenu opens and closes Framez's windows and switches the workspace. Its list depends on the workspace, and a window that is open shows a tick."},{"t":"Inspector","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-inspector","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Inspector Opens or closes the Inspector, the window that shows the numbers and settings of whatever is selected. It is available in all three workspaces. Framez opens it docked on the right the first time, and after that Studio remembers "},{"t":"Reference Video","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-reference","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Reference video Workspace Animate Opens or closes the Reference video window, which plays a video or a set of image frames beside your animation, locked to the timeline. See Also The Reference Video page describes the window."},{"t":"Poses","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-poses","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Poses Workspace Animate Opens or closes the Poses window, where you save poses with a picture of each and key them back with a click. See Also The Poses page describes the window."},{"t":"Curve Editor","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-curves","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Curve editor Workspace Animate Opens or closes the Curve editor, which draws each channel of the selected limb as a line over time and lets you reshape the motion between keys. See Also The Curve Editor page describes the window."},{"t":"Physics","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-physics","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Physics Opens or closes the Physics window, where one simulation for the clip is set up from bodies, forces and colliders. It is available in all three workspaces. See Also Physics Window describes how to use it."},{"t":"Modifiers","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-modifiers","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Modifiers Opens or closes the Modifiers window, which lists the results that tools have made, so that you can change them afterwards. It is available in all three workspaces. See Also How every tool works explains how a tool's result beco"},{"t":"Subtitle Lines","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-sublines","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Subtitle lines Workspace Scene Opens or closes the window that lists the subtitle lines of the scene. See Also Subtitle Lines describes the window."},{"t":"Camera Preview","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-shotview","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Camera preview Workspace Scene Opens or closes the Camera preview, which shows the shot as one of the scene's cameras sees it. See Also Camera Preview describes the window."},{"t":"Check Animation","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-check","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Check animation Workspace Animate Opens or closes the window that holds the list Check animation makes. Closing the window leaves a way back to the list from this row. See Also Check Animation describes what the check looks for."},{"t":"Animate, Scene and Game Data","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-workspace","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Animate View ‣ Windows ‣ Scene View ‣ Windows ‣ Game data These three rows switch the workspace, and the one you are in is marked. A workspace changes only what the window shows, not the animation. The Animate workspace is for posing and "},{"t":"Reset This Workspace","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-resetws","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Reset this workspace Puts the current workspace back as it started, with the windows, the Inspector page and the overlays it opened with. Other workspaces are not touched, and neither is the animation."},{"t":"Track Groups","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-groups","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Track groups Switches the track list between groups and a plain tree. A rig with many joints, such as a creature with dozens of bones, lists its tracks in groups. Framez makes a group from each chain of joints between one fork and the nex"},{"t":"Reset Window Layout","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-win-resetlayout","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Reset window layout Puts every Framez window back where it was docked or floated when you first used it. Use it when a window has been moved off screen or onto a monitor you no longer have."},{"t":"Hide","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#view-hide","x":"Hiding works on the view only. A hidden part gets a transparency that Roblox never saves, nothing is keyed, and the game never sees it. The same three rows are in the right-click menu of a limb, under Hide."},{"t":"Hide","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-hideview","x":"Menu View ‣ Hide ‣ Hide Right-click A limb ‣ Hide ‣ Hide Shortcut H Hide hides the selected limb and the parts it carries, such as a hat or a weapon on its weld, in Studio's view. The row reads Show while the selected limb is already hidden, and choosing it br"},{"t":"Isolate","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-hide-isolate","x":"Menu View ‣ Hide ‣ Isolate Right-click A limb ‣ Hide ‣ Isolate Shortcut Ctrl + H Isolate keeps the selected limb, and the limbs that hang from it, in view and hides every other joint of the rig. In a scene with more than one rig, an Isolate rig row also appear"},{"t":"Show All","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-hide-showall","x":"Menu View ‣ Hide ‣ Show all Right-click A limb ‣ Hide ‣ Show all Shortcut Alt + H Show all brings back everything that has been hidden or isolated. The row is greyed while nothing is hidden."},{"t":"Reference","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#view-reference","x":"The Reference submenu holds the commands that place the reference video over the viewport and mark points on it. They work with the Reference video window, which has to hold a clip or a set of image frames."},{"t":"Image Plane","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-viewref","x":"Menu View ‣ Reference ‣ Image plane Workspace Animate Image plane lays the current reference frame, see-through, over the viewport, so you can pose the rig on top of the picture. The row is ticked while the picture shows. See Also Image Plane on the Reference "},{"t":"Image Plane Settings","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-viewref-settings","x":"Menu View ‣ Reference ‣ Image plane settings... Workspace Animate Image plane settings... opens a small box with the opacity, size and place of the picture over the viewport, as numbers, along with a Reset button. See Also Image Plane describes each value."},{"t":"Mark the Weapon Tip","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-viewref-mark","x":"Menu View ‣ Reference ‣ Mark the weapon tip Workspace Animate Mark the weapon tip starts marking on the reference picture. While it is ticked, a click on the picture places a mark for the current frame, where the weapon tip is. A right-click takes the mark off"},{"t":"Mark Options","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-viewref-markopts","x":"Menu View ‣ Reference ‣ Mark options... Workspace Animate Mark options... chooses what you mark, which can be the weapon tip, a hand or a foot, and clears the marks of this clip. See Also Marks describes each choice."},{"t":"Stepped Preview","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-stepped","x":"Menu View ‣ Stepped preview Workspace Animate Stepped preview plays and scrubs the clip in steps, so you can judge its timing before the in-betweens are polished. Each pose is held for a set number of frames instead of moving smoothly through them. It changes "},{"t":"Bookmarks","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#view-bookmarks","x":"Bookmarks mark frames or stretches of the clip with a name and a colour, so you can jump between them. They are kept with the Framez data and never reach the game clip."},{"t":"Add Bookmark Here","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-bookmarks","x":"Menu View ‣ Bookmarks ‣ Add bookmark here... Right-click The ruler Workspace Animate Add bookmark here... opens a dialog for a bookmark at the playhead. A bookmark can be a single frame, which shows as a flag in the Bookmarks lane under the events lane, or a r"},{"t":"Next Bookmark and Previous Bookmark","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-bookmark-nav","x":"Menu View ‣ Bookmarks ‣ Next bookmark View ‣ Bookmarks ‣ Previous bookmark Shortcut Ctrl + Right , Ctrl + Left Workspace Animate Next bookmark moves the playhead to the first bookmark after it, and Previous bookmark moves it to the nearest one before it. Both "},{"t":"Bookmarks Lane","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-bookmark-lane","x":"Right-click A bookmark in the Bookmarks lane Workspace Animate The Bookmarks lane sits under the events lane and shows when the clip has a bookmark. Clicking a bookmark goes to its frame, dragging it moves it, and double-clicking it opens the dialog to rename "},{"t":"Play Only the Selected Keys","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-range","x":"Menu View ‣ Play only the selected keys Workspace Animate With this row ticked, playback loops only the stretch between the first and last selected keys, round and round, instead of the whole clip. The stretch shows as a red band on the ruler. Unticking the ro"},{"t":"Playback Speed","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-speed","x":"Menu View ‣ Playback speed Playback speed sets how fast Framez plays the clip while you work. It changes playback inside Framez only. The animation, the frame rate and the saved clip are not affected. Options 0.25x, 0.5x, 1x, 2x Quarter speed, half speed, norm"},{"t":"Timeline Zoom","p":"View Menu","u":"view.html#f-view-zoom","x":"Menu View ‣ Timeline zoom Timeline zoom changes how much of the clip the timeline shows. It affects the view only. Options Frame all Fits the whole clip in the timeline. Zoom in, Zoom out Show fewer or more frames at once, about the middle of what is on screen"},{"t":"Help Menu","p":"Menus","u":"help.html","x":"The Help menu points to this manual and to the Framez Helper, shows whether the helper is running, lists the other people animating in the same Team Create place, and opens the Scripting window. Studio plugins cannot ope"},{"t":"Framez Manual","p":"Help Menu","u":"help.html#f-helpguide","x":"Menu Help ‣ Framez manual Framez manual shows the link to this manual, which covers every window, menu and shortcut, as well as the setup for video reference and Blender. The link is in a box that selects itself when you click it, so Ctrl + C copies it. Exampl"},{"t":"Get the Framez Helper","p":"Help Menu","u":"help.html#f-helpergetit","x":"Menu Help ‣ Get the Framez Helper Get the Framez Helper shows where to download the helper, the free Windows app that video reference, beats, joints from video, the Blender link, exact lip sync and webcam capture need. Image frames work without it. The box hol"},{"t":"Helper Status","p":"Help Menu","u":"help.html#f-helpercheck","x":"Menu Help ‣ Helper status Helper status reports in the status line whether the Framez Helper is running. When it is, the message gives its version and the clip folders it reads. When it is not, the message says why. Notes If nothing answers on this computer, t"},{"t":"Team","p":"Help Menu","u":"help.html#f-team","x":"Menu Help ‣ Team Needs A Team Create place Several people can animate in one Team Create place, one rig each. Nothing needs to be set up, because everyone who runs Framez takes part automatically. The Team row lists the other people who have Framez open, and e"},{"t":"Scripting","p":"Help Menu","u":"help.html#f-openscripting","x":"Menu Help ‣ Scripting... Scripting opens the Scripting window, which lists the functions that let Studio's Command Bar or a plugin of your own work on the open animation, with an example you can copy. The window and its functions are described on the Scripting"},{"t":"Inspector","p":"Windows","u":"inspector.html","x":"The Inspector shows the numbers and settings of whatever is selected and lets you type them. It has up to three pages, chosen with the switch at the top. The first page is named after the selection, such as the selected "},{"t":"Working in the Inspector","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#inspector-general","x":""},{"t":"Sections and Controls","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspectorui","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Inspector Every page is a stack of sections. A section folds when you click its name or the arrow beside it, and Framez remembers which ones are folded. Animation layers, Preview and In the game start folded. A short note beside a section"},{"t":"The First Page","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#inspector-key","x":"The first page changes with what is selected. Its sections are in the order described here, and a section that does not apply to the selection is left out."},{"t":"The First Page","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-insppage","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page The first page is named after what is selected. For a limb it carries the limb's name. With nothing selected it is called Key. It reads Face while a Face row is selected, Physics while a row is selected in the Physics window, and ta"},{"t":"Foot and Hand","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspfoot","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Foot Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Hand Needs A control rig Workspace Animate The Foot section appears when you select a leg that has a control, and the Hand section when you select an arm that has one. It comes first on the page, befo"},{"t":"Curve","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspcurve","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Curve Needs A control rig with a curve control Workspace Animate The Curve section appears when you select a joint of a chain that has a curve control, such as a tail, a cape or a rope. The control lays the chain along a smooth cu"},{"t":"Look at Control","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-insplookat","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Look at Needs A control rig with a Look at control Workspace Animate The Look at control is a yellow ball in front of the face. The head turns toward the ball by the shortest swing from the pose its own keys give, and its roll is "},{"t":"Rotation and Position","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-insprotpos","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Rotation Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Position These two sections show the selected limb's turn and move as numbers, in the limb's own axes. X is the red ring, Y is the green ring and Z is the blue ring. Rotation is the turn about the"},{"t":"Leg and Arm","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-ik","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Leg Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Arm This section appears for a selected arm or leg and sets how that limb is posed. With FK , dragging a limb swings the whole limb from its hip or shoulder. With IK , you drag the foot or hand and the"},{"t":"Limits","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-insplimits","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Limits Workspace Animate The Limits section sets how far the selected joint may turn while you pose. The note beside the name says whether the joint is a ball, a hinge or free. It is shown for limbs that have a parent and are not "},{"t":"Full-Body IK","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspfullbody","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Full-body IK Workspace Animate This section sets how the body gives when Full-Body IK is on, and it is shown only while that is on. It appears for an arm or a leg, the torso and the root. The values run from 0% to 100% and are kep"},{"t":"Squash and Stretch","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspsquash","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Squash and stretch Pose ‣ Posing ‣ Squash and stretch ‣ Open the settings Workspace Animate This section squashes or stretches the selected part, or the whole body, and keys the size. A note reading keyed shows beside the name onc"},{"t":"Controllers","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspcontrollers","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Controllers Workspace Animate The Controllers section lets you drag a pose controller to blend its limbs between the poses you set for it. It lists every pose controller that is switched on, with a count beside the name, and it ap"},{"t":"Parent","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspparent","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Parent The Parent section shows what the selected limb, the whole rig, a held object, a prop or a camera rides, and lists every switch of parent. It is the Inspector's side of Parent to... , which makes the switches. A switch mean"},{"t":"Slider","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inbetween","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Slider Workspace Animate The Slider section is the slider built into the Inspector. A list of modes sits over a bar, and you drag the knob to change the pose. The knob springs back to the middle after each drag, so a second drag w"},{"t":"Easing and Extra Turns","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-spin","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Easing The Easing section sets how the limb speeds up and slows down on its way from the selected key to the next. It appears when one key is selected and that key has a key after it, and the note beside its name gives the two fra"},{"t":"Mirror and Reset Limb","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspmirror","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page Two buttons end the page of a selected limb. Mirror onto , followed by the name of the limb's twin as in Mirror onto LeftHand, copies the pose of the selected limb, mirrored, onto the opposite limb at this frame. A limb with no twin"},{"t":"Several Keys","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspmany","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page ‣ keys When more than one key is selected, the first page has a section named by the count, such as 4 keys on 2 limbs. It holds a dropdown with the easing, where choosing a style or where it slows applies the choice to every selecte"},{"t":"Nothing Selected","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspnone","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ Key With no limb or key selected, the first page is called Key and offers what can be done at the playhead. The At frame section, named with the current frame, has Insert key on all limbs , which keys every limb of the rig, and Copy pose and P"},{"t":"The Clip Page","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#inspector-clip","x":"The Clip page holds the settings of the whole animation. Which sections it shows depends on the workspace: Animate shows Held objects and Animation layers, and Game data shows Preview and In the game."},{"t":"Clip","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspclip","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ Clip Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Animation settings The Clip section holds the clip's own settings, in one place. Priority and Looping are written to the saved clip the next time you save it. Options Length The length of the timeline, in frames. A bare "},{"t":"Held Objects","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-weapon","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ Clip ‣ Held objects Inspector ‣ First page ‣ Held by Workspace Animate A held object is anything on the rig that is not part of the body, such as a game weapon, a part joined to the rig or a hat. This section sets who holds each one from a cho"},{"t":"Animation Layers","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-animlayers","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ Clip ‣ Animation layers Key ‣ Layers Workspace Animate Animation layers hold keys of their own on top of the clip, and they are saved with it. The base is the clip's own keys. The list shows the layers with the top one first and the base last,"},{"t":"Preview","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-layers","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ Clip ‣ Preview Key ‣ Layers ‣ Preview Workspace Game data for the Inspector section, Animate for the Key menu Preview mutes or solos parts of the rig, and plays another animation under this one, so you can judge a clip in context. It is for wa"},{"t":"In the Game","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspingame","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ Clip ‣ In the game Workspace Game data The In the game section holds what links the clip to your game. A note beside the name says published or not published. Options Asset ID The Roblox ID of the published animation, which the game's scripts "},{"t":"Also Save a _MOVING Copy","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-moving","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ Clip ‣ In the game ‣ Also save a _MOVING copy Workspace Game data With this switch on, saving the clip also saves a second clip named with _MOVING added, which holds only the arms and the weapon. A game plays it over the run when a skill is ca"},{"t":"Body Mask and Masked Copies","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspmask","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ Clip ‣ In the game ‣ Body mask Game ‣ Body mask Workspace Game data The Body mask row sets which parts this clip plays on in the game. Saving writes keys only for the parts in the mask, and the parts outside it play whatever plays under the cl"},{"t":"Edit Mask","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-maskedit","x":"Menu Any body mask list ‣ Edit mask... Edit mask makes a mask of your own. It opens a dialog with the rig's parts as a tree. Each part has a tick and a share from 0% to 100%, where 100% plays all of the part and 50% plays half of it. A mask with every part at "},{"t":"The Posing Page","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#inspector-posing","x":""},{"t":"Posing","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-posing","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ Posing Workspace Animate The Posing page sets how the rest of the body behaves while you pose one part. Its settings are kept between sessions. Options Legs, when the torso moves Three tiles. Follow torso moves the legs with the torso. Stay up"},{"t":"Pages for Other Selections","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#inspector-other","x":"Some selections open a page of their own on the Inspector's first page. This part describes the pages of hit windows, clip blocks and physics rows."},{"t":"Hit Window Page","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-insphit","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ Hit window Workspace Game data When you select a hit window, the Inspector shows its page, titled with the window's kind, for example Hit window or Parry window. In the Game data workspace, where Clip is the only page, the hit window's page re"},{"t":"Clip Block Page","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspblock","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ Block Workspace Animate Selecting a clip block opens its page. A block is another clip that plays over your keys, and a generated walk is a block too. The first section is named Block, or Walk for a walk, and its first line gives the block's n"},{"t":"Physics Page","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspphysics","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ Physics When you select a row in the Physics window, the Inspector opens on a Physics page with the values of that row. What the page shows depends on the row. Options A body part Mode, which is Simulated or Plays its keys, and Strength over t"},{"t":"Pages for Other Selections","p":"Inspector","u":"inspector.html#f-inspother","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ First page Several other selections give the first page a layout of their own. These are described with the feature they belong to. Options A Face row Shows the face map and the sliders of the face. See Face Map . A scene item A prop, camera, "},{"t":"Curve Editor","p":"Windows","u":"curves.html","x":"The Curve editor draws each channel of a limb as a line over time. Change the line and the motion between the keys changes with it. It is for finding why a move looks stiff or wobbly, and for shaping the motion by hand w"},{"t":"The Graph","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-curvegraph","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Curve editor Workspace Animate The graph shows the selected limb as six channels: Rotation X, Y and Z in degrees, and Position X, Y and Z in studs. Time runs from left to right with the frame numbers along the top, and the value goes up t"},{"t":"Selecting and Dragging Keys","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-curveselect","x":"Workspace Animate You change a curve by selecting keys and dragging them. A drag changes the keys' values, and the rig follows as you drag. A handle is the short line that comes out of a selected key, and it sets the slope of the curve as it leaves or arrives "},{"t":"Frame and Value Boxes","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-curveboxes","x":"Workspace Animate When a key is selected, two boxes appear in the toolbar with its frame and its value. Type a frame to move the selected keys there. Type a value to set every selected key on that curve to it, in degrees for a rotation or studs for a position."},{"t":"Smooth","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-curvesmooth","x":"Menu Curve editor toolbar ‣ Smooth Workspace Animate Smooth turns the selected keys into smooth curves that never overshoot their keys. It gives each key the handle type Smooth (auto clamped), which is smooth through the neighbouring keys and flat at a peak or"},{"t":"Handles","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-curvehandles","x":"Menu Curve editor toolbar ‣ Handles Shortcut V Workspace Animate The Handles dropdown sets how the curve passes through the selected keys, choosing from eight handle types. When one key is selected, the dropdown reads Handles with the type of that key's curve."},{"t":"The View Menu","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-curveview","x":"Menu Curve editor toolbar ‣ View Workspace Animate The View menu gathers the commands that change how the curves are drawn and how a drag treats the keys round the one you move. Normalize and Falloff are also on the two tiles beside the menu, and a tick in the"},{"t":"Normalize","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-curvenormalize","x":"Menu Curve editor toolbar ‣ View ‣ Normalize Curve editor toolbar ‣ Normalize Workspace Animate Rotation curves are in degrees and position curves are in studs, so on one scale a small move looks flat beside a large turn. Normalize draws every curve between -1"},{"t":"Falloff","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-curvefalloff","x":"Menu Curve editor toolbar ‣ View ‣ Falloff Curve editor toolbar ‣ View ‣ Falloff shape Curve editor toolbar ‣ Falloff Shortcut O in the Curve editor Workspace Animate With Falloff on, dragging selected keys up or down moves the other keys of their curves as we"},{"t":"Buffer and Ghost Curves","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-curvebuffer","x":"Menu Curve editor toolbar ‣ View ‣ Snapshot Curve editor toolbar ‣ View ‣ Swap with the buffer Curve editor toolbar ‣ View ‣ Show the buffer Curve editor toolbar ‣ View ‣ Clear the buffer Curve editor toolbar ‣ View ‣ Ghost curves Curve editor toolbar ‣ View ‣"},{"t":"Several Limbs","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-curvemany","x":"Workspace Animate Limbs that have selected keys on the timeline show their curves beside the current limb's, lighter and thinner. Their keys are selected and dragged in the same way as the current limb's, and the toolbar names the current limb with the number "},{"t":"Clean up and Curve Modifiers","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-curvecleanup","x":"Menu Curve editor toolbar ‣ Clean up Curve editor toolbar ‣ Modifiers Workspace Animate Two buttons on the toolbar open the same commands as the Key menu, so you can use them without leaving the graph. When a clean-up dialog previews its result, the original c"},{"t":"Sound to Curve","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-soundcurve","x":"Menu Curve editor toolbar ‣ Modifiers ‣ Sound to curve... Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Curve modifiers ‣ Sound to curve... Needs An animation open on a rig Workspace Animate Sound to curve turns the loudness of a sound into keys on one curve, and the loud beats of the sou"},{"t":"Speed View","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-speedview","x":"Menu Curve editor toolbar ‣ Speed Workspace Animate The Speed view shows how fast the end of the limb moves on every frame, as one orange line. The end is the hand, the foot, the head or the weapon tip, or the far end of the part. The toolbar title gives the t"},{"t":"Curve Editor Shortcuts","p":"Curve Editor","u":"curves.html#f-curvekeys","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Keyboard shortcuts... Workspace Animate These keys work only while you are in the Curve editor, and the same key can do something else in the viewport. You can change them in the keyboard shortcuts window, in the Curves group. Changing o"},{"t":"Modifiers","p":"Windows","u":"modifiers.html","x":"The Modifiers window lists the results of the tools you have run on the open animation, so that you can change a result later, turn it off, reorder it, apply it or delete it."},{"t":"Modifiers Window","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-modwin","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Modifiers Right-click A limb that a result has changed Shortcut F9 opens the values of the last result Workspace All Most tools write ordinary keys, and those keys play and save as they always do. A tool whose result is kept in the Modifi"},{"t":"Add","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-modadd","x":"Workspace All The Add button in the window's top bar, and in its empty state, lists the tools whose results are kept. The tools are in groups named Generate, Physics, Clean up and Posing, then Timing, and then any group that a script has added. Choosing a tool"},{"t":"Tools That Make a Result","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-modtools","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Generate Pose ‣ Physics Pose ‣ Constraints Key ‣ Clean up Workspace Animate Each result is named after the tool that made it. The tools whose results are kept are these. The Generate group holds Locomotion cycle , Cycle from poses , Add breathing ,"},{"t":"Result Cards","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-modcard","x":"Right-click A card's heading row Workspace All Each result is a card. Its heading row holds the controls for the result, and its values are hidden until you open the card. A card that is selected has a lighter border. Opening a card shows the Presets row, the "},{"t":"Changing a Result","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-modchange","x":"Shortcut F9 Workspace All To change a result, open its card and change a value, choose Adjust from its menu to show its values in the Adjust panel, double-click its bar on the timeline, or press F9 with the result selected. Every change runs the tool again fro"},{"t":"Turn Off and On","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-modoff","x":"Right-click A result's bar on the timeline Workspace All The eye on a card turns the result off. The animation then shows as if the tool had not been run, while the result stays in the list with its values. Turning it on runs the tool again. Results that come "},{"t":"Move up and Move Down","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-modorder","x":"Right-click A card's heading row, or a result's bar Shortcut Alt + ↑ and Alt + ↓ Workspace All Results run in the order of the list, the earliest first, and a later result works on what an earlier one made. Move up and Move down swap the selected result with i"},{"t":"Rename and Duplicate","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-modname","x":"Right-click A card's heading row, or a result's bar Shortcut F2 to rename, Ctrl + D to duplicate Workspace All Rename gives the result a name of your own, which helps when the animation holds several results of one tool. You can also rename by clicking the nam"},{"t":"Apply","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-modapply","x":"Right-click A card's heading row, or a result's bar Workspace All Apply keeps the keys that the result made and forgets how they were made. The card is removed from the list, the keys stay in the animation as ordinary keys, and the tool's values can no longer "},{"t":"Delete","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-moddelete","x":"Right-click A result's bar on the timeline Shortcut Del Workspace All Delete turns the result off and removes it from the list, which takes out what the tool made and puts the animation back as it was before the tool ran. Keys you set by hand on top of it stay"},{"t":"Keys for the Selected Result","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-modkeys","x":"Shortcut F9 , F2 , Ctrl + D , Del , Alt + ↑ , Alt + ↓ Workspace All With a result selected, and no keys selected on the timeline, a few keys act on the result. F9 opens its values in the Adjust panel, or opens the values of the last tool you ran. F2 renames it"},{"t":"Modifiers Row","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#any-made-row","x":"Right-click A bar Workspace Animate When the animation holds at least one result, a Modifiers row appears under the rig on the timeline. Its name shows how many results there are, and clicking the name opens the Modifiers window. Each result is a bar spanning "},{"t":"Strength Lane","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-modstrength","x":"Right-click A point on the lane Workspace Animate The selected result gets a lane under the Modifiers row, named after it with the word strength. The lane draws the result's strength over its frames, with 100 percent at the top. The line takes in the Strength "},{"t":"Results from Scripts","p":"Modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#f-modscripts","x":"Menu Help ‣ Scripting... A script can use the same results. It can list them, run a tool, change a result's values, turn a result on or off, move it, apply it and delete it, and it can add a tool of its own. A tool added this way appears in the Add list under "},{"t":"Physics","p":"Windows","u":"physics.html","x":"The Physics window runs one physics simulation over an animation and writes the result as keys, for a hit, a fall, wind or floating. You build the simulation from bodies, forces and colliders. The Ragdoll window, describ"},{"t":"Physics Window","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-physicswin","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Physics window... View ‣ Windows ‣ Physics The Physics window sets up one simulation for the animation from bodies, forces and colliders, shows the result as a purple ghost of the rig, and writes it into the animation as keys. The animati"},{"t":"Toolbar","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#physics-toolbar","x":"The toolbar runs along the top of the window. Presets and Show are at the left, and Live, Simulate and Bake to keys are at the right."},{"t":"Presets","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-physics-presets","x":"Presets opens the list of starting points and your own presets. Choosing one replaces the values in the window. Frames inside a preset are kept relative to its start, so a preset fits wherever the simulation starts in the animation. The colliders you had chose"},{"t":"Show","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-physics-show","x":"Show chooses what the simulation draws in the viewport while the window is open. Each choice is a tick, and the choices are remembered. Options Bodies Blue boxes on the simulated parts. A box is more solid where the part's strength is higher at the playhead. I"},{"t":"Live","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-physics-live","x":"Live makes the simulation run again whenever a value changes. A moment after the last change the result on the ghost is replaced. Turning Live on runs the simulation once at once. It is off by default. Example For example, with Live on, drag the Speed of a pus"},{"t":"Simulate","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-physics-simulate","x":"Simulate runs the simulation and shows the result on the purple ghost, without touching the animation. While it runs, the button reads Stop, and a bar at the foot of the window shows how far it has got. The foot of the window then says which frames were simula"},{"t":"Bake to Keys","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-physics-bake","x":"Bake to keys writes the simulation into the animation as its Physics item. Keys are written on the simulated joints only, and a part set to Plays its keys keeps its own keys. The new keys are one for every frame from the start of the simulation, thinned to the"},{"t":"Body","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#physics-body","x":"The Body rows decide how each part of the rig behaves in the simulation."},{"t":"Body","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-physics-body","x":"The Body lists four regions, Head, Spine, Arms and Legs. Click the arrow of a region to open its parts. Every part of the rig is a body, and so is what the rig holds, which is welded to its hand. A part uses its own values if it has any, then its region's valu"},{"t":"Hold Its Place","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-physics-hold","x":"Hold its place is how hard the whole body is pulled to the place its keys put it in the world, as a curve over the animation. At 0 the body falls freely, and at 100 % it stays where it is keyed. It acts on the torso and carries the rest of the body with it. Th"},{"t":"Forces","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#physics-forces","x":"Forces push the simulated body. Each force has a row in the tree and a lane that you drag in time."},{"t":"Forces","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-forces","x":"The plus button on the Forces row adds a force. The choices are Push, Wind, Pull, Blast, Lift, Black hole and Drag. A new force starts at the playhead, or at the start of the simulation if the playhead is earlier. Its row names the force, and its lane shows a "},{"t":"Push","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-force-push","x":"A Push is a hit. It gives one body part a sudden shove at a point on it, away from where the hit comes from, at one frame or spread over several frames. Options At frame, Over frames The frame where the push lands. Over frames spreads it across that many frame"},{"t":"Wind","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-force-wind","x":"Wind blows steadily on the whole body over a span of frames, with gusts that come and go. With a radius it blows only near its centre. Options From, To The frames the wind blows. A new wind runs from the playhead to the end of the animation. Around, Up The dir"},{"t":"Pull","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-force-pull","x":"A Pull draws the body toward a point over a span of frames. The point can be centred on an object or can ride a path. Options From, To The frames the pull acts. A new pull lasts 30 frames from the playhead. Strength How strongly it pulls, from −2000 to 2000 st"},{"t":"Blast","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-force-blast","x":"A Blast throws the body outward from a point, at one frame or spread over several. A part closer to the centre is thrown harder. Options At frame, Over frames As for a push. Speed How fast it throws things, from 0 to 1000 studs a second. The default is 40. Lau"},{"t":"Lift","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-force-lift","x":"A Lift pushes the body upward over a span of frames, inside its radius. Set Around and Up to push it in any other direction. Options From, To The frames the lift acts. A new lift lasts 30 frames. Around, Up The direction of the lift. Until you set them, the li"},{"t":"Black Hole","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-force-blackhole","x":"A Black hole is a pull that also swirls what it draws in round its centre, over a span of frames. Options From, To The frames it acts. A new black hole lasts 45 frames. Strength How strongly it pulls, from −2000 to 2000 studs a second squared. The default is 6"},{"t":"Drag","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-force-drag","x":"Drag slows everything down, like thick air or water, over a span of frames. It has no centre and no radius. Options From, To The frames the drag acts. A new drag runs from the playhead to the end of the animation. Thickness How much it slows things, from 0 to "},{"t":"Colliders and Settings","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#physics-colliders","x":"These two rows set what the body hits and how long the simulation runs."},{"t":"Colliders","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-physics-colliders","x":"Colliders sets what the simulated body can hit. The simulation can hit the whole place, or only the objects you choose, and the floor under the rig can be added to those. Options Hits The whole place, or Selected objects only. And the floor under the rig With "},{"t":"Settings","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-physics-settings","x":"Settings sets when the simulation starts, how long it lasts, how gravity acts, and how the body returns to the animation afterwards. The row shows the start frame beside it. Options Start at The frame where the simulation starts. Before it, the animation plays"},{"t":"Ragdoll","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#physics-ragdoll","x":"The Ragdoll window is a quicker tool for one kind of simulation, a body that is pushed and falls."},{"t":"Ragdoll","p":"Physics","u":"physics.html#f-ragdoll","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Ragdoll... Pose ‣ This limb ‣ Ragdoll... Right-click A limb ‣ Ragdoll... Ragdoll... pushes the body at a frame and lets Roblox physics take it, for falls, deaths, knockdowns and hit reactions. The result is baked to keys. Right-click the "},{"t":"Reference Video","p":"Windows","u":"reference.html","x":"The Reference video window plays a recording beside your animation, locked to the timeline, so you can pose against it. Videos come from the Framez Helper , which reads the videos in your clip folders. A set of image fra"},{"t":"Select a Clip","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-refpick","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Reference video Workspace Animate Needs The Framez Helper, for videos The clip button at the top left of the window lists the videos in your clip folders and opens the one you choose. Each video is listed with its size, and the one in use"},{"t":"Clip Folders","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-folders","x":"Menu Clip button ‣ Clip folders Workspace Animate Needs The Framez Helper Clip folders are the folders the clip list comes from. Each person sets their own, because the helper keeps the list on your computer. Only videos directly in a folder are listed, not th"},{"t":"Image Frames from Files","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-imgframes","x":"Menu Clip button ‣ Image frames from files... Workspace Animate This uses a numbered set of PNG or JPG frames as the reference instead of a video. It needs no helper, so it works on a computer that does not have one. Studio's own file dialog opens, you select "},{"t":"Link","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-link","x":"Workspace Animate The link button in the footer ties the video frame on screen to the playhead. From then on the video follows the timeline, whether you scrub, step or play, and the keys of the animation show under the ruler. Pressing the button again unlinks "},{"t":"Clip Time","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-cliptime","x":"Workspace Animate The readout in the middle of the footer shows the clip frame and, when linked, the animation frame, for example Clip 6954 and Frame 24. When the clip is not linked it says so. Clicking it opens the Clip time box. Options Go to Moves the video"},{"t":"Zoom and Pan","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-refzoom","x":"Workspace Animate The wheel zooms the picture about the cursor, up to 800%, and dragging pans it. Double-clicking the picture fits it again, as the fit button at the top right does. The zoom list next to it offers Fit, 150%, 200%, 300%, 400% and 600%. When the"},{"t":"Clip Ruler","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-refruler","x":"Workspace Animate The ruler under the picture shows the clip's time, labelled in seconds with small dots between the labels. When the clip is linked, the stretch that your animation covers is drawn darker, with a diamond under the ruler for each key of the ani"},{"t":"Image Plane","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-over3d","x":"Menu View ‣ Reference ‣ Image plane View ‣ Reference ‣ Image plane settings... Right-click The image plane button in the footer Workspace Animate The image plane lays the video frame, see-through, over the viewport so you can pose the rig on top of the picture"},{"t":"Marks","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-marks","x":"Menu View ‣ Reference ‣ Mark the weapon tip View ‣ Reference ‣ Mark options... Right-click The marks button in the footer Workspace Animate Marks record where the weapon tip, a hand or a foot is on each frame of the reference. With marking on, clicking the pic"},{"t":"Joints","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-joints","x":"Workspace Animate Needs The Framez Helper The Joints button, drawn as a small figure, draws the tracked skeleton of the person in the video over the picture. The left side is blue and the right side is orange. You can pose against the lines instead of guessing"},{"t":"Pose from Video","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-refposevideo","x":"Menu Pose ‣ Generate ‣ Pose from video Workspace Animate Needs The Framez Helper Pose from video keys the torso, head, arms and legs of the rig from the person in the linked video, every few frames. It works from any camera angle, because the pose is measured "},{"t":"Beats Strip","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-beats","x":"Workspace Animate Needs The Framez Helper The beats strip is a thin band over the ruler that shows how much the picture changes from one frame to the next. Grey bars show the movement, and the taller the bar, the more the picture changes. Red marks are the sti"},{"t":"Save as FRAME","p":"Reference Video","u":"reference.html#f-saveframe","x":"Workspace Animate The button at the right of the footer reads Save as FRAME followed by the clip frame on screen, for example Save as FRAME 6954. It opens the save dialog with that name filled in, so a save is named after the frame of the reference it was made"},{"t":"Poses","p":"Windows","u":"poses.html","x":"The Poses window keeps named poses, each shown as a small picture of the rig, and keys them at the playhead with a click. A character that returns to the same few poses, such as a stance, a guard or the wind-up of a swin"},{"t":"Saving Poses","p":"Poses","u":"poses.html#poses-saving","x":""},{"t":"Save Pose","p":"Poses","u":"poses.html#f-posesave","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Poses Workspace Animate The Save pose button at the top left of the window saves the rig as it stands at the playhead. You choose how much of the rig to keep, and where to keep it. The new pose appears at once with a picture. Its name is "},{"t":"Using Poses","p":"Poses","u":"poses.html#poses-using","x":""},{"t":"Key a Pose","p":"Poses","u":"poses.html#f-poses","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Poses Workspace Animate Clicking a picture keys that pose at the playhead on every limb it holds, as one undo step. The Blend field at the top right of the window sets how far the rig moves toward the pose. At 100% the limbs take the pose"},{"t":"Blend by Dragging","p":"Poses","u":"poses.html#f-poseblend","x":"Workspace Animate Pressing a picture and dragging sideways blends the pose in while you watch. The press is 0%, which is the rig as it stands. The width of one picture is 100%, so dragging right by one picture applies the whole pose. Dragging further right goe"},{"t":"Pose Menu","p":"Poses","u":"poses.html#f-posemenu","x":"Right-click A pose in the Poses window Workspace Animate Right-clicking a pose opens its menu. The first rows key the pose in different ways, and the rest manage it. Options Apply Keys the pose at the playhead at the Blend value, like a click. Apply mirrored K"},{"t":"Where Poses Are Kept","p":"Poses","u":"poses.html#poses-libraries","x":""},{"t":"My Poses and This Place","p":"Poses","u":"poses.html#f-poselibs","x":"Workspace Animate The window has two sections, each with its own heading and a note beside it. My poses are the same in every place on this computer. This place holds poses that are saved in the place itself, so they travel with it and are shared with everyone"},{"t":"Camera Preview","p":"Windows","u":"camera.html","x":"The Camera preview shows what a scene camera sees while the viewport stays on another angle, so you can pose a character from any side and watch the shot at the same time. It opens as a floating window that you can dock "},{"t":"Camera Preview","p":"Camera Preview","u":"camera.html#f-shotview","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Camera preview Right-click A camera's row ‣ Preview it Workspace Scene The window shows the camera the game sees at the playhead, following the cuts, or one camera that you choose at its top. It has three parts. A header along the top hol"},{"t":"Header","p":"Camera Preview","u":"camera.html#camera-header","x":"The header holds the shot selector on the left. On the right it holds the name of the camera and the frame, then the eye, the Key button and the more button."},{"t":"Shot Selector","p":"Camera Preview","u":"camera.html#f-shotselect","x":"Menu Camera preview ‣ the button at the left of the header Right-click A camera's row ‣ Preview it Workspace Scene The shot selector chooses which camera the window shows. Its button reads The shot while the window follows the cuts, and the camera's name while"},{"t":"Key","p":"Camera Preview","u":"camera.html#f-shotkey","x":"Menu Camera preview ‣ the Key button in the header Workspace Scene The Key button in the header keys the camera that is showing, with its place and field of view, at the playhead. It does the same as Key Camera , which also has the shortcut K . Use it after yo"},{"t":"Picture and Modes","p":"Camera Preview","u":"camera.html#camera-picture","x":""},{"t":"Picture","p":"Camera Preview","u":"camera.html#f-shotpicture","x":"Workspace Scene The picture sits in a dark well with a margin on every side, at the shape of the shot, with bars where the window is a different shape. The shape follows the Aspect ratio in Framing, so a vertical short shows as a tall picture. The guides you h"},{"t":"Modes","p":"Camera Preview","u":"camera.html#f-shotmodes","x":"Workspace Scene The rail beside the picture holds five icon buttons, one for each way of flying the camera. Click one to make it the mode, and it shows filled. Orbit is the mode when the window first opens. Pointing at a button shows its name and what its drag"},{"t":"More Menu","p":"Camera Preview","u":"camera.html#camera-more","x":"The three dots at the right of the header open the more menu, which holds the guides, the preview's brightness, Clean view and Refresh."},{"t":"Guides","p":"Camera Preview","u":"camera.html#f-shotguides","x":"Menu Camera preview ‣ more menu ‣ Guides Workspace Scene Guides opens the same list as the Framing menu of the Scene menu: Aspect ratio, Show the frame, Thirds, Safe areas, More guides, Guide sets and Guide settings. The guides draw over the picture, and Aspec"},{"t":"Brightness","p":"Camera Preview","u":"camera.html#f-shotbright","x":"Menu Camera preview ‣ more menu ‣ Brightness Workspace Scene Brightness changes how brightly the preview is lit. It affects only the preview. What the game shows is lit by the place itself. The level is remembered by Studio. Options 70%, 85%, 100%, 115%, 130%,"},{"t":"Clean View","p":"Camera Preview","u":"camera.html#f-cleanview","x":"Menu Camera preview ‣ eye button in the header Camera preview ‣ more menu ‣ Clean view Workspace Scene Clean view hides the header, the rail and the guides, so the picture fills the whole window. Click the eye in the header, or choose Clean view in the more me"},{"t":"Refresh","p":"Camera Preview","u":"camera.html#f-shotrefresh","x":"Menu Camera preview ‣ more menu ‣ Refresh Workspace Scene Refresh makes the preview's copy of the place, and of the terrain, again straight away. The preview already updates itself when parts move, resize or change colour, and it copies the place again when th"},{"t":"Subtitle Lines","p":"Windows","u":"sublines.html","x":"Subtitle lines are the timed dialogue of a scene. The Subtitle lines window lists them and adds many at once from a pasted script or an .srt file, and the subtitle page of the Inspector writes each line and sets how it l"},{"t":"Subtitle Lines Window","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-sublines","x":"Menu View ‣ Windows ‣ Subtitle lines Right-click A subtitle block ‣ Subtitle lines The Subtitles row ‣ Subtitle lines Workspace Scene The Subtitle lines window lists every subtitle line of the scene and adds many lines at once. It opens as a floating window, a"},{"t":"The Subtitle Page","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#sublines-page","x":""},{"t":"The Subtitle Page","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-subpage","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line Workspace Scene The subtitle page writes a line and sets how it looks, moves and reads word by word. It shows in the Inspector when a subtitle block is selected and nothing else takes the Inspector first, because a sel"},{"t":"Text","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#sublines-text","x":""},{"t":"Text","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-subtext","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line ‣ Text Workspace Scene The Text part holds what the line says and when it shows. The first section, Line, has the text and its speaker, and the second, Timing, has the times. The speaker and the starting look are expla"},{"t":"Starting Looks and My Looks","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-sublooks","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line ‣ Text ‣ Look Right-click A subtitle block ‣ Look Workspace Scene A starting look fills in every value of the line's Look and Motion at once, so one choice gives a finished style. Each value stays yours to change after"},{"t":"Look","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#sublines-look","x":"The Look part is split into six sections. Wherever a value is a colour, it is a swatch and a hex code. Click the swatch for a palette of sixteen colours, or type a hex code such as FFD640, which may also be written with three digits. Where a colour may be empt"},{"t":"Text Style","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-subtype","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line ‣ Look ‣ Text Workspace Scene Text Style sets the typeface and the basic look of the letters. A line starts with Builder Sans in SemiBold, 4.6 percent of the shot's height, in white. Options Font The typeface. The font"},{"t":"Outline and Shadow","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-suboutline","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line ‣ Look ‣ Outline and shadow Workspace Scene This section keeps the text readable against any background. The outline is a solid edge around each letter, the shadow is a soft shape behind the text, and the glow is a col"},{"t":"Box","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-subbox","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line ‣ Look ‣ Box Workspace Scene The box is a coloured panel behind the whole line, including the speaker's name and the portrait. A new line has no box, because its opacity is 0. Options Colour The colour of the panel, bl"},{"t":"Place","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-subplace","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line ‣ Look ‣ Place Workspace Scene Place decides where the line sits in the shot and how wide it may grow. Lines wrap inside their width. Options Position Nine tiles, top, middle and bottom by left, centre and right, with "},{"t":"Speaker's Name","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-subspeaker","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line ‣ Look ‣ Speaker's name Workspace Scene This section shows only for a line that has a speaker. The name is drawn in capitals above the line, in its own colour and size. Options Colour The colour of the name, a pale gol"},{"t":"Portrait","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-subportrait","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line ‣ Look ‣ Portrait Workspace Scene A portrait is a small image shown with the line, such as a character's face. Type an image's asset id to add one. Once an image is set, three more rows appear. Options Image The asset "},{"t":"Motion","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#sublines-motion","x":""},{"t":"Motion","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-submotion","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line ‣ Motion Workspace Scene The Motion part gives a line movement. It has three phases, chosen by a switch at the top. In moves play as the line appears. During motions keep running for as long as the line shows. Out move"},{"t":"The Move Box","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-submove","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line ‣ Motion ‣ Add a move Workspace Scene The move box edits one move. Its title reads Add a move or Change the move for the In and Out phases, and Add a motion or Change the motion for During. Its rows follow what the mov"},{"t":"Transform","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-subtransform","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line ‣ Motion ‣ Transform Workspace Scene Transform animates the whole line over its time with keys, on top of its moves. Each value is keyed at the playhead, which must be inside the line. Typing a value keys it there. The"},{"t":"Words","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#sublines-words","x":""},{"t":"Words","p":"Subtitle Lines","u":"sublines.html#f-subwords","x":"Menu Inspector ‣ a selected subtitle line ‣ Words Workspace Scene The Words part gives single words a look of their own. It shows a chip for each word of the line. Click a chip to select that word, with a red ring round it, and its values appear below. A small"},{"t":"Test Bench","p":"Windows","u":"bench.html","x":"The Test Bench tries the open clip the way your game will play it: on your character in a running game, joined to the clips around it, on other body shapes, under a game camera, and against other versions of itself. It i"},{"t":"Starting a Test","p":"Test Bench","u":"bench.html#bench-start","x":"The top of the window holds the test scene and the five tabs. Everything below them belongs to the selected tab."},{"t":"Test Bench Window","p":"Test Bench","u":"bench.html#f-benchwin","x":"Menu Game ‣ Test bench... Workspace Game data The Test Bench opens as a floating window. It has a test scene picker with a more button at the top, then the tabs Game, Transitions, Bodies, Camera and Compare. Each tab tests one part of how the clip plays and ha"},{"t":"Test Scenes","p":"Test Bench","u":"bench.html#f-benchscene","x":"Menu Game ‣ Test bench..., the scene picker at the top of the window Workspace Game data A test scene keeps every value of every tab, including which tabs are in the test, so you can return to a setup in one click. The picker at the top of the window lists the"},{"t":"Tabs","p":"Test Bench","u":"bench.html#bench-tabs","x":"Each tab tests one part of how the clip plays. A tab does nothing until its In the test switch is on."},{"t":"Game Tab","p":"Test Bench","u":"bench.html#f-benchgame","x":"Menu Game ‣ Test bench..., Game tab Workspace Game data Needs Studio in Play, for Try in the game The Game tab puts the open clip on your own character when Studio is in Play, set up the way your game would play it. The clip plays in a slot, with a priority, f"},{"t":"Transitions Tab","p":"Test Bench","u":"bench.html#f-benchtrans","x":"Menu Game ‣ Test bench..., Transitions tab Workspace Game data The Transitions tab tests how the clip joins the clips around it. Add the clip that plays before it and the clip that plays after it, and the bodies play the whole sequence with the fades between t"},{"t":"Bodies Tab","p":"Test Bench","u":"bench.html#f-benchbodies","x":"Menu Game ‣ Test bench..., Bodies tab Workspace Game data The Bodies tab plays the clip on other body shapes side by side with the rig, in the viewport. Each body stands with its feet on the rig's floor and plays the clip through its own Animator at Framez's f"},{"t":"Camera Tab","p":"Test Bench","u":"bench.html#f-benchcamera","x":"Menu Game ‣ Test bench..., Camera tab Workspace Game data The Camera tab shows the clip through a game camera, so you can judge it the way a player will see it. The camera follows a body while Framez plays, and it only moves Studio's camera while the clip play"},{"t":"Compare Tab","p":"Test Bench","u":"bench.html#f-benchcompare","x":"Menu Game ‣ Test bench..., Compare tab Workspace Game data The Compare tab shows where the clip differs from another version of itself. It plays the other version on a copy of the rig and tints the parts of the copy that are further from your keys than a limit"},{"t":"Check Animation","p":"Windows","u":"check.html","x":"The Check Animation window lists what will look wrong in your game, each problem at its frame, and gives every problem a button that fixes it. It reads the animation as it will be saved and changes nothing until you pres"},{"t":"Check Animation","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-check","x":"Menu Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Check animation View ‣ Windows ‣ Check animation Workspace Animate Check animation reads every frame of the animation and lists what will look wrong in your game. Each problem is one row with a short description and its frame, grouped und"},{"t":"Problems","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#check-problems","x":"Each kind of problem has its own heading in the list. The rows of a kind describe what was found, and the fix named under each kind is the button its rows carry."},{"t":"Sliding Feet","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-check-slide","x":"A foot on the floor that moves between frames. The check lists a foot whose sole is within 0.25 studs of the floor on two frames in a row and moves more than 0.03 studs sideways between them. Each run of frames is one row. It names the foot, the greatest dista"},{"t":"Through the Torso","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-check-torso","x":"An arm or a leg that goes into the torso. The check lists a limb whose box is inside the torso's box by more than 0.1 studs. For an upper arm or an upper leg, which hangs from the torso, only the part from 40 % of its length outward is tested, because a should"},{"t":"Pops","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-check-pop","x":"A limb end whose speed jumps in a single frame. The check measures the speed of the hands, the feet, the head, the weapon and the root, and lists a frame where the speed changes by more than a third of the limb's top speed. A limb that never moves faster than "},{"t":"Dead Stops","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-check-stop","x":"A limb end that stands completely still between two moves. The check lists a limb whose speed stays under 3 % of its top speed for three frames or more, with motion before and after. Limbs that stop on the same frames are one row, such as \"RightHand stops dead"},{"t":"Overshoot","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-check-overshoot","x":"A move whose curve goes past its own keys. The check lists a span between two keys, at least two frames long, whose curve was shaped in the Curve editor and passes both of its keys by more than 1° or 0.02 studs. Back and Elastic easing overshoot on purpose and"},{"t":"Rotation Flips","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-check-flip","x":"A limb that flips half a turn in one frame. The check lists a limb that turns by more than 100° between two frames when a half turn about one of its own axes brings it back to within 30°. A row looks like \"RightHand turns 172 degrees in one frame\". The fix is "},{"t":"Loop Seam","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-check-seam","x":"A looping animation whose last frame does not match its first. The check runs only while Looping is on. It lists a limb whose pose on the last frame differs from its pose on frame 0 by more than 1° or 0.02 studs, with a row at the last frame such as \"RightArm "},{"t":"Frame 0","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-check-frame0","x":"Limbs that have keys but none on frame 0. A single row names all of them, such as \"LeftArm, RightArm have no key on frame 0\". When no limb has a key on frame 0, the row says that frame 0 has no keys and on which frame the animation starts. The fix is Key Frame"},{"t":"Priority","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-check-priority","x":"An animation saved with the priority Core, the lowest, which the game's own walk and idle animations play over. The row reads \"Priority is Core, the lowest: raise it\". Clicking the row opens the animation settings, where the priority is set. The fix is Set Act"},{"t":"Faces","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-check-face","x":"Face keys that cannot be shown. One row says that the animation has face keys but the head has no Decal of the name they need. Another says that a face image will not load and asks you to select another. Only the second kind has a fix, Select... ."},{"t":"Fixes","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#check-fixes","x":"A row that can be fixed has a button on its right, named for what it does. Pressing it fixes that row as one undo step, and then the list checks again. The arrow beside a button opens that fix's own values, and the button on its own uses the values from the la"},{"t":"Fix All","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-fix-all","x":"Workspace Animate Fix all, in the bar at the top of the window, runs the fix of every row that needs no choice, one after another, as a single undo step. Afterwards the list checks again, and the message says how many problems were fixed, how many could not be"},{"t":"Plant","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-fix-plant","x":"Plant fixes a Sliding feet row. It holds the foot where it was on the first frame of the stretch for every frame of the stretch, and the leg follows to keep it there. It is the Foot Lock tool applied to the frames of the row, and it has no values of its own be"},{"t":"Push Out","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-fix-pushout","x":"Push out fixes a Through the torso row. On each frame of the row it turns the limb at its shoulder or hip, the joint under the torso that carries it, only as far as the limb needs to leave the torso. The turn eases in and out over a few frames on each side, an"},{"t":"Average","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-fix-average","x":"Average fixes a Pops row. It replaces the frames of the pop, and one frame either side of them, with keys averaged from the frames around them, on the limbs the row names and by default on the limbs they hang from as well. The message names the frames and the "},{"t":"Ease","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-fix-ease","x":"Ease fixes a Dead stops row. It slows the move into the stop and speeds the move out of it, so the limb comes to rest gradually and leaves gradually instead of stopping dead. It changes the easing of the key that ends the move into the stop and of the key that"},{"t":"Clamp","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-fix-clamp","x":"Clamp fixes an Overshoot row. It sets the two keys of the span to Smooth, a curve that eases in and out and never goes past its keys. It has no options."},{"t":"Close","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-fix-close","x":"Close fixes a Loop seam row. It keys the last frame with the pose of frame 0 and spreads the difference over the frames before it, so the loop closes without a visible jump. It works on the limb of the row. The arrow opens the options. Options Spread over The "},{"t":"Unflip","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-fix-unflip","x":"Unflip fixes a Rotation flips row. It runs the Euler Filter on the limb of the row, which turns the flipped frames back and keeps the limbs it carries in place. It has no options of its own."},{"t":"Key Frame 0","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-fix-keyzero","x":"Key frame 0 fixes a Frame 0 row. It keys frame 0 on every limb the row names, with the pose each limb already shows there, so the animation starts exactly as it looks now. It has no options."},{"t":"Set Action","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-fix-setaction","x":"Set Action fixes a Priority row. It sets the animation's priority to Action, which plays over the game's own walk and idle animations. The arrow opens the animation settings, where you can choose a different priority. Set Action is included in Fix all."},{"t":"Select...","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-fix-select","x":"Select... is the button on a Faces row whose face image will not load. It opens the list of faces for that face key, so you can select another image. Clicking the row opens the same list. This fix needs a choice from you, so Fix all leaves it alone."},{"t":"Check Again","p":"Check Animation","u":"check.html#f-checkagain","x":"Workspace Animate Check again, in the bar at the top of the window, reads the animation again and builds the list from scratch. Use it after you change the animation in a way the window did not notice. The list also checks again after every fix, and by itself "},{"t":"Preferences","p":"Windows","u":"prefs.html","x":"The Preferences window holds every setting that you set once and keep. They cover how the handles look, how the timeline and viewport behave, what new animations start with, which overlays show, and how Framez saves your"},{"t":"The Preferences Window","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-settings","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... The Framez Preferences window opens as a floating window. A search box runs across the top, and down the left is a list of categories that begins with All. Choosing All lists every setting in category order, and choosing a"},{"t":"Handles and Posing","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#prefs-handles","x":"These settings change the rings and arrows you drag to pose a limb, and whether letting go of a handle keys the pose."},{"t":"Handle Style","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-handlestyle","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Handles and posing Handle style sets how the rotate rings and the move arrows look and how they are sized. The default is Minimal. Options Minimal The transform gizmo as Blender draws it, the same size on the screen at a"},{"t":"Handle Size","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-handlesize","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Handles and posing The Size setting changes how big the rings and arrows are. With the Minimal handle style the handles keep that size on the screen at any distance. The default is Normal. Options Small, Normal, Large Sm"},{"t":"Line Width","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-linewidth","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Handles and posing Line width sets how thick the control rig's shapes and the motion paths are drawn, in pixels, from 1 to 6. The lines keep that width at any distance. The default is 3."},{"t":"Values","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-values","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Handles and posing Values sets where the angle or distance of a drag shows. The status bar shows it with either option. The default is Status bar. Options Status bar The value shows in the status bar under the timeline o"},{"t":"Rotate with R, Move with G","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-rgkeys","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Handles and posing Shortcut R , G With a limb selected, R rotates it and G moves it with the pointer at once, without grabbing a handle. The handles hide while the transform runs. The default is On; with Off, R switches "},{"t":"Drag a Part to Rotate It","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-dragpart","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Handles and posing When this is on, dragging an arm, a leg or the head in the viewport rotates it about its joint so the point you pressed follows the pointer. On a leg or arm in IK the hand or foot follows the pointer i"},{"t":"Precision and Snapping","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-precision","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Handles and posing Precision and snapping sets how holding a modifier key changes a handle drag. Three boxes sit side by side. They can be typed into or dragged, and one reset button returns all three to their defaults. "},{"t":"Auto Key","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-autokey","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Handles and posing Auto key decides whether a pose made with the handles is keyed when you let go. The default is On. Options On Letting go of a handle keys the limb at the playhead. Off The pose is applied to the rig bu"},{"t":"Timeline","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#prefs-timeline","x":"These settings change how the mouse wheel and the stepping commands move along the timeline."},{"t":"Wheel Zoom","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-wheelzoom","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Timeline Wheel zoom sets how much one notch of the mouse wheel zooms the timeline, as a factor from 1.05 to 3. The default is 1.25, so each notch zooms the timeline by that factor."},{"t":"Frame Step","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-framestep","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Timeline Frame step sets how many frames the Previous frame and Next frame commands move, from 1 to 100. The default is 1. Their keys are Alt + Left and Alt + Right . Example For example, set Frame step to 5 to scrub thr"},{"t":"Key Jump","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-keyjump","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Timeline Key jump sets how many keys the Previous key and Next key commands move, from 1 to 20. The default is 1. Their keys are Left and Right ."},{"t":"New Clips and Keys","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#prefs-newclips","x":"These settings decide how a new key behaves and what a new animation starts with."},{"t":"How a New Key Plays into the Next","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-newkeys","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ New clips and keys This setting chooses the easing that a newly made key gets, which decides how the motion travels from that key to the next one. It does not change keys that already exist. The default is Smooth. Option"},{"t":"New Animation Frame Rate","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-newfps","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ New clips and keys The Frame rate under New animations sets the frames per second of every animation started with New Animation , from 1 to 240. The default is 60. The frame rate of an animation that already exists is ch"},{"t":"New Animation Length","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-newlength","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ New clips and keys The Length under New animations sets how long the timeline of a new animation is, from 0.1 to 600 seconds. The default is 1 second. Example For example, if most of your animations are 2 second attacks "},{"t":"Navigation","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#prefs-navigation","x":"Navigation changes how the middle mouse button, the wheel and the number pad move the view in the viewport."},{"t":"Viewport","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-viewport","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Navigation The Viewport setting chooses how the middle mouse button and the wheel move the view. The default is Studio. The same switch is the Blender navigation row of the View menu. Options Studio Studio's own navigati"},{"t":"Orbit Speed","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-orbit","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Navigation Orbit speed sets how far a drag with the middle mouse button turns the view, as a percentage from 10 to 400. The default is 100. It applies when Viewport is set to Blender."},{"t":"Pan Speed","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-pan","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Navigation Pan speed sets how far Shift with a middle drag, Ctrl with the wheel and Shift with the wheel slide the view, as a percentage from 10 to 400. The default is 100. It applies when Viewport is set to Blender."},{"t":"Zoom Toward the Cursor","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-zoomcursor","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Navigation Zoom toward the cursor decides which point the wheel closes in on. With Off, the default, the wheel zooms toward the pivot. With On, it closes in on the point under the mouse pointer instead. It applies when V"},{"t":"Overlays","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#prefs-overlays","x":"These settings change how motion paths, the onion skin and bones are drawn. The overlays themselves are switched on in the View menu."},{"t":"Motion Path Length","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-pathlength","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Overlays The Length under Motion paths chooses how much of each path is drawn. The default is Whole clip. Options Whole clip Each path covers the whole animation. Around current frame Each path covers only the frames eit"},{"t":"Motion Path Frames Each Side","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-pathwindow","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Overlays Frames each side sets how far a motion path reaches before and after the playhead when its length is Around current frame, from 1 to 600 frames. The default is 30."},{"t":"Motion Path Frame Numbers","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-pathnumbers","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Overlays Frame numbers draws frame numbers beside the selected limb's motion path. The default is Off. Options Off No numbers are drawn. Every key A number is drawn at each key of the path. Every 5 frames A number is dra"},{"t":"Onion Skin Before Colour","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-onionbefore","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Overlays Before colour is the tint of the onion skin ghosts that show the frames before the playhead. It is a colour of six hexadecimal digits, such as 282A30, shown next to a swatch. Anything that is not six hexadecimal"},{"t":"Onion Skin After Colour","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-onionafter","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Overlays After colour is the tint of the ghosts that show the frames after the playhead, entered the same way as Before colour. The default is F0F0F5."},{"t":"Onion Skin Ghost Strength","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-onionstrength","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Overlays Ghost strength sets how solid the onion skin ghosts are, as a percentage from 10 to 200. The default is 100. A lower value makes the ghosts fainter, and a higher one makes them stand out against a busy scene. Se"},{"t":"Bone Style","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-bonestyle","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Overlays Bone style draws each joint as a bone over the rig. The default is Off. The same choice is in the Bones overlay of the View menu. Options Off No bones are drawn. Octahedral A diamond-shaped bone from each joint "},{"t":"Bone X-Ray","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-bonexray","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Overlays X-ray decides whether bones are drawn through the body or hidden behind it. With On, the default, they show through the body. With Off, the parts of a bone that lie behind the body are hidden."},{"t":"Curves and Drawing","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#prefs-curves","x":"These settings change how automatic handles are placed in the Curve editor and how strokes drawn with the Annotate pen are smoothed."},{"t":"Auto Handles","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-autohandles","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Curves and drawing Auto handles sets how an automatic handle slopes through the keys on either side of it. It applies to every automatic and clamped key in the Curve editor . The default is Standard. Options Standard The"},{"t":"Pen Smoothing","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-pen","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Curves and drawing Pen smoothing sets how many times a stroke drawn with the Annotate pen is smoothed when you let go of it, from 0 to 8. The default is 1. With 0 the stroke stays exactly as drawn, and each extra pass ro"},{"t":"Capture","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#prefs-capture","x":"The Capture category holds the setting for recording and keying out the rigs."},{"t":"Green Screen Colour","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-greenscreen","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Capture The Colour under Green screen sets the flat colour behind the rigs when the green screen is on. The default is Green. Options Green, Blue A standard keying green or blue. Your own A colour you set in the Green sc"},{"t":"Saving","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#prefs-saving","x":"These settings control the safety copies Framez keeps of your work."},{"t":"Auto-Recovery","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-recovery","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Saving Auto-recovery sets how often Framez keeps a copy of unsaved work on this computer while the animation changes. A copy is made a few seconds after your last edit, and no more often than this setting allows. The def"},{"t":"Recovery Copies Kept","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-kept","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Saving The Kept setting sets how many unsaved animations are kept as recovery copies. When there are more, the oldest copy goes. The choices are 3, 5 and 10, and the default is 5."},{"t":"Versions Kept","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-versions","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Saving Versions kept sets how many saved versions of each animation are kept in the place, in the FramezHistory folder of ServerStorage. The choices are 5, 10, 25 and 50, and the default is 10. When there are more versio"},{"t":"Backup Folder","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-backup","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Saving Needs The Framez Helper The Backup folder is the folder where the Framez Helper copies every Save. Each save is written there as a .json file with the keys and an .rbxm file that can be dragged back into Studio. L"},{"t":"Workspaces","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#prefs-workspaces","x":"The Workspaces category decides how much of Framez each workspace shows."},{"t":"Menus and Tools","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-workspaces","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Workspaces Menus and tools sets what each workspace shows. The default is Its own. Options Its own Each workspace shows its own menus and tools. All Every workspace shows every menu, tool, track and Inspector section. Ex"},{"t":"Keyboard","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#prefs-keyboard","x":"The Keyboard category opens the shortcuts window."},{"t":"Keyboard Shortcuts Button","p":"Preferences","u":"prefs.html#f-pref-keyboard","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Preferences... ‣ Keyboard The Keyboard shortcuts... button opens the Keyboard Shortcuts window. Choosing the Keyboard category in the list opens the same window."},{"t":"Keyboard Shortcuts","p":"Windows","u":"keyboard.html","x":"The Keyboard Shortcuts window lists every command with its key and lets you give any command a key of your own. You can search by name, find out what a key does by pressing it, start from a set of keys that suits you, an"},{"t":"The Keyboard Shortcuts Window","p":"Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"keyboard.html#f-shortcutswin","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Keyboard shortcuts... Right-click A menu row or a Menu search result, then Keyboard shortcuts... The window opens as a floating window. A search box and a By key button are at the top, the list of commands fills the middle, and the foot "},{"t":"Search Commands","p":"Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"keyboard.html#f-shortcutsearch","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Keyboard shortcuts... The search box at the top of the window narrows the list as you type. Every word you type must appear in the command's name, in the submenu it belongs to, or in its keys, so \"mirror\" lists the mirroring commands and"},{"t":"By Key","p":"Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"keyboard.html#f-shortcutbykey","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Keyboard shortcuts... By key answers the opposite question to the search box, which is what a given key does. Click the button, then press the keys. The list then shows only the commands that use that combination, and the search box show"},{"t":"Changing a Key","p":"Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"keyboard.html#f-shortcutchange","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Keyboard shortcuts... To change a key, click the key box on the command's row, which shows a dash when the command has no key. The box turns orange and reads Press keys. Press the new keys, with any modifiers held, and the command has th"},{"t":"Several Keys on One Command","p":"Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"keyboard.html#f-shortcutmore","x":"Right-click A command's row in the window A command can have more than one key. Right-clicking a row opens a small menu for it. Options Change shortcut... Replaces all of the command's keys with the one you press next. Add another key... Gives the command an e"},{"t":"Start From","p":"Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"keyboard.html#f-shortcutstart","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Keyboard shortcuts... Start from replaces the starting point that your changes sit on. The button names the set in use, for example Start from: Framez, and opens a list of sets. Keys that a set does not mention keep the Framez key, unles"},{"t":"Save Mine to This Place","p":"Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"keyboard.html#f-shortcutsave","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Keyboard shortcuts... Save mine to this place... is the last row of the Start from list. It keeps your keys in the place under a name, so that everyone working in it can choose them from the This place part of the list. A small box asks "},{"t":"Reset All","p":"Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"keyboard.html#f-shortcutreset","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Keyboard shortcuts... Reset all returns every key to the set you started from. It asks \"Undo your N changes?\" with a red Reset all button and Cancel, so it never changes anything by accident. When you have made no changes, the status lin"},{"t":"Keys That Depend on the Window","p":"Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"keyboard.html#f-shortcutcontexts","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Keyboard shortcuts... Some commands work only while a particular window is the one receiving the key presses. The commands of the Curve editor are the example. They have their own group in the list, and a key given to one of them is kept"},{"t":"Fixed Keys","p":"Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"keyboard.html#f-shortcutfixed","x":"Menu Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Keyboard shortcuts... At the end of the list, the keys that only mean something while a tool is busy are shown without key boxes you can click. They cannot be changed, and they are listed so that you know which keys are taken while a too"},{"t":"Change Shortcut","p":"Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"keyboard.html#f-changeshortcut","x":"Right-click Any menu row, or a Menu search result Change shortcut... gives a single command a key of your own without opening the Keyboard Shortcuts window. Right-click the command in any menu, or in the results of Menu search, and choose it. A small box reads"},{"t":"Scripting","p":"Windows","u":"scripting.html","x":"The Scripting window lists the functions that let a script work on the open animation. You can run them from Studio's Command Bar or from a plugin of your own, to open and save animations, read and write keys, run Framez"},{"t":"The Scripting Window","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-scripting","x":"Menu Help ‣ Scripting... The Scripting window opens as a floating window. At the top is an example script in a box. Under it, the functions are listed in four groups with a line on what each does, and a final line says that a joint is given by its name, as Fra"},{"t":"Clip","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#scripting-clip","x":"The Clip functions open, save and describe the animation that Framez has open."},{"t":"Framez.version()","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-version","x":"Framez.version() returns the version of Framez as text. It takes no arguments."},{"t":"Framez.rig()","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-rig","x":"Framez.rig() returns the rig that is open, as a Model, or nil when no animation is open."},{"t":"Framez.open(rig, clip?)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-open","x":"Framez.open opens a rig for animating, and optionally opens one of its saved animations. It returns the rig. If another rig is open, Framez lets go of it first without asking, so save the animation with Framez.save before opening another rig. Options rig A Mod"},{"t":"Framez.save(name?)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-save","x":"Needs An open animation Framez.save saves the open animation, under its own name or under the name given, and returns the name it saved. Nothing is saved when the animation has no keys, and the function then returns nil with the message \"Nothing was saved: key"},{"t":"Framez.clip()","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-clip","x":"Needs An open animation Framez.clip() returns a table that describes the open animation. Its fields are name , fps for the frame rate, frames for the number of frames, and loops , which is true when the animation loops."},{"t":"Keys","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#scripting-keys","x":"The Keys functions read and write the keys of one joint. A key is the pose of a joint at one frame, stored as a CFrame, which is the same value that Framez keeps for the joint."},{"t":"Framez.joints()","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-joints","x":"Framez.joints() returns a list with the name of every joint of the open rig, parents first. These names are what the other functions accept as a joint. It takes no arguments and returns an empty list when no animation is open."},{"t":"Framez.key(joint, frame)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-key","x":"Framez.key returns the CFrame of the key that the joint has at that frame. When the joint has no key there, it returns nil with the message \"No key there.\" Options joint A joint name from Framez.joints() . frame The frame number."},{"t":"Framez.keys(joint)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-keys","x":"Framez.keys returns every key of a joint as a table of CFrames indexed by frame number. A joint with no keys gives an empty table."},{"t":"Framez.value(joint, frame)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-value","x":"Framez.value returns the CFrame that the joint has at a frame, as Framez shows it. Unlike Framez.key , it works on any frame, not only on a key, and it includes the effect of animation layers and curve modifiers. When the joint has no keys, it returns nil and "},{"t":"Framez.setKey(joint, frame, cf, style?)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-setkey","x":"Needs An open animation Framez.setKey sets a key on a joint and returns true . A key that already exists at that frame is replaced. Outside Framez.batch , each call is one undo step. Options joint A joint name from Framez.joints() . frame A frame from 0 to the"},{"t":"Framez.removeKey(joint, frame)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-removekey","x":"Needs An open animation Framez.removeKey removes the key that a joint has at a frame and returns true . When there is no key there it returns nil with the message \"No key there.\" A locked track is not changed. Outside Framez.batch , each call is one undo step."},{"t":"Framez.batch(fn)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-batch","x":"Needs An open animation Framez.batch runs a function so that everything it does is one undo step, and redraws the timeline once at the end. The function receives the Framez table as its argument. The result is whatever the function returns, or true when it ret"},{"t":"Tools","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#scripting-tools","x":"The Tools functions run the tools whose results appear as items in the Modifiers window, and work on the items that they made."},{"t":"Framez.kinds()","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-kinds","x":"Framez.kinds() returns a table of the tools that can be run from a script, with each tool's id as the key and its name as the value. The ids are what Framez.run and the other tool functions accept."},{"t":"Framez.run(kindId, params?)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-run","x":"Needs An open animation Framez.run runs a tool and returns the id of the item it made in the Modifiers window. The tool runs with its usual values, changed by whatever you pass in params, and it keys the animation as it does when run from a menu. Options kindI"},{"t":"Framez.change(itemId, params)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-change","x":"Framez.change changes values of an item and makes it again with them, as dragging a value in its card does. It returns true . Options itemId The id that Framez.run returned, or the id of an item from Framez.items() . params A table of the values to change. Val"},{"t":"Framez.move(itemId, dir)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-move","x":"Framez.move moves an item one place earlier or later in the Modifiers window and returns true . Each item is made again on top of whatever is then under it. At the end of the list it returns nil and says that the item is already at that end. Options itemId The"},{"t":"Framez.items()","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-items","x":"Framez.items() returns a list of the items in the open animation, in the order of the Modifiers window. Each entry is a table with id , kind for the tool's id, on for whether it is turned on, and params for its values. The values are copies, so changing them d"},{"t":"Framez.setOn(itemId, on)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-seton","x":"Framez.setOn turns an item on or off, as the eye button on its card does. The second value must be true or false . It returns true ."},{"t":"Framez.apply(itemId)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-apply","x":"Framez.apply keeps an item's keys and forgets how they were made, so the item leaves the Modifiers window and its keys stay in the animation. It returns true ."},{"t":"Framez.delete(itemId)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-delete","x":"Framez.delete takes an item out together with the keys it made, so the animation returns to how it was under it. It returns true ."},{"t":"Framez.addKind(def)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-addkind","x":"Framez.addKind adds a tool of your own. It appears in the Add menu of the Modifiers window, under My tools or under the group you name, and its results are items that can be changed, turned off and undone like those of any other tool. The function returns the "},{"t":"Events","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#scripting-events","x":"The Events function lets a script react to what happens to the open animation."},{"t":"Framez.on(event, fn)","p":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#f-api-on","x":"Framez.on calls a function whenever an animation is opened, saved or changed. The function receives a table that describes the animation, with the same fields as Framez.clip() , and for a save the name field is the name that was saved. The call returns a funct"},{"t":"Tutorials","p":"Tutorials","u":"recipes.html","x":"Each tutorial takes one goal from start to finish, with the menu path for every step and a link to the reference page of each tool it uses. Follow one from the top, or skip the steps your animation does not need."},{"t":"Making a Combat Skill","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-skill.html","x":"This tutorial takes a sword swing from the first blocked pose to a clip the game can play. By the end you have a saved animation that plays once at Action priority, with its hit frames marked for your scripts and its upp"},{"t":"Set up the Clip","p":"Making a Combat Skill","u":"r-skill.html#r-skill-1","x":"Open the animation settings . They open the Inspector's Clip page, where the clip's length, priority and looping are set. Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Animation settings Type the skill's cast time in Length , for example 0.8s . A bare number counts frames, so 24 makes th"},{"t":"Block Out the Poses","p":"Making a Combat Skill","u":"r-skill.html#r-skill-2","x":"Pose the rig on frame 0 in its guard stance. Select a limb in the viewport and drag the rings that appear round it. While Auto key is on, letting go of a handle keys the limb. Move the playhead to the wind-up, pose the rig again, and give every limb a key on t"},{"t":"Fix the Timing and Shape the Moves","p":"Making a Combat Skill","u":"r-skill.html#r-skill-3","x":"Select the keys of the swing by dragging a box round them on the timeline. A box with a handle at each end appears round the selection. Drag an end of the box to make the swing faster or slower. The keys spread or squeeze toward the other end, and Alt stretche"},{"t":"Add Weight","p":"Making a Combat Skill","u":"r-skill.html#r-skill-4","x":"Let the arms keep moving after the body stops. Follow-through makes the head and arms, or the limb you have selected, trail behind what moves them, swing past and settle. Its values open in Adjust at the foot of the Framez window, and dragging a value makes th"},{"t":"Keep the Feet Planted","p":"Making a Combat Skill","u":"r-skill.html#r-skill-5","x":"Turn on the Foot sliding overlay. A red mark appears on the events lane wherever a foot that is on the floor slides. View ‣ Overlays ‣ Foot sliding At a red mark, select the foot and run Foot lock . The foot stays where it is over the frames you choose, and th"},{"t":"Mark the Hit for the Game","p":"Making a Combat Skill","u":"r-skill.html#r-skill-6","x":"Switch to the Game data workspace. Its Game menu holds everything the game reads from the clip. View ‣ Windows ‣ Game data Put the playhead on the first frame where the sword can hurt and add a hit window . A bar appears on the Hit windows row under the rig. D"},{"t":"Check and Test It","p":"Making a Combat Skill","u":"r-skill.html#r-skill-7","x":"Run Check animation . It lists each problem at its frame, such as sliding feet, a limb that jumps in one frame, a limb that stops dead, a curve that passes its key and comes back, and a limb that goes through the torso. Click a row to go to its frame, and use "},{"t":"Save It and Bring It into the Game","p":"Making a Combat Skill","u":"r-skill.html#r-skill-8","x":"If the skill must play while the character runs, set a body mask before you save. With Upper body, the legs play whatever is under the skill, such as the run. Game ‣ Body mask ‣ Upper body Save the animation, then save the place. File ‣ Save Publish the clip s"},{"t":"Making a Hit with Effects","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-animehit.html","x":"This tutorial builds a punch with a trail, sparks, a shockwave and an impact frame, all keyed on the timeline. By the end you have a saved clip whose effects, screen look and hit-stop play in your game every time the cli"},{"t":"Prepare the Punch","p":"Making a Hit with Effects","u":"r-animehit.html#r-animehit-1","x":"Key the wind-up, the contact and the recovery of the punch. Note the frame where the fist lands, because the effects are timed to it. Switch to the Scene workspace. Effects are added from its Scene menu. View ‣ Windows ‣ Scene Put the playhead on the contact f"},{"t":"Add the Effects","p":"Making a Hit with Effects","u":"r-animehit.html#r-animehit-2","x":"Add the sparks. The menu names the selected limb, and each built-in effect is made of ordinary particle emitters at the end of that limb. Hit sparks is a flash and a burst of sparks, each on its own row of the timeline. The + at the right of the Filter box ope"},{"t":"Place and Tune the Effects","p":"Making a Hit with Effects","u":"r-animehit.html#r-animehit-3","x":"Move each effect to where the hit lands. Where it hits puts the effect's point on the nearest part of another character's body at that frame. You can also move the point with Studio's Move tool, and the move is keyed at the playhead. Right-click the effect's r"},{"t":"Add a Shockwave from a Part","p":"Making a Hit with Effects","u":"r-animehit.html#r-animehit-4","x":"Put a Ball part with the ForceField material in the place, where the punch lands. Select the part in the Explorer and add it to the scene. It gets a row of its own, and any property you change in the Properties window is keyed at the playhead. Scene ‣ Add ‣ Se"},{"t":"Add the Impact Frame","p":"Making a Hit with Effects","u":"r-animehit.html#r-animehit-5","x":"Add an impact frame on the contact frame. It adds an Impact event there and opens its page in the Inspector, where you build the screen look for the hit. The same command is also in the Game menu of the Game data workspace. Right-click the contact frame on the"},{"t":"Bring the Effects into the Game","p":"Making a Hit with Effects","u":"r-animehit.html#r-animehit-6","x":"Add the FramezEffects script to the game, once per game. It plays the impact frames, the effects keyed in Framez and the keyed properties from any clip saved by Framez. See Effects in the game . Game ‣ In the game ‣ Effects in the game Save the animation, then"},{"t":"Making an Idle or Fidget","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-idle.html","x":"This tutorial builds a standing idle that breathes and loops without a jump at the seam, and a fidget such as a head scratch or a weapon check that starts from the same stance. By the end you have a looping clip at Idle "},{"t":"Set up the Clip and the Stance","p":"Making an Idle or Fidget","u":"r-idle.html#r-idle-1","x":"Open the animation settings , set Priority to Idle and switch Looping on. Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Animation settings Pose the stance you want the character to stand in on frame 0, then give every limb a key. Key ‣ Insert key on all limbs Save the stance in the Poses"},{"t":"Key the Fidget","p":"Making an Idle or Fidget","u":"r-idle.html#r-idle-2","x":"Move the playhead and pose the fidget, keying each pose as you go. For a head scratch, for example, key the arm and the head and let the other limbs hold the stance. If you change a pose that is held across several keys, the held keys keep the old pose. Propag"},{"t":"Close the Loop","p":"Making an Idle or Fidget","u":"r-idle.html#r-idle-3","x":"Close the loop with Make cyclic and choose Same pose at start and end. It keys the last frame as frame 0, turns Looping on, and opens its values in Adjust. Adjust is the panel at the foot of the Framez window, and dragging a value runs the tool again with it. "},{"t":"Check the Seam and the Hand-Over","p":"Making an Idle or Fidget","u":"r-idle.html#r-idle-4","x":"Run Check animation . It reports a loop seam when the last frame does not match frame 0, and its Close button fixes it. Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Check animation Switch to the Game data workspace. View ‣ Windows ‣ Game data Check how another clip flows into the idle. T"},{"t":"Making a Walk, Run, Strafe or Backpedal","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-cycle.html","x":"This tutorial makes a locomotion cycle, fixes its feet, marks where each foot lands and checks how it blends with your other cycles. By the end you have a looping clip at Movement priority with planted feet and foot cont"},{"t":"Generate the Cycle","p":"Making a Walk, Run, Strafe or Backpedal","u":"r-cycle.html#r-cycle-1","x":"Open the animation settings and set Priority to Movement. Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Animation settings Run Locomotion cycle . A walk plays at once, and it already loops. Its values open in Adjust, the panel at the foot of the Framez window, and dragging a value makes "},{"t":"Or Start from Your Own Poses","p":"Making a Walk, Run, Strafe or Backpedal","u":"r-cycle.html#r-cycle-2","x":"Key the contact pose on one frame, with the legs apart and one foot down, and the passing pose on another frame, with the legs crossing under the body. Key ‣ Insert key on all limbs Run Cycle from poses . In Adjust, set Contact pose and Passing pose to those t"},{"t":"Match the Two Sides","p":"Making a Walk, Run, Strafe or Backpedal","u":"r-cycle.html#r-cycle-3","x":"Change the pose of one arm or leg, then copy it onto its twin. The row names the twin, so on the right upper arm it reads Mirror onto LeftUpperArm. See Mirror onto twin . Right-click the limb ‣ Mirror onto twin To rebuild the second half of the cycle from the "},{"t":"Find and Fix the Feet","p":"Making a Walk, Run, Strafe or Backpedal","u":"r-cycle.html#r-cycle-4","x":"Turn on Foot sliding . A red mark appears on the events lane wherever a foot on the floor slides. View ‣ Overlays ‣ Foot sliding Turn on Motion paths to see the path of the feet and spot a hitch in a step. View ‣ Overlays ‣ Motion paths ‣ Show motion paths At "},{"t":"Mark the Footsteps for the Game","p":"Making a Walk, Run, Strafe or Backpedal","u":"r-cycle.html#r-cycle-5","x":"Switch to the Game data workspace. View ‣ Windows ‣ Game data Run Find foot contacts . It reads each landing and lift off the clip as it plays and shows them on the Foot contacts row, a strip for each foot with a bar while that foot is down. Drag a bar to move"},{"t":"Blend It with Another Cycle","p":"Making a Walk, Run, Strafe or Backpedal","u":"r-cycle.html#r-cycle-6","x":"If the game blends this cycle with another, such as a walk into a run, preview the blend. Blend with plays both clips in a Roblox Blend1D on a see-through copy of the rig. Select the other clip from the list and drag Blend value from 0 to 1. Game ‣ Preview wit"},{"t":"Watch a Skill over the Run","p":"Making a Walk, Run, Strafe or Backpedal","u":"r-cycle.html#r-cycle-7","x":"To judge a skill's arms against the run it will play on, open the skill and play the run under it. Base clip loops a saved animation of the rig under the open one, and Base clip plays on chooses which limbs it plays on. Key ‣ Layers ‣ Preview ‣ Base clip"},{"t":"Copying a Move from a Video","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-video.html","x":"This tutorial keys a character's body from the person in a video, such as game footage, a phone video or a dance, and then cleans the result up. By the end you have a first pass of the move on your rig, with smooth motio"},{"t":"Bring the Video In","p":"Copying a Move from a Video","u":"r-video.html#r-video-1","x":"Open the Reference video window. It sits beside the timeline and plays the video locked to it. View ‣ Windows ‣ Reference video Click the clip button at the top of the window and select your video from the list, which shows the videos in your clip folders with"},{"t":"Key the Body","p":"Copying a Move from a Video","u":"r-video.html#r-video-2","x":"Run Pose from video . It keys the torso, head, arms and legs every few frames from the person in the linked video. The camera's angle does not matter, because the pose is measured from the person's own hips. Pose ‣ Generate ‣ Pose from video Play the result be"},{"t":"Clean up the Motion","p":"Copying a Move from a Video","u":"r-video.html#r-video-3","x":"Run Mocap cleanup . It smooths the motion, puts the feet on the floor wherever one is planted, and plants feet that slide. Choose Keep the travel or In place under Travel , and change the other values in Adjust until the motion reads well. Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Moc"},{"t":"Match a Weapon or Hand Path","p":"Copying a Move from a Video","u":"r-video.html#r-video-4","x":"Mark the weapon tip on the video with Marks . Click the picture where the tip is to place a mark, and the marks draw a path 30 frames each side. Right-click the mark button to mark a hand or a foot instead. View ‣ Reference ‣ Mark the weapon tip Show the rig's"},{"t":"Animating a Dance to Music","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-dance.html","x":"This tutorial keys a dance so that its moves land on the beats of a song. By the end you have a clip whose key poses sit on the music's hits and still moments, ready to loop or to turn into an emote."},{"t":"Bring in the Music","p":"Animating a Dance to Music","u":"r-dance.html#r-dance-1","x":"Link a video that has the song, as described in Copying a Move from a Video . The video follows the timeline from then on, and the helper reads its picture and its sound for the beats. View ‣ Windows ‣ Reference video Look at the beats strip over the ruler. Wh"},{"t":"Key the Moves and Snap Them to the Beat","p":"Animating a Dance to Music","u":"r-dance.html#r-dance-2","x":"Key the poses of the dance roughly, a pose near each beat. Move the playhead to a frame, pose the rig, and give every limb a key. Key ‣ Insert key on all limbs Select the keys you have placed, by dragging a box round them on the timeline. Run Snap keys to vide"},{"t":"Polish the Moves","p":"Animating a Dance to Music","u":"r-dance.html#r-dance-3","x":"For a wave that ripples down the arms, select the keys of the limbs along one arm and run Offset limbs . Each limb's keys land a few frames after the limb above it, and Frames between in Adjust sets the gap. Key ‣ Timing ‣ Offset limbs Select the keys of one m"},{"t":"Making an Emote to Sell","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-ugc.html","x":"This tutorial checks a dance or gesture against Roblox's rules for emotes, fixes whatever Roblox would refuse, and takes it through Roblox's two upload windows. By the end you have an emote that passes the upload check a"},{"t":"Make the Animation on an R15","p":"Making an Emote to Sell","u":"r-ugc.html#r-ugc-1","x":"Add a new R15 rig. It stands on the ground in front of the camera and opens at once. File ‣ Rig ‣ New R15 rig Make the dance or gesture on it, as described in Animating a Dance to Music . Keep the body near where the character stands, with no part more than 5 "},{"t":"Check It Against Roblox's Rules","p":"Making an Emote to Sell","u":"r-ugc.html#r-ugc-2","x":"Open Emote . It checks the clip against Roblox's rules for emotes and lists each rule as a row. A red row is something Roblox would refuse, a grey row is something Save leaves out of the emote, and a green row passes. Click a row to go to its frame. The switch"},{"t":"Save It and Upload It","p":"Making an Emote to Sell","u":"r-ugc.html#r-ugc-3","x":"Click Save as emote at the bottom right of the window. It saves the emote as NAME_EMOTE beside the rig's saves, and the window then shows Roblox's two upload steps as cards. For step 1, click Open Roblox's upload window . In it, set Animation Type to Emote, ti"},{"t":"Making an Animation Pack","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-pack.html","x":"This tutorial gathers a character's idle, walk, run, jump, fall, climb and swim clips into one pack and prepares it either for the Marketplace or for every character in your own game. By the end you have a checked pack, "},{"t":"Build the Pack","p":"Making an Animation Pack","u":"r-pack.html#r-pack-1","x":"Save the clip that is open. Checking the pack opens each of its clips in turn. File ‣ Save Open Animation pack . The window has one slot for each clip of a pack: Idle, Idle 2, Walk, Run, Jump, Fall, Climb, Swim and Swim idle. File ‣ Export ‣ Animation pack... "},{"t":"Check It Against the Marketplace's Rules","p":"Making an Animation Pack","u":"r-pack.html#r-pack-2","x":"Click Check . It opens each clip in turn and lists what the Marketplace would refuse. The rules are an R15, a loop on every clip except Jump, a length of at most 10 s, only the LowerTorso moving and no more than 1.5 studs from where it starts, and Roblox's own"},{"t":"Sell It on the Marketplace","p":"Making an Animation Pack","u":"r-pack.html#r-pack-3","x":"Click For the Marketplace . Each clip becomes a CurveAnimation named after the pack and its slot, kept in the pack's folder and selected in the Explorer. The window then lists the values that Roblox's Save to Roblox asks for on each clip, which are Animation t"},{"t":"Or Use It in Your Own Game","p":"Making an Animation Pack","u":"r-pack.html#r-pack-4","x":"Publish each clip of the pack, so that every clip has an asset id. File ‣ Publish to Roblox... Click Use in this game . It adds a LocalScript named FramezMovement to StarterCharacterScripts, which puts the pack's clip ids into each character's Animate script. "},{"t":"Animating Jumps, Flips and Spins","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-acro.html","x":"This tutorial animates a leap with a flip so that the body flies a true arc, turns the way physics would turn it, and lands without sliding. By the end you have a jump whose air time, height and spin have been checked ag"},{"t":"Key the Take-Off and the Landing","p":"Animating Jumps, Flips and Spins","u":"r-acro.html#r-acro-1","x":"Pose the rig as it leaves the ground, with the knees bent and the arms swung back, and give every limb a key on that frame. Key ‣ Insert key on all limbs Move the playhead to the frame where the feet touch down again and key the landing pose. Leave at least 2 "},{"t":"Give the Body a Real Arc","p":"Animating Jumps, Flips and Spins","u":"r-acro.html#r-acro-2","x":"Select the two root keys, or put the playhead between them, and run Ballistic trajectory . The torso flies an arc between the two poses. Its values open in Adjust, the panel at the foot of the Framez window. Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Ballistic trajectory Leave Roblox g"},{"t":"Flip, Roll or Spin the Body","p":"Animating Jumps, Flips and Spins","u":"r-acro.html#r-acro-3","x":"Move the playhead to a frame in the air and run Pivot rotation . Choose the point to turn about, such as a foot, a hand, the weapon tip or the floor, then choose Spin, Flip or Roll. Drag Angle and the whole body turns as you drag. Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Pivot rotati"},{"t":"Check the Air Time","p":"Animating Jumps, Flips and Spins","u":"r-acro.html#r-acro-4","x":"Turn on Spin check . In each jump, teal ghosts show the turn and the height that physics would give, because a body turns faster when it is tucked and slower when it is stretched, and its centre of mass flies gravity's arc. The take-off and landing stay as you"},{"t":"Land Cleanly","p":"Animating Jumps, Flips and Spins","u":"r-acro.html#r-acro-5","x":"Turn on Centre of mass and scrub through the landing. The disc under the body is green while the centre of mass is over the feet and red where the body would tip. View ‣ Overlays ‣ Centre of mass Plant the landing foot with Foot lock , so it stays where it tou"},{"t":"Making a Ragdoll Fall or Hit Reaction","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-ragdoll.html","x":"This tutorial makes a knockdown, a death fall or a stagger by pushing the body at one frame and letting Roblox physics take over. By the end you have the fall baked into the clip as keys you can change, either in the cli"},{"t":"Set the Push","p":"Making a Ragdoll Fall or Hit Reaction","u":"r-ragdoll.html#r-ragdoll-1","x":"Go to the frame where the hit lands. Right-click the limb that is hit and open Ragdoll . The Ragdoll window opens with its sections for Start, Push, Body and Length. Right-click the limb ‣ Ragdoll... Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Ragdoll... The Ragdoll window, with the sta"},{"t":"Choose How the Body Behaves","p":"Making a Ragdoll Fall or Hit Reaction","u":"r-ragdoll.html#r-ragdoll-2","x":"Under Body, click Limp to let the whole body fall freely. Click Powered to keep part of the pose, and then set a strength from 0 to 100 % for the Head, the Spine, the Arms and the Legs. Keep standing pulls the torso toward its keyed place. Under Length, set Up"},{"t":"Simulate and Bake the Fall","p":"Making a Ragdoll Fall or Hit Reaction","u":"r-ragdoll.html#r-ragdoll-3","x":"Click Simulate , then scrub the timeline. The purple ghost plays the result. The rig itself does not move while it simulates, because a copy falls and hits the parts of the place. To make the character get up again, turn on Blend back under Back to the animati"},{"t":"Add Forces over Time with the Physics Window","p":"Making a Ragdoll Fall or Hit Reaction","u":"r-ragdoll.html#r-ragdoll-4","x":"For wind, a blast or a pull that acts over several frames, or for more than one body, open the Physics window . The tree on its left holds the Body, Forces, Colliders and Settings, and each row has a lane over the clip on the right. Pose ‣ Physics ‣ Physics wi"},{"t":"Animating Talking, Reacting and Pointing","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-acting.html","x":"This tutorial animates a character who looks at what matters, gestures with their arms and moves with weight, framed the way the shot will be seen. By the end you have a performance clip with a saved camera view, a head "},{"t":"Frame the Shot","p":"Animating Talking, Reacting and Pointing","u":"r-acting.html#r-acting-1","x":"Fly Studio's camera until the shot is framed the way players will see it. Save the view with the animation. It is saved under a name such as View 1, and you return to it any time by choosing it in the same menu. Rename view and Delete view appear there once a "},{"t":"Aim the Head and the Arms","p":"Animating Talking, Reacting and Pointing","u":"r-acting.html#r-acting-2","x":"Turn the head to face its target. Right-click the head and choose Look at . If a part or model is selected in the Explorer, the head looks at it, and with nothing selected it looks at the place where Studio's camera is now. Right-click the head ‣ Look at Aim a"},{"t":"Block the Poses and Add Weight","p":"Animating Talking, Reacting and Pointing","u":"r-acting.html#r-acting-3","x":"Block the performance in poses, and view it as held poses while you judge the timing. Stepped preview set to On keys holds each key until the next one. View ‣ Stepped preview ‣ On keys Add Overlap , so the head and arms start moving a few frames after the body"},{"t":"Keep the Gestures You Use Again","p":"Animating Talking, Reacting and Pointing","u":"r-acting.html#r-acting-4","x":"Pose a gesture you will want again, such as a shrug or a raised hand. Click Save pose in the Poses window to keep it. View ‣ Windows ‣ Poses In any animation, click the gesture's picture to key it at the playhead. Press the picture and drag sideways to blend t"},{"t":"Go Further","p":"Animating Talking, Reacting and Pointing","u":"r-acting.html#r-acting-5","x":"For more than one character, a moving camera, lights or sound, continue with Making a Cutscene . For expressions and talking, see Animating Faces on a Classic Head and Animating Faces on a Dynamic Head ."},{"t":"Making a Cutscene","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-cutscene.html","x":"This tutorial builds a story moment, a boss introduction or a short film. Several characters and props share one timeline, keyed cameras cut between shots, and lights, sound, subtitles and screen effects are added on top"},{"t":"Set up the Characters and Props","p":"Making a Cutscene","u":"r-cutscene.html#r-cutscene-1","x":"Open or start the animation on the first character. Stand the other characters where they begin, using Studio's Move tool. Switch to the Scene workspace. It shows the Scene menu and the rows for cameras, props, lights and sound. View ‣ Windows ‣ Scene Add each"},{"t":"Film the First Shot","p":"Making a Cutscene","u":"r-cutscene.html#r-cutscene-2","x":"Add a camera. It is placed where Studio's view is now, and it is drawn as a camera body with its view as lines. See Camera . Scene ‣ Add ‣ Camera from view Look through camera. The shot appears in the viewport with its frame drawn and the outside dimmed. Press"},{"t":"Keep a Character Framed and Cut Between Shots","p":"Making a Cutscene","u":"r-cutscene.html#r-cutscene-3","x":"Keep a character in the shot. Select the camera in the list and choose Look at , then choose the character's head. The camera turns to keep it in view from the playhead on. See Look at . Scene ‣ Motion ‣ Look at On the camera's Inspector page, set Frame at to "},{"t":"Move Characters and Props Along Paths","p":"Making a Cutscene","u":"r-cutscene.html#r-cutscene-4","x":"Draw a path on the ground. Click its points in the viewport, then press Enter or double-click to end it. Backspace takes the last point back and Esc cancels. Scene ‣ Add ‣ Path Make a character walk it. The character eases up to its pace, stops at the end, and"},{"t":"Add Light, Sound and Subtitles","p":"Making a Cutscene","u":"r-cutscene.html#r-cutscene-5","x":"Add a light from the lights in the place. Type Brightness or Colour on its Inspector page to key it at the playhead. To move the light, add the part it sits in as a prop. Scene ‣ Add ‣ Light ‣ the lamp Add a sound . Paste its asset id, or choose one of the Sou"},{"t":"Save the Scene and Put It in the Game","p":"Making a Cutscene","u":"r-cutscene.html#r-cutscene-6","x":"Save. One Save keeps every character's clip and the scene itself. Then save the place. File ‣ Save Open Put in the game . One dialog opens with the scene's name as its title. Scene ‣ Put in the game... The Put in the game dialog, with the five ways a scene can"},{"t":"Animating a Grab, Throw or Finisher","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-grab.html","x":"This tutorial animates one character acting on another, such as a grab, a throw or a finisher, with both characters keyed on one timeline. At the end you have a scene in which both characters play together, saved and rea"},{"t":"Put Both Characters in One Scene","p":"Animating a Grab, Throw or Finisher","u":"r-grab.html#r-grab-1","x":"Click the attacker in the viewport, or in the list Framez shows, and start a new animation for it. See New Animation . File ‣ New animation Switch to the Scene workspace by clicking the Scene tab at the left of the menus. The Scene menu and the scene's rows ar"},{"t":"Pose the Attacker","p":"Animating a Grab, Throw or Finisher","u":"r-grab.html#r-grab-2","x":"Pose the attacker with the handles in the viewport at each frame where the move changes, such as the reach, the grab, the lift and the throw. Letting go of a handle keys the pose while Auto key is on, which is the default. Drag the playhead along the timeline "},{"t":"Pose the Second Character","p":"Animating a Grab, Throw or Finisher","u":"r-grab.html#r-grab-3","x":"Click one of the second character's limbs in the viewport to pose that character. Its row opens with a posing tag, and the handles, the Inspector and the Pose tools now work on it. The attacker folds to a single lane of diamonds and keeps playing its own keys."},{"t":"Add the Impact Sound and Put the Scene in the Game","p":"Animating a Grab, Throw or Finisher","u":"r-grab.html#r-grab-4","x":"Move the playhead to the frame where the attack lands and add an event named Hit. A script in your game can listen for it. The Game menu is in the Game data workspace. See Add Event . Game ‣ Add event here... Add the sound for the impact. Type its asset id or "},{"t":"Picking Up, Passing and Throwing an Object","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-hold.html","x":"This tutorial animates a cup, sword, box or hat that a hand picks up, passes to the other hand or to another character, throws or drops, and picks up again. At the end the object follows the character through every chang"},{"t":"Put the Object in a Hand","p":"Picking Up, Passing and Throwing an Object","u":"r-hold.html#r-hold-1","x":"Select the object in the Explorer. Right-click the hand that holds it in the viewport and put the object in that hand with Hold the Selected Object . An object that stands far from the hand lands in it. A Tool sits in the hand by its Grip, and any other object"},{"t":"Pass It to Another Holder","p":"Picking Up, Passing and Throwing an Object","u":"r-hold.html#r-hold-2","x":"Move the playhead to the frame where the object changes hands. Right-click the object and choose the new holder with Parent To . The list offers the hands, the head, the torso and the feet of this rig, then the holders of any other character in the scene, then"},{"t":"Throw, Drop or Hide It","p":"Picking Up, Passing and Throwing an Object","u":"r-hold.html#r-hold-3","x":"Move the playhead to the frame where the hand lets go. Right-click the object and choose Throw or drop. In the box, Release at is the frame the object lets go, Distance is how far it travels in studs along the way the hand was moving, or in front of the charac"},{"t":"Hold It with Two Hands","p":"Picking Up, Passing and Throwing an Object","u":"r-hold.html#r-hold-4","x":"Right-click the free hand and choose the Pin row that reads Hand on followed by the object's name. The hand stays where it is on the object from the playhead on, wherever the object goes, until you release the pin. See Hand on Object . Right-click the free han"},{"t":"Play It in Your Game","p":"Picking Up, Passing and Throwing an Object","u":"r-hold.html#r-hold-5","x":"Add the script that moves held objects in the game. This is done once for each game. It adds FramezHeld to ReplicatedStorage and FramezHeldClient to StarterPlayerScripts, and every clip in which an object changes holder then moves it for every player and puts "},{"t":"Animating Faces on a Classic Head","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-faces.html","x":"This tutorial changes the face of a character whose head has a face Decal, with blinks, a shout on the hit and a smirk at the end. At the end the character changes faces as the animation plays, in Framez and in your game"},{"t":"Key the Faces","p":"Animating Faces on a Classic Head","u":"r-faces.html#r-faces-1","x":"Move the playhead to the frame where the face changes and open the face picker, or click the lane of the Face row, under Head, at that frame. The picker shows Own face first, then the faces this clip already uses, then My faces, In this place and Roblox faces."},{"t":"Check the Faces","p":"Animating Faces on a Classic Head","u":"r-faces.html#r-faces-2","x":"Drag the playhead along the timeline to see each face change on the rig. Turn on the Animator preview. A see-through copy of the rig stands beside it and changes face the way the game will. The Game menu is in the Game data workspace. See Animator Preview . Ga"},{"t":"Show the Faces in Your Game","p":"Animating Faces on a Classic Head","u":"r-faces.html#r-faces-3","x":"Add the face script to the game. This is done once for each game. The box says whether the game already has it, and its button reads Add it to this game, Update it or Add it again. If Studio stops Framez from adding scripts, allow it in the Plugins tab, under "},{"t":"Animating Faces on a Dynamic Head","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-dynface.html","x":"This tutorial keys raised brows, a smile, a frown or a shout on a head with FaceControls, such as a Roblox avatar head or a MeshPart head set up for face animation. At the end the face is part of the clip and plays in yo"},{"t":"Pose the Face with the Face Map","p":"Animating Faces on a Dynamic Head","u":"r-dynface.html#r-dynface-1","x":"Click the Face row under Head. The Inspector shows the face map, a front view of the face with a dot for each part, including the brows, eyes, cheeks, nose, lip corners, lips, tongue, jaw and chin. The row opens to Brows, Eyes, Jaw, Mouth and Tongue, and each "},{"t":"Use a Ready-Made Expression","p":"Animating Faces on a Dynamic Head","u":"r-dynface.html#r-dynface-2","x":"Open the list of ready faces. It holds expressions such as Smile, Frown, Angry, Surprised, Scared, Shout, Smirk L, Smirk R, Pout and Tongue out, then your own saved faces under My poses, then the lip-sync mouth shapes. Point at one to see it on the head, and c"},{"t":"Save and Publish","p":"Animating Faces on a Dynamic Head","u":"r-dynface.html#r-dynface-3","x":"Save the animation and publish it as usual. The face is part of the clip, saved as Roblox's own face animation, so the game plays it with no script. Clips made in Roblox's Face Animation editor open in Framez with their face keys. File ‣ Save File ‣ Publish to"},{"t":"Setting up Lip Sync","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-lipsync.html","x":"This tutorial times mouth shapes to a voice line, for a short, a cutscene or an NPC's greeting. At the end the character's mouth moves with the voice and the lip sync is saved with the clip."},{"t":"Put the Voice on the Timeline","p":"Setting up Lip Sync","u":"r-lipsync.html#r-lipsync-1","x":"Switch to the Scene workspace by clicking the Scene tab at the left of the menus. View ‣ Windows ‣ Scene Add the voice line as a sound. Type its asset id or link, or choose one of the Sounds in the place. The sound gets a row with a block that shows its loudne"},{"t":"Prepare the Head","p":"Setting up Lip Sync","u":"r-lipsync.html#r-lipsync-2","x":"On a classic head, give the head a mouth. This is done once for each character. It adds a Decal named Mouth on the face side of the head, which shows Framez's own mouth shapes at once with nothing to upload. A dynamic head has its own mouth, so this row is gre"},{"t":"Make the Lip Sync","p":"Setting up Lip Sync","u":"r-lipsync.html#r-lipsync-3","x":"Open the Lip sync box. The box reads the sound that is selected, or the first sound on the timeline when none is selected. Sound id lets you type an id instead of using a sound on the timeline, and Text only makes the shapes from the typed line alone, between "},{"t":"Check and Correct It","p":"Setting up Lip Sync","u":"r-lipsync.html#r-lipsync-4","x":"Drag the playhead along the timeline, or play the animation, and watch the mouth against the voice. See Lip Sync Row . To correct a shape, right-click its block on the Lip sync row and choose another shape. Drag a block to move the change to another frame. The"},{"t":"Save It and Play It in Your Game","p":"Setting up Lip Sync","u":"r-lipsync.html#r-lipsync-5","x":"Save the animation. A dynamic head's mouth is stored as face control keys, which the game plays with no script. File ‣ Save On a classic head, add the face script to the game once for each game, because the mouth is a Decal that changes image. The Game menu is"},{"t":"Capturing a Face from a Webcam","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-capture.html","x":"This tutorial records your own expressions and speech with a webcam and puts them on the character's face. At the end the take is keyed on the head, which is faster than keying each expression by hand."},{"t":"Record the Take","p":"Capturing a Face from a Webcam","u":"r-capture.html#r-capture-1","x":"Move the playhead to the frame where the take should start. Open the face capture. The capture page opens in your browser and a box opens in Framez. Keep the box open until the take is placed. On a dynamic head the character's face follows yours live while the"},{"t":"Place It on the Face","p":"Capturing a Face from a Webcam","u":"r-capture.html#r-capture-2","x":"In Framez, set how the take is applied. Strength scales the expressions, from 0 to 200 %, with 100 % as the starting value. Smooth smooths the motion over a number of frames, from 0 to 10, with 2 as the starting value. On a dynamic head, Head turns too also ke"},{"t":"Keep the Voice","p":"Capturing a Face from a Webcam","u":"r-capture.html#r-capture-3","x":"If you recorded your voice, the page saves it as a WAV file in the Framez folder inside your LocalAppData folder. Upload it to Roblox to use it as a sound, and add it to a scene when you want it to play with the animation. Scene ‣ Add ‣ Sound..."},{"t":"Testing an Animation in the Game","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-bench.html","x":"This tutorial runs the open animation on your own character in Play with the game's priority and fades, then checks it on other body types, through the game's camera and against earlier saves. At the end you have seen ho"},{"t":"Open the Test Bench","p":"Testing an Animation in the Game","u":"r-bench.html#r-bench-1","x":"Switch to the Game data workspace by clicking the Game data tab at the left of the menus. View ‣ Windows ‣ Game data Open the Test bench. It is a floating window with a test scene picker at the top and five tabs under it, named Game, Transitions, Bodies, Camer"},{"t":"Play It on Your Character","p":"Testing an Animation in the Game","u":"r-bench.html#r-bench-2","x":"Open the Game tab and choose the Slot the game plays the clip in. The choices are Action on a key, Idle, Walk, Run, Jump, Fall, Climb and Swim. See Game Tab . The Game tab of the Test bench, set to play the clip as an action on the F key. When the Slot is Acti"},{"t":"Check How It Joins Other Animations","p":"Testing an Animation in the Game","u":"r-bench.html#r-bench-3","x":"Open the Transitions tab and turn on In the test . Press Clip before or Clip after to add an animation on the lane above this clip. Choose what each one plays from the Clip list, which holds the game's own Idle, Walk, Run, Jump, Fall and Climb and the other sa"},{"t":"Check It on Other Bodies","p":"Testing an Animation in the Game","u":"r-bench.html#r-bench-4","x":"Open the Bodies tab and turn on In the test . Under Play on , turn on the body types to try. An R15 rig offers R15 Classic, Rthro, Slender, Scaled 1.5 and My avatar. An R6 rig offers R6, Scaled 1.5, Scaled 0.75 and My avatar. To add any model from the place, s"},{"t":"Check It Through the Game's Camera and Against Other Versions","p":"Testing an Animation in the Game","u":"r-bench.html#r-bench-5","x":"Open the Camera tab and turn on In the test . Choose a view from Free, Third person, First and Shoulder. Set Distance from 1 to 60 studs, which a first person view does not use, Field of view from 20° to 120°, and Follows , which is the body the camera follows"},{"t":"Keep Your Settings","p":"Testing an Animation in the Game","u":"r-bench.html#r-bench-6","x":"Save the test scene so the next test starts from the same values. A test scene keeps every tab's values. The button with three dots beside the picker offers Save as my test scene..., which keeps the scene for you, and Save in this place..., which keeps it in t"},{"t":"Editing an Animation in Blender","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-blender.html","x":"This tutorial sends the open animation to Blender, edits it with Blender's tools and brings it back into Framez as keys. At the end the edited animation is in Framez, and you know how to keep both programs in step while "},{"t":"Send the Animation to Blender","p":"Editing an Animation in Blender","u":"r-blender.html#r-blender-1","x":"In Framez, send the open animation to Blender. It carries the rig, the pose on every frame, and which frames are keys and how each one eases. Events, weapon switches, face keys, hit windows and foot contacts arrive as timeline markers. See Send to Blender . Fi"},{"t":"Edit It in Blender","p":"Editing an Animation in Blender","u":"r-blender.html#r-blender-2","x":"Edit the animation in pose mode with Blender's tools. Blender sends back the pose of every bone on every frame, so constraints and IK in Blender can be used to make the pose. To bring a Blender rig's constraints, limits and drivers into Framez as well, select "},{"t":"Bring It Back into Framez","p":"Editing an Animation in Blender","u":"r-blender.html#r-blender-3","x":"In Blender, click Send to Framez . Blender posts every bone's pose on every frame of the scene's frame range. In Framez, take the result into the open animation. Frames that Blender marks as keys become keys with Blender's easing, and Framez adds a key whereve"},{"t":"Work in Both Programs at Once","p":"Editing an Animation in Blender","u":"r-blender.html#r-blender-4","x":"With the animation open, turn on the live link. A tick shows beside the row while it is on. See Live Link With Blender . File ‣ Export ‣ Live link with Blender In Blender, open the Framez tab and click Live . Edit in either program. An edit in Framez reaches B"},{"t":"Starting from an Existing Animation","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-import.html","x":"This tutorial brings in a catalog animation, an emote, a clip made for another rig, a Mixamo or mocap file, or a Moon Animator 2 project as keys on your rig. At the end you have the motion as ordinary keys, cleaned up an"},{"t":"Open the Rig","p":"Starting from an Existing Animation","u":"r-import.html#r-import-1","x":"Click the rig in the viewport, or in the list Framez shows, and start the animation that the imported motion goes into. To add to an animation you already made, open it instead. See New Animation and Open . File ‣ New animation File ‣ Open... The Import submen"},{"t":"Bring in a Roblox Animation","p":"Starting from an Existing Animation","u":"r-import.html#r-import-2","x":"Open the Roblox animation box. See Roblox Animation . File ‣ Import ‣ Roblox animation... The Roblox animation dialog. Paste the asset id or link, then choose whether it replaces the clip or goes in at the playhead. Choose Replace the clip to put the motion in"},{"t":"Bring in an FBX or BVH File","p":"Starting from an Existing Animation","u":"r-import.html#r-import-3","x":"Send the rig to Blender. Framez sends the rig, and Blender puts the clip on it. See FBX Animation . File ‣ Import ‣ FBX animation... In Blender, hover over the 3D Viewport, press N , open the Framez tab and click Import a clip... . Choose the FBX or BVH file. "},{"t":"Bring in a Moon Animator 2 Project","p":"Starting from an Existing Animation","u":"r-import.html#r-import-4","x":"Open the list of Moon Animator 2 saves. It holds every save in ServerStorage and ReplicatedStorage under MoonAnimator2Saves or MoonAnimatorSaves, newest first, nine at a time with a More row. See Moon Animator 2 File . File ‣ Import ‣ Moon Animator 2 file... C"},{"t":"Clean up the Result","p":"Starting from an Existing Animation","u":"r-import.html#r-import-5","x":"For motion captured from video or by mocap, run Mocap Cleanup to smooth the motion and put the feet on the floor. See Mocap Cleanup . Key ‣ Clean up ‣ Mocap cleanup For a clip with a key on every frame, thin it with Reduce Keys, which removes the keys that the"},{"t":"Previewing Blends, Layers and Aim Offsets","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-graph.html","x":"This tutorial previews how Roblox's Animation Graphs mix your clips in the game, such as a walk turning into a run by speed, breathing layered over everything, and an upper body that aims where the player looks. At the e"},{"t":"Blend a Walk into a Run","p":"Previewing Blends, Layers and Aim Offsets","u":"r-graph.html#r-graph-1","x":"Open the walk. See Open . File ‣ Open... Start the blend with the run. The box plays both clips together in a Blend1D on a see-through copy beside the rig. See Blend With . Game ‣ Preview with another clip ‣ Blend with ‣ the run Drag Blend value from 0 to 1 an"},{"t":"Blend Every Direction","p":"Previewing Blends, Layers and Aim Offsets","u":"r-graph.html#r-graph-2","x":"Open the directional blend. The box has a row for Centre, Forward, Back, Left and Right. See Directional Blend . Game ‣ Preview with another clip ‣ Directional blend... Choose a save for each row, the idle for Centre and a cycle for each direction you have. Th"},{"t":"Layer Breathing or a Recoil over Any Clip","p":"Previewing Blends, Layers and Aim Offsets","u":"r-graph.html#r-graph-3","x":"Make the layer as an animation of its own, keyed over the pose it belongs to. For example, key a few frames of breathing on top of the stance pose. Save it as an additive animation. In the box, choose the reference with Frame 0 , This frame or Rest pose , whic"},{"t":"Aim the Upper Body","p":"Previewing Blends, Layers and Aim Offsets","u":"r-graph.html#r-graph-4","x":"Open the stance and put the playhead on the pose to aim from. Make the aim poses. In the box, Left and right is how far the poses turn sideways, from 5° to 120° with 60° as the starting value, and Up and Down are how far they tilt, from 5° to 90° with 45° as t"},{"t":"Build the Graph in Your Game","p":"Previewing Blends, Layers and Aim Offsets","u":"r-graph.html#r-graph-5","x":"Publish the clips, then build the game's graph in Roblox's Graph Editor on the Avatar tab. Use a Blend1D for the walk and run, a Blend2D for the directions and for the aim poses, and an Add node for the additive clip. Framez previews these graphs with Roblox's"},{"t":"Setting up R15, Creature and Custom Rigs","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-rig.html","x":"This tutorial prepares a rig that is not a plain R6, such as an R15 character, a creature, a door or a skinned mesh with bones, so that every Framez tool works on it. At the end the model opens in Framez and animates lik"},{"t":"Start with a Standard Rig","p":"Setting up R15, Creature and Custom Rigs","u":"r-rig.html#r-rig-1","x":"Add a new rig. It appears on the ground in front of the camera, facing it, with a name that no other rig uses, and it is selected. An R15 rig gets Motor6D joints and opens at once. The avatar choices use the avatar of the account that is signed in to Studio, w"},{"t":"Rig a Model That Has No Joints","p":"Setting up R15, Creature and Custom Rigs","u":"r-rig.html#r-rig-2","x":"Select the model in the Explorer, such as a door, a chest or a creature. If it is the rig that is open in Framez, close the animation first. Give every piece of the model a joint. Each piece gets a Motor6D to the model's main part, which is its PrimaryPart or "},{"t":"Tell Framez Which Part Is Which","p":"Setting up R15, Creature and Custom Rigs","u":"r-rig.html#r-rig-3","x":"Click the creature or renamed character and start an animation on it. File ‣ New animation Open the Rig mapping window. It shows a body with a dot for each part. A white dot is mapped, a dot with a dark red fill is needed and still empty, and a dot with a grey"},{"t":"Animate a Skinned Mesh Through Its Bones","p":"Setting up R15, Creature and Custom Rigs","u":"r-rig.html#r-rig-4","x":"Click the model, which is one mesh with bones, and start an animation on it. Its bones show through the body. See Mesh Bones . File ‣ New animation Click a bone, or the skin over it, to select it. The bone under the cursor turns white and the selected bone is "},{"t":"Work with Many Joints","p":"Setting up R15, Creature and Custom Rigs","u":"r-rig.html#r-rig-5","x":"A rig with many joints lists its tracks in groups, such as Body, Tail and Thigh.L. Groups turn on by themselves for rigs with mesh bones and for rigs with 25 joints or more. View ‣ Windows ‣ Track groups Click a group's row to fold or open it. Double-click it "},{"t":"Animating a Lunge on a Rolling Foot","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-lunge.html","x":"This tutorial animates a lunge in which the front foot lands on its heel and flattens while the back foot rolls up onto its toes without sliding. At the end the legs follow the foot controls, and the animation saves as o"},{"t":"Build the Controls","p":"Animating a Lunge on a Rolling Foot","u":"r-lunge.html#r-lunge-1","x":"Build the control rig. A box appears under each foot with a handle behind the heel, and rings appear round the hips and the head. The status line lists what was made. Each foot is keyed where it stands, so a new animation has a key for each foot at frame 0. Se"},{"t":"Step the Front Foot Forward","p":"Animating a Lunge on a Rolling Foot","u":"r-lunge.html#r-lunge-2","x":"Move the playhead to frame 24. Click the box under the front foot to select its control. If rings show, press R to switch to the arrows. Drag the arrow that points forward until the foot is a stride ahead. Letting go keys the foot control at frame 24, and the "},{"t":"Roll the Feet","p":"Animating a Lunge on a Rolling Foot","u":"r-lunge.html#r-lunge-3","x":"Move the playhead to frame 20 and select the front foot. In the Foot section of the Inspector, type -25 in Roll . The toes tip up, so the heel meets the floor first. Roll is 0 at frame 24, so the foot flattens as it lands. See Foot and Hand . Move the playhead"},{"t":"Save the Lunge","p":"Animating a Lunge on a Rolling Foot","u":"r-lunge.html#r-lunge-4","x":"Save the animation. The joints of both legs are saved as ordinary keys, a key a frame and then thinned, so the game plays the lunge as it plays any other animation. The controls come back when you open the animation again. File ‣ Save"},{"t":"Making a Hand Move with a Sword","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-swordhand.html","x":"This tutorial keeps a second hand on the grip of a sword while the sword swings, lets go of it and takes hold again. At the end the hand follows the sword without a key on every frame."},{"t":"Build the Controls and Switch the Hand to IK","p":"Making a Hand Move with a Sword","u":"r-swordhand.html#r-swordhand-1","x":"Build the control rig. Both arms start on FK, so each arm shows a ring on each of its parts. See Control Rig . File ‣ Rig ‣ Control rig ‣ Build control rig Move the playhead to frame 0, right-click the left hand and switch the arm to IK. The hand control is ke"},{"t":"Put the Hand on the Grip","p":"Making a Hand Move with a Sword","u":"r-swordhand.html#r-swordhand-2","x":"Click the ring round the left wrist. If rings show, press R to switch to the arrows, and drag them until the hand holds the grip. Letting go keys the hand control at the playhead. In the Hand section of the Inspector, set Moves with to A part... , then click t"},{"t":"Swing the Sword","p":"Making a Hand Move with a Sword","u":"r-swordhand.html#r-swordhand-3","x":"Move the playhead to a later frame and turn the right arm to swing the sword. Key the right arm at each pose of the swing, as for any limb. Play the animation. The left hand stays on the grip through the swing, and its arm bends to reach it. The left hand need"},{"t":"Let Go and Take Hold Again","p":"Making a Hand Move with a Sword","u":"r-swordhand.html#r-swordhand-4","x":"Move the playhead to the frame where the hand lets go. Set Moves with to World . The hand stays where it is while the sword and the hips move on. Move the playhead to the frame where the hand takes hold again. Drag the hand back onto the grip, then set Moves w"},{"t":"Setting Your Own Keyboard Shortcuts","p":"Tutorials","u":"r-keys.html","x":"This tutorial gives any command a key of your own, or switches to the set of keys you know from another tool. At the end the commands you use most are on keys that suit you."},{"t":"Give One Command a Key","p":"Setting Your Own Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"r-keys.html#r-keys-1","x":"Right-click the command in any menu, or in the results of Menu search, which opens with Ctrl + K , and choose Change shortcut... from the menu that opens. See Change Shortcut and Menu Search . Right-click a menu row ‣ Change shortcut... Press the new keys whil"},{"t":"Set Many Keys in One Window","p":"Setting Your Own Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"r-keys.html#r-keys-2","x":"Open the Keyboard Shortcuts window. It lists every command by menu, with its key. See Keyboard Shortcuts . Edit ‣ Settings ‣ Keyboard shortcuts... The Keyboard shortcuts window, with the search box and By key at the top and the commands listed by group. Find t"},{"t":"Start from Another Set of Keys","p":"Setting Your Own Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"r-keys.html#r-keys-3","x":"Press the Start from button at the foot of the window and choose Framez, Blender or Moon Animator 2, or a set saved in this place. The Blender set puts Insert key on I , deletes keys with X , steps a frame with Left and Right , and turns with R and moves with "},{"t":"Share Your Keys with the Place","p":"Setting Your Own Keyboard Shortcuts","u":"r-keys.html#r-keys-4","x":"Press Start from and choose Save mine to this place.... Type a name and press Save . The keys are saved in the place, and everyone working in it can choose them from the same list under This place."},{"t":"Shortcut List","p":"Reference","u":"shortcuts.html","x":"These are the keys that Framez starts with, grouped as the Keyboard Shortcuts window groups them. They work while a Framez window is active, so click in it first. Any of them can be changed in Keyboard Shortcuts , where "},{"t":"Timeline","p":"Shortcut List","u":"shortcuts.html#shortcuts-timeline","x":"These keys play the animation and move the playhead and the keys along the timeline. Key Command Space Play / pause Left Previous key Right Next key Alt + Left Previous frame Alt + Right Next frame Home Go to the start End Go to the end Shift + Left Move keys "},{"t":"File","p":"Shortcut List","u":"shortcuts.html#shortcuts-file","x":"Key Command Ctrl + S Save"},{"t":"Edit","p":"Shortcut List","u":"shortcuts.html#shortcuts-edit","x":"Key Command Ctrl + K Menu search Ctrl + Z Undo Ctrl + Y Redo A Select all Esc Select none Del or Backspace Delete keys , or the selected item of the Modifiers window when no keys are selected"},{"t":"Key","p":"Shortcut List","u":"shortcuts.html#shortcuts-key","x":"Key Command K Insert key . In the Scene workspace it is Key camera, which keys the selected camera, prop or light. 7 Easing Tab Looping"},{"t":"Pose","p":"Shortcut List","u":"shortcuts.html#shortcuts-pose","x":"The slider keys open the slider at the pointer in the mode named, and the key T opens it in the mode you used last. Key Command R Switches between Rotate and Move Ctrl + L World space , on or off O Proportional editing , on or off T Open the slider at the poin"},{"t":"Face","p":"Shortcut List","u":"shortcuts.html#shortcuts-face","x":"Key Command B Blink here ] Next face here [ Previous face here"},{"t":"Scene","p":"Shortcut List","u":"shortcuts.html#shortcuts-scene","x":"Key Command C Look through camera"},{"t":"View","p":"Shortcut List","u":"shortcuts.html#shortcuts-view","x":"The number pad keys move the viewport whichever navigation mode is chosen in Preferences . Key Command P Handles, on or off F or Num . Frame selected Num 1 View from the front Ctrl + Num 1 View from the back Num 3 View from the right Ctrl + Num 3 View from the"},{"t":"Curve Editor","p":"Shortcut List","u":"shortcuts.html#shortcuts-curves","x":"These keys work only while the Curve editor window is the one receiving the key presses, so click in it first. A key given to one of these commands is kept for that window alone. Key Command A Select every key on the shown curves X or Del Delete the selected k"},{"t":"Modifiers Window","p":"Shortcut List","u":"shortcuts.html#shortcuts-modifiers","x":"These keys act on the item selected in the Modifiers window when no keys are selected. They are not listed in the Keyboard Shortcuts window and cannot be changed. Key Command F9 Open the values of the selected item again, or of the last item made when none is "},{"t":"Fixed Keys","p":"Shortcut List","u":"shortcuts.html#shortcuts-tool","x":"These keys only mean something while the tool or list named is in use. They cannot be changed. Key Command Enter In a dialog: press its main button, also while typing in a field. Slider open: keep the result. Camera path being drawn: finish the path. Esc Cance"},{"t":"Keys with the Mouse","p":"Shortcut List","u":"shortcuts.html#shortcuts-mouse","x":"These combinations hold a key while you click or drag. They are not commands, so they are not in the Keyboard Shortcuts window. Key Action Shift +click a limb Adds the limb to the selected limbs, or takes it off again Alt +click on the body Turns the selected "},{"t":"Troubleshooting","p":"Reference","u":"trouble.html","x":"Each entry names something that looks wrong, says what usually causes it and how to put it right. The entries are grouped by where the problem shows up. Pressing Ctrl + K in Framez and typing a word also finds the tool t"},{"t":"Menus, Keys and the Interface","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#trouble-interface","x":""},{"t":"Purple or Green Lines on the Rig","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-jointlines","x":"These lines are Studio's own display of joints, attachments and constraints, not Framez. Studio draws them for every jointed model in the place. To hide them, open Visualization Options at the top right of the viewport and turn off the constraint, joint and at"},{"t":"A Menu or Tool Is Missing","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-missing","x":"The tool most likely belongs to another workspace . Switch workspaces with the tabs before the menus, or press Ctrl + K and search for the tool, because Menu Search lists the commands of every workspace and switches to the right one when you run it. To show ev"},{"t":"A Menu Row Is Greyed","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-greyed","x":"A greyed row cannot run yet. Point at it, and its tooltip says what it needs first, such as selecting a limb, selecting keys or opening a scene."},{"t":"A Keyboard Shortcut Does Nothing","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-keyfails","x":"Framez's keys work while a Framez window is active, so click in the Framez window first. A key also does nothing while a tool that is running owns it, for example while the slider is open, which is described under Fixed Keys . If you have changed keys, click B"},{"t":"Studio's Own Undo Does Nothing While I Animate","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-studioundo","x":"While an animation is open, Framez switches Studio's undo off, and Ctrl + Z undoes Framez's own steps only. Close the animation to use Studio's undo again. If you delete the rig itself while its animation is open, Restore Deleted Rig brings it back."},{"t":"A Limb Will Not Select or Will Not Move","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-locked","x":"Its track is locked. Right-click the limb's name in the track list, open the Track submenu and choose Lock track to unlock it."},{"t":"Animation","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#trouble-animation","x":""},{"t":"The Game Plays the Animation Differently","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-gamediffers","x":"Check Animator Preview , which plays the animation the way Roblox will. Then look at the priority on the Clip page of the Inspector, because an animation with a higher priority that plays on the same joints wins. The Test Bench in its Game view says which anim"},{"t":"A Limb Jumps in One Frame, or Passes Its Key and Comes Back","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-jump","x":"Check Animation lists each of these problems at its frame, with its fix. In the Curve Editor , setting the selected keys to Smooth gives them handles that never pass their keys."},{"t":"Feet Slide or Float Halfway Through a Blend","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-feetblend","x":"The feet of the two animations land at different points of their loops. The ruler in Blend With shows where each animation's feet land, and Match Steps lines them up."},{"t":"Match Steps Says Two Keys Would Land on One Frame","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-matchsteps","x":"Two keys of one limb cannot share a frame, so Match Steps refuses to move them there and names the two keys. Delete one of them, or make the animation longer with Scale Keys , and then match again."},{"t":"A Pose Tool Is Greyed","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-posegreyed","x":"An additive animation layer is selected, and the pose tools do not work on it. Pose with the handles on that layer instead, or make Base the active layer from the Layers submenu of the Key menu."},{"t":"The Character Bounces After a Punch","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-bounce","x":"A whole-body squash that is keyed right after a move dips the body and then brings it back. Key a whole-body squash on landings, and for a punch stretch the arm instead. See Squash and Stretch ."},{"t":"Scenes and Effects","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#trouble-scenes","x":""},{"t":"An Effect Starts in the Wrong Place","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-fxplace","x":"Right-click the effect's row, open Place it, and choose Where it hits, Where I click or At the limb's end. You can also move the effect's point with Studio's Move tool, and the move is keyed at the playhead. See Place ."},{"t":"A Change to an Effect Is Not Keyed","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-fxkey","x":"A change made while Loop Around It is playing is not keyed. Choose Stop looping, go to the frame, and then change the value."},{"t":"The Camera Preview Shows Only the Picture","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-camerapreview","x":"Clean view is on. Point at the window and click the eye in its top right corner to bring the controls back. See Clean View ."},{"t":"Clicking the Other Character Does Nothing","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-otherchar","x":"A character joins the scene only once it has a row in the scene, so add it from the Rig list under Add in the Scene menu. When one limb is behind another limb of the posed character, a click goes to the one in front. Click its row in the list instead."},{"t":"The Camera Jumps Back When I Move the Playhead","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-camjump","x":"Flying while looking through a camera moves it, but that movement is not keyed. Changing the frame puts the camera back on its keys. Press K to key the camera before you move the playhead."},{"t":"The Scene's Sound Is Silent in Studio","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-sound","x":"The sound's id must be one that your account or group may use. Its row says \"Loading the sound...\" until Roblox provides it. Also check the Volume on the sound block's page in the Inspector."},{"t":"Put in the Game Says an Animation Is Not Published","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-notpublished","x":"When Motion is set to Uploaded animations, every character's animation must be published first. Publish them, or set Motion to In the scene, which carries the motion inside the scene itself. See Put in the Game ."},{"t":"Faces","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#trouble-faces","x":""},{"t":"The Face Changes in Framez but Not in the Game","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-facegame","x":"The game needs the FramezFace scripts. Choose Face swaps in the game... from the In the game submenu of the Game menu, and then choose Add it to this game. Publish the animation again afterwards. See Face Swaps in the Game ."},{"t":"There Is No Face Row","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-noface","x":"The head has neither a Decal nor FaceControls, so there is nothing to key. In the Face swaps submenu of the Face menu, Add face decal adds a face Decal. A Face row that is folded under Head opens with the arrow beside Head."},{"t":"Exact Lip Sync Is Greyed or Fails","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-lipsync","x":"Exact lip sync needs version 1.2 or newer of the Framez Helper. Start the helper, or install it again with Get the Framez Helper . The sound must also be one that your account may use."},{"t":"A Pasted Decal Id Gives No Face","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-decal","x":"Roblox would not open that decal for Framez. Paste the id of the image itself instead. Insert the decal from the Toolbox and copy the number from its Texture property."},{"t":"The Helper, Video and Blender","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#trouble-helper","x":""},{"t":"Framez Says the Framez Helper Is Not Running","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-helperoff","x":"Start Framez Helper from the Start menu and click Retry. If it is not installed yet, install it . Image frames work without the helper."},{"t":"\"Studio Stopped Framez from Reaching the Framez Helper\"","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-helperblocked","x":"Studio asked whether Framez may connect to 127.0.0.1 and the answer was no. Allow it by opening Manage Plugins on the Plugins tab, selecting Framez, and allowing HTTP requests."},{"t":"\"This Place Has HTTP Requests Turned Off\"","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-httpoff","x":"Turn them on for the place. Open the place's Experience Settings, go to Security, turn on Allow HTTP Requests, and click Retry in Framez."},{"t":"Framez Cannot Add a Script to the Game","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-script","x":"Studio has to allow it. Open Manage Plugins on the Plugins tab, select Framez, and allow Script injection."},{"t":"My Videos Are Not in the Clip List","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-novideo","x":"The helper reads only the folders it has been given. Add the folder that holds the videos in Clip Folders . Videos that sit in folders inside it are not listed, so add each of those folders as well."},{"t":"The Folder Picker Did Not Come Up","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-folderpicker","x":"Paste the folder's path into the box under Add a clip folder... and press Enter ."},{"t":"Blender Has No Framez Tab","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-blendertab","x":"The add-on is not switched on. Open Blender's Preferences, go to Add-ons, search for Framez and tick Framez Link. The add-on needs Blender 4.2 or newer."},{"t":"Joints Are on the Wrong Person, or Jump Around","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-wrongperson","x":"The joint tracking follows the part of the picture that lies inside the white box in the Reference video window. Zoom the window in until the person fills the white box."},{"t":"Pose from Video Flipped a Limb","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-flippedlimb","x":"Delete the bad keys and key them by hand against the Joints overlay, or run Euler Filter on the animation to remove the flips."},{"t":"An Imported FBX Animation Has a Limb Wrong","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-fbxlimb","x":"The Framez tab in Blender says which skeleton it read, such as Mixamo, R15, R6 or \"named by words\", and which joints it kept still. A bone with an unusual name may not have been recognised. Rename it to a standard name, for example the names Mixamo uses such a"},{"t":"Get from Blender Says \"Not on This Rig\"","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-notonrig","x":"Those joints have different names in Blender than on the rig. Rename the bones in Blender to the Motor6D names, or leave them out. See Get from Blender ."},{"t":"Saving and Importing","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#trouble-saving","x":""},{"t":"The Animation Is Gone After Reopening Studio","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-gone","x":"The place was not saved after the animation was. Saving in Framez writes into the place, so save in Framez and then save the place. While the Framez Helper runs, every save is also written to disk. Drag its .rbxm file, which is in the backup folder under the r"},{"t":"Save Says There Is Nothing to Save","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-nothingtosave","x":"An animation with no keys is not saved. Key a pose first, and then save."},{"t":"A Change Made It Worse, and Undo Cannot Reach Back","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-worse","x":"Open Version History and select the version from before the change."},{"t":"Import Says Roblox Would Not Give the Animation","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-importdenied","x":"Roblox only provides your own animations, those of your groups, and Roblox's own. Import the animation from the place that it lives in, or ask its owner. See Roblox Animation ."},{"t":"Emotes and Rigs","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#trouble-rigs","x":""},{"t":"The Emote Upload Says \"missing a Valid UGC Emote Marker\"","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-emotemarker","x":"The animation was uploaded as a plain animation. Open Emote and click Save as emote. In step 1, open Roblox's upload window, set Animation Type to Emote and tick Create Animation Object in Workspace. Step 2 then uploads the Animation that step 1 made. The step"},{"t":"The Emote Upload Says a Body Part Is Too Far, Too Low or Too Fast","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-emotebody","x":"Open the animation and then Emote , where the same rule is listed at its frame. Pull the root back, or bend the knees where the body crouches."},{"t":"Framez Asks to Give a Rig Motor6Ds","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-motors","x":"The rig is joined with AnimationConstraints, as Roblox builds a new R15. Accept the offer. Each joint becomes a Motor6D between the same two attachments, and animations made on it play on either kind of rig. Undo puts the AnimationConstraints back."},{"t":"Create Joint Asks Me to Close the Animation First","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"trouble.html#f-fix-closefirst","x":"The two parts belong to the rig that is open, and a rig's own parts cannot be joined or welded while its animation is open. Close the animation, make the joint, and open the animation again. To attach an object while you animate, use Hold the selected object f"}];
